window.AH={"chapters":[{"n":1,"title":"Foundations of Life and Health (Ayushkameeya)","detail":"What this chapter is about\n\nThis opening chapter sets out the whole framework of Ayurveda. Its name, Ayushkameeya, means \"the desire for a long life.\" The idea is simple: a long, healthy life is the vehicle that lets a person pursue the four real aims of human living, so caring for the body and mind is the first duty. The text then lays out the basic building blocks it will use everywhere else in the book: the three doshas, the body tissues and wastes, the six tastes, the qualities of matter, and the definitions of health, disease and treatment.\n\nThe four aims of life\n\nAyurveda frames health as a means, not an end. The four aims it serves are Dharma (living rightly and honestly), Artha (earning a living by fair means), Kama (meeting one's legitimate desires), and Moksha (spiritual liberation). Because none of these can be pursued by a sick or short-lived person, the text says a wise person should put effort into a long life. It also names the single most important quality for learning this science: Vidheya, a humble willingness to follow the teaching and discipline.\n\nHow Ayurveda came down and how it is organised\n\nThe knowledge is described as passed from Brahma to Prajapati, to the Ashwini twins, to Indra, to the sage Atreya Punarvasu, to Agnivesha and other disciples who each wrote it down. Because those original texts are very long and hard to study, Ashtanga Hridaya was composed to hold just the essence, in a form that is neither too brief nor too elaborate. Ayurveda itself has eight branches (ashtanga): Kaya Chikitsa (general internal medicine), Bala Chikitsa (child health), Graha Chikitsa (mental and psychiatric care), Urdhvanga Chikitsa (ear, nose, throat, eye and head care), Shalya Chikitsa (surgery), Damshtra Chikitsa (toxicology, bites and poisons), Jara Chikitsa (care in old age and rejuvenation), and Vrushya Chikitsa (reproductive and virility care).\n\nThe three doshas (Tridosha)\n\nThe body is governed by three working forces called doshas: Vata (the principle of movement, linked to air and space), Pitta (the principle of transformation and heat, linked to fire), and Kapha (the principle of structure and lubrication, linked to water and earth). When the three are in balance the body stays well; when any is too high or too low, illness follows. The doshas fill the whole body, but each has a home region. If the body is split into three zones, the area from the chest upward is mainly Kapha, the area between chest and navel is mainly Pitta, and the area below the navel is mainly Vata.\n\nThe doshas also shift through time. Across a lifetime, childhood is the Kapha stage, the middle years are the Pitta stage, and old age is the Vata stage. Within a single day (and again within the night), the first part is Kapha-dominant, the middle part Pitta-dominant, and the last part Vata-dominant. During a meal and its digestion the same order repeats: the early phase is Kapha, the middle Pitta, and the final phase Vata.\n\nQualities of each dosha\n\nKnowing a dosha's qualities (gunas) is the key to balancing it, because like increases like and opposites reduce it. Vata is rough (ruksha), light (laghu), cold (sheeta), coarse (khara), subtle or able to reach tiny spaces (sookshma), and mobile (chala). Pitta is slightly oily (sasneha), sharp and penetrating (teekshna), hot (ushna), light (laghu), foul-smelling (visra), flowing (sara), and liquid (drava). Kapha is oily (snigdha), cold (sheeta), heavy (guru), slow and dense (manda), smooth (shlakshna), slimy or sticky (mrutsna), and stable or unmoving (sthira).\n\nWhen doshas go out of balance singly or two together, the state is called Samsarga; when all three are disturbed at once it is called Sannipata, which is the most serious.\n\nThe digestive fire (Agni)\n\nDigestion is run by Agni, and its behaviour follows the doshas, giving four types. Vishama Agni (irregular, Vata-driven) swings between strong and weak appetite. Teekshna Agni (sharp, Pitta-driven) gives a very strong appetite and fast digestion. Manda Agni (dull, Kapha-driven) gives weak appetite and slow digestion. Sama Agni (balanced, from even doshas) gives steady appetite and digestion at the right time; this is the ideal.\n\nTypes of bowel (Koshta)\n\nThe gut also comes in three natures. Kroora Koshta (hard, Vata-type) digests slowly with irregular, hard-to-pass motions. Mrudu Koshta (soft, Pitta-type) is sensitive and fast, so that even plain milk can loosen the bowels. Madhya Koshta (balanced) digests well with motions at the proper time.\n\nBody type (Prakruti)\n\nEvery person has an inborn constitution, fixed at conception by the state of the parents' reproductive material, much as poison is natural to a poisonous creature. The text ranks them: a Vata-dominant constitution is called lower grade, Pitta-dominant is middling, Kapha-dominant is good, and a balanced tridosha constitution is best of all. Two-dosha mixed types (Vata-Pitta, Pitta-Kapha, Vata-Kapha) are regarded as less favourable. This is a traditional ranking of resilience, not a judgement of a person's worth.\n\nThe body tissues (Dhatus) and wastes (Malas)\n\nThe tissues and wastes are together called Dushyas because they are the things the doshas act upon and spoil in disease. There are seven dhatus in sequence: Rasa (the first fluid formed right after food is digested, broadly like plasma or lymph), Rakta or Asruk (blood), Mamsa (muscle), Meda (fat), Asthi (bone and cartilage), Majja (marrow), and Shukra (the reproductive tissue and its secretions in both sexes). There are three main malas (wastes): Pureesha or Shakrut (faeces), Mootra (urine), and Sweda (sweat).\n\nThe rule of increase and decrease\n\nThe central law of all treatment: similar qualities increase a thing and opposite qualities reduce it. For example, dryness is a Vata quality, so dry cold weather raises Vata and dries the skin; applying oil, whose oiliness is the opposite of dryness, brings Vata back down.\n\nThe six tastes (Rasa)\n\nFood acts through six tastes: Madhura (sweet), Amla (sour), Lavana (salty), Tikta (bitter), Katu (pungent), and Kashaya (astringent). Listed in that order they carry progressively less nourishing energy, so sweet gives the most strength to the body and astringent the least.\n\nHow tastes move the doshas\n\nThe first three tastes, sweet, sour and salty, calm Vata but build Kapha. The last three, bitter, pungent and astringent, calm Kapha but raise Vata. For Pitta: astringent, bitter and sweet calm it, while sour, salty and pungent inflame it. Food can therefore be classed as Shamana (brings a raised dosha back to normal), Kopana (raises a dropped dosha back to normal), or Swasthahita (keeps all three balanced in a healthy person).\n\nPotency (Veerya) and post-digestive effect (Vipaka)\n\nEvery substance also has a potency: Ushna Veerya (heating, like pepper) or Sheeta Veerya (cooling, like milk). As a rule, astringent, bitter and sweet things tend to be cooling, while sour, salty and pungent things tend to be heating. After the first stage of digestion a food's taste transforms into a final effect called Vipaka, of three kinds: Madhura Vipaka (sweet, usually from sweet and salty tastes), Amla Vipaka (sour, from sour taste), and Katu Vipaka (pungent, from bitter, pungent and astringent tastes).\n\nThe twenty qualities (Gunas)\n\nAll matter and action can be described by twenty qualities arranged as ten opposite pairs: heavy/light (guru/laghu), slow/sharp (manda/teekshna), cold/hot (hima/ushna), oily/dry (snigdha/ruksha), smooth/rough (shlakshna/khara), solid/liquid (sandra/drava), soft/hard (mrudu/kathina), stable/moving (sthira/chala), subtle or small/gross or big (sookshma/sthula), and non-slimy/slimy (vishada/picchila). Treatment always works by applying the opposite quality to whatever is disturbed.\n\nWhat causes health and disease\n\nThree factors decide health: time (kala), the senses and their objects (artha), and our actions (karma). When our contact with these is correct and balanced (samyak yoga) we stay well. When contact is too little, too much, or wrong (hina, ati or mithya yoga) disease results. In short, health is the balance of the doshas (dosha samya) and disease is their imbalance (dosha vaishamya).\n\nTypes of disease\n\nBy origin, disease is either Nija (from internal imbalance, such as gastritis from too much hot food) or Agantu (from outside causes, such as injury); both can strike body or mind. The mind has its own two doshas: Rajas (the drive to activity and agitation) and Tamas (dullness, inaction and lethargy). Mental imbalance is treated by Dhee (sharpening understanding), Dhairya (building courage and steadiness) and Atmavijnana (self-knowledge).\n\nExamining the patient and the disease\n\nA patient is assessed three ways: Darshana (observation and inspection), Sparshana (examination by touch), and Prashna (questioning). A disease is studied through five lenses: Nidana (causes), Purvarupa (early warning signs), Lakshana (the actual signs and symptoms), Upashaya (response to trial measures, used as a diagnostic clue), and Samprapti (how the disease developed, its pathogenesis).\n\nLand and time\n\nSetting matters too. Desha (habitat) is of two kinds: the land (Bhumi) and the body (Deha). Land is of three types: Jangala (dry, desert-like, breezy, sparse water and plants, Vata-predominant), Anupa (marshy, wet, lush, little sun, Kapha-predominant), and Sadharana (moderate, balanced in all three doshas). Time (kala) is considered in two senses: ordinary time, and the stage a disease has reached.\n\nTypes of medicine and treatment\n\nTreatment is of two broad kinds. Shamana (palliative) brings a disturbed dosha back to balance and suits early stages. Shodhana (purification) drives the excess dosha out of the body and suits advanced stages. The matching remedies: for Vata, Basti (medicated enema) purifies and oil pacifies; for Pitta, Virechana (purgation) purifies and ghee pacifies; for Kapha, Vamana (therapeutic vomiting) purifies and honey pacifies.\n\nThe four pillars of treatment and their sixteen qualities\n\nGood care rests on four pillars (Chikitsa Chatushpada): the physician (Bhishag), the medicine (Dravya), the attendant or nurse (Upastha), and the patient (Rogi). Each needs four qualities, giving sixteen in all. The physician should be Daksha (alert and disciplined), learned in the texts (Shastra), experienced in practice (Drushtakarma), and clean (Shuchi). The medicine should be versatile across forms like decoction, powder and oil (Bahukalpa), rich in useful properties (Bahuguna), well prepared (Sampanna), and suitable for the specific illness (Yogya). The attendant should be caring (Anurakta), clean (Shuchi), capable (Daksha), and intelligent (Buddhiman). The patient should have the means (Adya), full obedience to the physician (Bhishagvashya), good memory (Jnapaka), and the strength to endure illness and treatment (Satvavaan).\n\nWhich diseases can be cured\n\nDiseases fall into Sadhya (curable) and Asadhya (incurable). Curable ones are either Sukha Sadhya (easy) or Krichra Sadhya (hard). Incurable ones are either Yapya (manageable only with lifelong medicine) or Anupakrama (beyond help). A disease is easy to cure when the patient can tolerate all medicines, is young, has self-control, the vital organs are untouched, the cause and symptoms are mild, there are no complications, and the dosha, tissue, place, season and constitution are not all pulling the same way; when all sixteen pillar qualities are present; and when the disease is single-dosha, in one channel only, and of recent onset. A disease is hard or impossible to cure when it needs sharp instruments, has run long, involves many vital tissues, produces fear, delusion and restlessness, shows fatal signs, or destroys the sense organs. The text also lists patients a physician may decline, such as those who are hostile, hopeless, uncooperative, dying, violent, or who presume to direct their own treatment; this is traditional guidance from its era, not a rule for modern care.\n\nThe map of the whole book\n\nThe chapter closes by listing the six sections of Ashtanga Hridaya: Sutrasthana (principles, 30 chapters), Sharira Sthana (anatomy and physiology), Nidana Sthana (diagnosis), Chikitsita Sthana (treatment), Kalpasiddhi Sthana (preparations and purificatory recipes), and Uttarasthana (child care, eyes, ENT, surgery, toxicology, rejuvenation and more)."},{"n":2,"title":"The Daily Routine (Dinacharya)","detail":"What this chapter is about\n\nDinacharya means the daily routine: Dina is day, Charya is regimen. This chapter lays out, step by step, what a healthy person should do each day to stay well, covering the body, speech and mind. The sequence runs from the moment of waking through personal care, massage, exercise, bathing, eating and sleep, and then expands into a long code of good conduct and personal hygiene. The whole aim is prevention: keeping the doshas steady through steady habits.\n\nWaking time (Prataruthana)\n\nA healthy person should rise in the Brahma Muhurta, the last stretch of night before dawn, roughly the three hours from about 3 am to 6 am, ideally around 45 minutes before sunrise. Rising at this quiet, fresh hour is said to protect life and health and sets the tone for the day.\n\nCleaning the teeth (Dantadhavana)\n\nAfter waking, first pass urine and stool, then clean the teeth. The traditional tool is a fresh chewing-twig from an astringent, pungent or bitter plant, such as Arka (Calotropis), Vata (banyan), Khadira (Acacia catechu), Karanja (Pongamia) or Kakubha (Arjuna). The twig should be about as thick as the tip of your little finger and about twelve finger-widths (angula) long, with one end chewed soft to form a brush. The brushing should clean the teeth without hurting the gums.\n\nWho should not brush with a twig\n\nTooth-brushing of this kind is to be skipped by people who are unwell in certain ways: those with indigestion, vomiting, breathlessness (dyspnoea), cough, fever, facial paralysis, severe thirst, mouth ulcers, heart disease, or disorders of the eyes, head or ears. In these states the vigorous action can aggravate the problem.\n\nEye care with collyrium (Anjana)\n\nA special eye preparation called Sauveera Anjana (antimony-based collyrium) may be applied to the eyes daily; it is considered good for the eyes, which are seen as fiery (tejas) in nature and therefore vulnerable to Kapha. Once a week a stronger preparation, Rasanjana (an aqueous extract of Berberis aristata), is applied to help drain excess Kapha secretions from the eyes. These are traditional practices described for context.\n\nNasal drops, gargling, smoke and betel (Nasya, Gandusha, Dhuma, Tambula)\n\nAfter eye care, a healthy person does Nasya (Navana), instilling drops into the nose, using milk, herbal decoctions or medicated oils. Next comes Gandusha, holding or gargling warm water, milk, herbal decoction or oil in the mouth. Then Dhuma, inhaling medicinal herbal smoke, followed by chewing betel leaf (Tambula). This cluster of practices is meant to care for the nose, mouth and throat and clear overnight congestion.\n\nWho should avoid betel chewing\n\nBetel leaf (Tambula) is to be avoided by those with wounds, bleeding disorders, excessive dryness, redness of the eyes, poisoning, repeated fainting, intoxication, or tuberculosis, because it can worsen these conditions.\n\nOil massage (Abhyanga)\n\nDaily oil massage, done in the morning, is one of the most valued practices. It slows ageing, relieves tiredness and eases the aches and pains of excess Vata. It improves eyesight, nourishes the body tissues, lengthens life, brings sound sleep, and improves the skin's tone and texture. It should be given special attention on the head, the ears and the feet. Abhyanga should be avoided when Kapha is aggravated in the body, immediately after purification therapy (Panchakarma), and during indigestion.\n\nExercise (Vyayama)\n\nExercise brings lightness to the body, improves the capacity for work, strengthens digestion, burns fat and gives the body a firm, well-proportioned shape. But it is not for everyone: people with Vata or Pitta disorders, children, the elderly and anyone with indigestion should not do it. The correct dose is to exercise only up to half of one's capacity (ardha shakti), stopping when effort first shows. It should be done regularly, especially by those who are strong and eat rich, oily food, and it is particularly suited to the cold season and spring (roughly December to May); in other, hotter times it should be kept mild. After exercising, the body should be gently massaged all over.\n\nHarm from over-exercise\n\nOverdoing exercise is dangerous. It can cause excessive thirst, wasting of the body, severe breathlessness, bleeding disorders, exhaustion, a feeling of weakness even without exertion, cough, fever and vomiting. The text warns that those who constantly over-exercise, stay awake late, walk very long distances, have too much sex, or laugh and talk to excess, wear themselves out and perish, like a lion that destroys itself after defeating an elephant. Moderation is the whole point.\n\nPowder massage (Udvartana)\n\nUdvartana is massage with dry herbal powders rather than oil. It calms aggravated Kapha, helps dissolve and burn fat (which is why Ayurvedic centres use it for weight reduction), firms and steadies the limbs, and improves the skin's strength and complexion.\n\nBathing (Snana)\n\nBathing strengthens digestion, acts as a tonic for vigour, lengthens life, and increases enthusiasm and strength. It washes away dirt, sweat, waste, tiredness, excessive thirst, burning sensation and germs. An important detail: pouring warm water over the body gives strength, but pouring hot water over the head harms the hair and the eyes, so the head is best washed with cooler water. Bathing is contraindicated for people with facial paralysis, diseases of the eyes, mouth or ears, diarrhoea, flatulence, a blocked or running nose (rhinitis), indigestion, and for anyone who has just eaten.\n\nEating and natural urges (good practice, Sad Vritta)\n\nThree simple rules govern food and the body's urges. First, eat only after the previous meal has digested, and eat in a measured, limited quantity (Jeerne hitam mitam cha adyat). Second, never force out a natural urge that is not present, such as straining to urinate when there is no need (Na vegan neerayet). Third, never suppress a natural urge that has arisen, attend to it at once rather than staying busy with other things (Na vegito anya karyaha). And one should seek treatment for any illness as early as possible.\n\nRighteous living (Dharma)\n\nAll beings seek happiness, and there is no lasting happiness without righteousness, so one should keep to the path of Dharma. Good friends should be served with affection and good deeds, while the harmful and wicked are best kept at a distance.\n\nThe ten acts to avoid\n\nTen harmful acts, spread across body, speech and mind, should be given up. Of the body: Himsa (violence or cruelty), Steya (stealing), and Anyatha-kama (unlawful or wrongful sexual conduct). Of speech: Paishunya (back-biting), Parusha (harsh words), Anruta (lying), and Sambhinna-alapa (divisive talk that breaks friendships). Of the mind: Vyapada (ill-will and the wish to harm), Abhidya (envy, not bearing others' good fortune), and Drg-viparyaya (perverse views, faultfinding, faithlessness toward scriptures and elders). The first three belong to the body, the next four to speech, and the last three to the mind.\n\nKindness, balance and social conduct\n\nThe text gives a wide code of character. Help those who have no livelihood, who are sick, or who are stricken with grief. Treat even insects and ants with respect, and honour physicians, kings and guests. Never disappoint, abuse or turn away a beggar. Be helpful even to those who are not helpful to you, even to foes. Keep an even mind in both calamity and prosperity (Sampat-vipatsu eka-manaha). Do not envy others' wealth or happiness.\n\nRight speech\n\nSpeak at the proper occasion (Kale bruyat), speak kind and pleasant words (Hitam bruyat), and speak little, only as much as needed (Mitam bruyat). Avoid arguing and avoid saying untrue things. Be the first to greet and open conversation, keep a smiling face, good character, and be gentle and courteous in word and act. Do not be someone who always wants to be alone; neither trust everyone blindly nor suspect everyone. Do not hastily decide that someone is your enemy or another's enemy. Do not publicly broadcast insults you have suffered, nor air disaffection toward your superior.\n\nThe middle path\n\nDeal with people according to their nature, in the way that best pleases them, becoming skilled in the art of winning others over. Neither strain the senses too hard nor pamper them. Avoid any undertaking empty of the three pursuits of Dharma, Artha and Kama, and never act against Dharma. In all dealings keep to the middle course and avoid extremes.\n\nPersonal hygiene and grooming\n\nCut the hair, nails and moustache regularly. Keep the feet, ears, nose, eyes, urethra and anus clean. Bathe daily, wear light pleasant scents, and dress in clean clothes that are neat and agreeable rather than showy. One may wear protective gemstones, sacred hymns and herbs (in amulets). When out, carry an umbrella against sun, wear footwear, and look ahead about four arm-lengths on the path. If urgent business calls at night, go out equipped with a stick, head-covering and a companion.\n\nPlaces and actions to avoid\n\nThe chapter lists many things a careful person avoids. Do not tread on the shade of a sacred tree, sacred objects, banners, heaps of ash, husk or dirt, sand dunes, boulders, or places of ritual offering and bathing. Do not cross rivers by swimming on your arms, walk straight into a large fire, travel in an unsafe boat, climb a tree of doubtful strength, or ride a vehicle in bad condition. Do not sneeze, laugh or yawn without covering the mouth. Do not blow the nose needlessly, scratch the ground for no reason, make ugly movements of the body, or sit squatting on the heels for long. Stop activity of body, speech and mind before you are worn out, and do not hold the knees up for long periods.\n\nWhere not to stay at night\n\nDo not spend the night at the foot of trees, at junctions of three or four roads, at recreation grounds, near sacred trees or temples. Even by day, avoid lingering in slaughter-places, deep forest, haunted houses and burial grounds.\n\nMore cautions for the senses and the body\n\nDo not gaze at the sun for long, carry heavy loads on the head, or keep staring at things that are tiny, dazzling, dirty or unpleasant. Avoid the dealings of selling, brewing, giving away or receiving wine. Avoid direct draughts, harsh sun, dust, dew and whirlwinds. Do not sneeze, belch, cough, sleep, eat or have sex in awkward postures. Avoid the company of wild animals, horned and biting animals, and of mean, wicked and cunning people, and avoid quarrels with good people. Do not take food, have sex, sleep, study or revise at the junctions of dusk and dawn. Do not eat food given by enemies, or offered insincerely, or by those who make a business of it. Do not make noises with the body, mouth or nails, or shake the hands and hair. Do not pass between two fires, two bodies of water, or two sacred objects. Avoid the smoke of a corpse, excess wine, and misplaced trust in or dependence on unreliable company.\n\nThe world as teacher\n\nFor a wise person the whole world is a teacher: observe how people and things behave, weigh the meaning and effects of their actions, and imitate what is good. In short, the essence of good conduct is compassion toward all living beings, generosity in giving, control over body, speech and mind, and caring for others' interests as one's own.\n\nThe reward\n\nOne who keeps thinking about how each day and night is passing, and steadily chooses the right way, never falls prey to sorrow. Whoever follows this routine of good conduct gains long life, health, wealth, good reputation and lasting well-being."},{"n":3,"title":"Seasonal Living (Ritucharya)","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis chapter maps out how to eat, drink, dress, and behave through each of the year's six seasons so the body stays balanced as the weather changes. The core idea is simple: our strength and our digestive fire rise and fall with the seasons, and the smart move is to adjust daily habits to match. The word Ritucharya joins ritu (season) with charya (the right routine, the do's and don'ts).\n\nThe six seasons\n\nThe old Ayurvedic calendar splits the year into six seasons of about two months each. Early winter / dewy season (Shishira, roughly mid-January to mid-March). Spring (Vasanta, mid-March to mid-May). Summer (Greeshma, mid-May to mid-July). Rainy season / monsoon (Varsha, mid-July to mid-September). Autumn (Sharath, mid-September to mid-November). Late-autumn-into-winter (Hemanta, mid-November to mid-January).\n\nTwo halves of the year: Adana and Visarga\n\nThe year divides into two big phases based on the sun's movement. In the northern course (Uttarayana), covering Shishira, Vasanta and Greeshma, the sun and wind grow harsh and drying. This is called Adana Kala, the \"taking away\" time, because the sun steadily drains the cooling, moist, nourishing quality of the earth and of our bodies. Our strength tends to be lower in this half. Across its three seasons the dominant harsh tastes increase in turn: bitter, then astringent, then pungent.\n\nIn the southern course (Dakshinayana), covering Varsha, Sharath and Hemanta, clouds, rain and cool wind restore moisture to the earth and the moon's cooling influence is said to be stronger. This is Visarga Kala, the \"giving back\" time, when our strength tends to be higher. Across its three seasons the gentler, building tastes dominate in turn: sour, then salty, then sweet.\n\nHow strength changes through the year\n\nPhysical strength is at its peak in the two cold seasons (Hemanta and Shishira, mid-November to mid-March). It is at its lowest in summer and the rains (mid-May to mid-September). In spring and autumn it sits in the middle. Knowing this helps you judge how much heavy food, exercise, or sexual activity your body can comfortably handle at a given time of year.\n\nEarly winter (Hemanta)\n\nWith the cold holding body heat in, the digestive fire turns strong and, if underfed, it starts to burn through the body's own tissues and cause wasting. So eat well and favour sweet, sour and salty foods. Nights are long, so morning hunger comes early. Begin the day with oil massage (abhyanga) using Vata-pacifying oils, paying special attention to the head and forehead, followed by gentle exercise or wrestling to about half your capacity, bathing, and warming touches like saffron paste and aromatic fumes. Because digestion is powerful, heavier foods suit the season: meat soups with fats, dairy and sugarcane preparations, fresh grains, wheat and black-gram dishes. Use warm water for washing, wear warm layers, keep the feet covered, and spend time near warmth and sunshine. Intimacy is well supported now. People who keep warm indoors avoid the illnesses that cold and dryness can bring.\n\nLate winter (Shishira)\n\nFollow the same winter routine, only more firmly, because this is the coldest and driest stretch of the year and it falls within the drying Adana half. Keep the body warmer, oilier, and better nourished than in early winter.\n\nSpring (Vasanta)\n\nThe Kapha that quietly built up over winter now melts under the spring sun, floods the system, weakens digestion and can trigger illness. The season's job is to clear excess Kapha. Favour light, dry, Kapha-reducing foods; older barley, wheat and honey suit well. Use dry massage and exercise, and under proper guidance Kapha-clearing therapies like therapeutic vomiting (Vamana) and nasal cleansing (Nasya) may be used. Bathe and apply light cooling pastes like sandalwood and camphor. Enjoy fragrant gardens and pleasant company in the middle of the day. Avoid heavy, cold, oily, sour and sweet foods, and avoid daytime sleep, since all of these feed Kapha.\n\nSummer (Greeshma)\n\nThe sun is fierce; Kapha keeps falling and Vata rises. Cut back on salty, pungent and sour foods, hard exercise and sun exposure. Eat sweet, light, cool, slightly oily and liquid foods; cold grain drinks with sugar after a cool bath help. Keep wine to a minimum, well diluted if taken at all, because too much brings swelling, weakness, burning and confusion. Cooling drinks, cold milk, and water kept cool in earthen pots are ideal. Rest in cool, shaded, breezy places by day, stay near water and fragrant cooling mists, and at night sleep on an open terrace in the moonlight with light clothing, sandalwood paste, and fans. Sexual activity is best avoided in the heat.\n\nRainy season / monsoon (Varsha)\n\nDigestion is at its weakest now, already drained by summer and further disturbed by damp, cold winds, wet dirty water, and the sour quality in the air, which stir up all three doshas. The aim is to protect and rekindle the digestive fire and keep the doshas settled. Under guidance, cleansing therapies and decoction enema (Asthapana Basti) may be used. Eat old grains, well-spiced meat soups and light sour-salty-oily dishes with a little honey; use rain or deep-well water that has been well boiled. Stay dry and comfortable: use perfumes, keep clothes pleasantly scented, live in the upper, drier part of the house, and travel by vehicle rather than walking through the wet. Avoid river water, heavy chilled drinks, daytime sleep, overexertion and sun exposure.\n\nAutumn (Sharath)\n\nThe body, now used to the cool rains, meets the returning heat of the sun, and the Pitta that built up during the rains flares. To settle it, favour bitter, sweet and astringent tastes in light, easy meals like rice, green gram, amla and gentle meats, and under guidance use purgation and bitter medicated ghee. A special drink of the season is Hamsodaka: water warmed by the day's sun and cooled by the night's moon over many clear nights, considered pure, balancing and neither clogging nor drying. Spend cool evenings in the moonlight with light sandalwood paste. Avoid rich curds, oils, fats, strong liquor, overeating, daytime sleep, harsh sun and the eastern breeze.\n\nTastes and food qualities by season\n\nAs a quick guide: use more sweet, sour and salty tastes in winter and the rains; more bitter, pungent and astringent in spring; mostly sweet in summer; and sweet, bitter and astringent in autumn. For food qualities, keep food dry (low moisture and fat) in autumn and spring, cold in summer and at the tail of the rains, and warm in the other seasons. The ideal overall is to include all six tastes every day while simply emphasising the ones the current season calls for.\n\nThe transition weeks (Ritu-sandhi)\n\nThe last seven days of one season and the first seven of the next form the \"seam\" between seasons. During this stretch, wind down the old season's routine gradually and ease into the new one gradually. Abrupt changes, dropping old habits overnight or jumping straight into new ones, upset the body because it has not had time to adapt, and this mismatch (asatmya) can itself cause illness."},{"n":4,"title":"Preventing Disease (Roganutpadaniya)","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis chapter is about keeping illness from starting in the first place. Its name, Roganutpadaniya, breaks down as roga (disease) plus anutpadana (non-production), so it literally means \"preventing disease from arising.\" The central teaching is to respect the body's natural signals, neither forcing them nor blocking them, so that waste and imbalance do not build up and set disease in motion.\n\nTwo ways we go wrong with urges\n\nThe chapter identifies two opposite mistakes. One is holding back a natural urge that the body genuinely needs to release (vega-dharana, suppression). The other is forcing an urge that has not naturally arisen (vega-udirana, straining). Both disturb the body's normal flow, and the main thing they derange is Vata, the moving force that governs elimination and downward flow. From that single root, a wide range of complaints can follow.\n\nThe thirteen urges you should not suppress\n\nThere are thirteen natural urges that should never be held back by force: downward wind or gas (flatus), stool, urine, sneezing, thirst, hunger, sleep, cough, the heavy breathing that comes after exertion, yawning, tears, vomiting, and semen (ejaculation). Each of these is the body clearing or regulating something, and blocking it pushes Vata out of its natural downward path, creating trouble that usually traces back to disturbed Vata.\n\nWhat suppressing each urge can cause, and how it is managed\n\nHolding back gas can bring on bloating and gaseous distension of the abdomen (udavarta), abdominal pain, tiredness, constipation, difficulty passing urine, dimmed vision, weakened digestion, and discomfort in the upper abdomen and chest, because Vata, which should move down, is forced upward instead.\n\nHolding back stool can cause cramping calf pain, a runny nose, headache, upward-moving Vata, a cutting or itching feeling at the anus, a stiff heavy chest, and even foul breath. The remedies for suppressed gas, stool and urine overlap: rectal and urethral suppositories, oil massage, sitz or tub baths, sweating therapy, enema (basti), and foods that gently loosen the bowels.\n\nHolding back urine can cause body pain, urinary stones, and pain in the bladder and genital region, along with the same kinds of problems seen with suppressed gas and stool. A specific help here is taking ghee before meals, and a larger dose of ghee once the previous meal has digested, a practice called Avapidaka Sneha.\n\nHolding back belching can cause loss of appetite, tremors, constipation, a stiff heavy chest, bloating, cough and hiccups; it is treated much like suppressed hiccups.\n\nHolding back a sneeze can cause headache, weakness of the senses, neck stiffness and facial palsy; treatment aims to bring the sneeze on and settle Vata, using measures like medicated smoke, collyrium for the eyes, nasal drops, briefly looking toward the sun, and massage, oleation and sweating.\n\nHolding back thirst can cause dryness and wasting, weakness, deafness, fainting, confusion and heart trouble; the answer is cooling measures such as cool drinks and cool baths.\n\nHolding back hunger can cause body ache, loss of appetite, weakness, weight loss, colicky pain and dizziness; the remedy is a light, warm, lightly oiled meal, since fasting lowers digestive power and raises Vata, and a heavy meal afterward only weakens digestion further, while warm oily food soothes the stomach and keeps the digestive fire going.\n\nHolding back sleep can cause confusion, heaviness of the head and eyes, laziness and yawning and body ache; rest and gentle massage set it right.\n\nHolding back a cough makes it worse and can lead to breathing difficulty, loss of appetite, heart trouble, wasting and hiccups; it is treated with the usual cough measures.\n\nHolding back the heavy breathing after exertion can cause abdominal masses, heart trouble and fainting; rest and Vata-settling care are the treatment.\n\nHolding back a yawn produces complaints like those of a suppressed sneeze, and is treated with Vata-balancing care.\n\nHolding back tears can cause a blocked or running nose, eye disorders, painful heart conditions, neck stiffness, loss of appetite, dizziness and abdominal masses; gentle comfort helps, such as sleep and warm, friendly conversation.\n\nHolding back vomiting can cause spreading skin diseases, rashes, other skin disorders, eye problems, itching, anaemia, fever, cough, breathing difficulty, nausea and facial pigmentation; since this mainly disturbs Pitta, treatment leans on clearing Pitta through gargling, medicated smoke, eating dry food and then inducing vomiting, exercise, bloodletting for skin issues, and purgation.\n\nHolding back ejaculation can cause dribbling of semen, groin pain, swelling, fever, cardiac distress, blocked urine, body ache, hernia, stone formation and impotence; care may include nourishing foods, rice, massage and immersion baths of the lower abdomen, bladder cleansing, medicated-milk treatment, and normal marital relations.\n\nWho should not be given these treatments\n\nSome people are not suitable candidates for the corrective therapies above: those in severe pain and thirst, those severely weakened and emaciated, those who habitually suppress their urges (because their Vata keeps getting disturbed and treatment tends not to hold), and those whose condition has gone so far that they vomit faecal matter.\n\nThe root cause of disease\n\nThe chapter states plainly that essentially all these disorders arise from two things: forcing urges that have not arisen, and suppressing urges that have. Because both upset the body's natural order, the dosha chiefly deranged is Vata, so the sensible treatment is food and measures that clear Vata's channels and restore its normal downward flow.\n\nThe urges you should restrain\n\nNot all urges should be let out. The mental and moral urges are the ones to hold back deliberately: greed, envy, hatred, jealousy, and unlawful or excessive craving and attraction. A wise person who masters the senses reins these in, and this restraint is itself part of staying healthy.\n\nCleansing at the right time (Shodhana)\n\nWaste and doshas should be cleared out at the proper seasons, before they pile up, because accumulated toxins can aggravate and, in the worst case, prove fatal. There is an important difference between methods: doshas merely pacified by fasting (langhana) and digestive therapies (pachana) can flare up again later, whereas doshas truly expelled by purification therapy (Shodhana, the Panchakarma procedures) do not return. After proper cleansing at the right time, rejuvenation (Rasayana) with suitable tonics restores and strengthens the body.\n\nRecovering after cleansing therapy\n\nPeople who feel drained after Panchakarma should be built back up gradually with nourishing foods, such as fast-maturing (sixty-day) rice, wheat, green gram, meat and ghee, combined with medicines that are good for the heart and that kindle digestion. Oil massage, powder massage, herbal baths and both decoction and oil enemas help restore the weakened. Through these the person regains health, ease, clear thinking, good vision, strong senses, sexual vigour and long life.\n\nDiseases from outside causes (Agantu)\n\nSome illnesses do not come from within but from external factors: microbes and unseen agents, poisons, violent wind, fire and burns, injuries and wounds, broken bones, and also strong emotions like desire, hatred and fear. These are called Agantu (incoming) diseases.\n\nGeneral conduct that prevents all disease\n\nThe chapter closes with broad guidance for preventing disease of every kind. Avoid wrong actions of body, speech and mind done against good sense, control the senses, learn from past experience and act wisely, understand your environment, the season and your own nature, follow good conduct and healthy routine, keep a steady and peaceful mind, and keep good company. Clear the doshas at their proper seasons too: expel what builds in winter during spring, what builds in summer during the rains, and what builds in the rains during autumn, so seasonal illnesses never take hold. In short, the secret of lasting health is a daily life of wholesome food and activity, good judgement, moderation with the senses, charity, honesty, patience, an even temper and the company of good people."},{"n":5,"title":"Drinks and Liquids (Dravadravya Vijnaniya)","detail":"This chapter sorts every kind of drinkable thing by its nature and its effect on the body and on digestion. It moves through waters, milks and dairy, sugarcane products, honey, oils and fats, alcoholic drinks, fermented sour liquids, and finally animal urine used as medicine.\n\nWaters (Toya varga)\n\nClean rainwater caught fresh in a vessel is treated as the finest water. When it has met sunlight, moonlight and wind it is light, cooling, thin, almost tasteless, soothing to mind and heart, and refreshing to the whole body. Its goodness still depends on where and in which season it falls. A simple old test: good rainwater, poured over boiled rice on a clean silver plate, neither makes the rice soggy nor changes its colour. Seawater is not for drinking except during the autumn month of Ashvayuja, when the star Canopus is said to clear water of its harshness. When clean rainwater is not available, use ground water that resembles it, drawn from an open clean place of black or white soil that gets sun and breeze.\n\nWater to refuse: anything muddy, full of algae, weeds or leaves, shut away from sun and wind, a mix of old and stale with fresh, thick and hard to digest, frothy, wormy, naturally warm, so icy it sets the teeth on edge, out of season, or fouled by spiders, saliva, urine or droppings.\n\nRivers carry the quality of the land they cross. Rivers flowing west into the Arabian Sea with sweet clean water are wholesome; east-flowing rivers are less so. Mountain streams that tumble and churn over rocks are healthy, but the same water left to stagnate breeds worms and brings on filariasis and diseases of the heart, throat and head. Different hill ranges give water with different faults, from piles to swellings of the belly to skin disease and anaemia, while sea water disturbs all three doshas. Well and pond water takes on the character of the surrounding terrain.\n\nWhen to drink and how much: those with weak digestion, abdominal tumours, anaemia, swollen belly, diarrhoea, piles, duodenal disease or dropsy should drink little or no plain water. Even healthy people should keep their water intake modest in every season except autumn and summer. Timing around meals matters too: sipping during the meal is the balanced habit, drinking a lot after the meal tends to put on weight, and drinking before the meal tends to thin a person down.\n\nTemperature of water: cold water settles alcoholic hangover, exhaustion, fainting, vomiting, fatigue, giddiness, thirst, burning heat, bleeding and Pitta trouble, and helps against poison. Hot water kindles appetite, aids digestion, is good for the throat, light, and cleanses the bladder; it relieves hiccup, bloating, aggravated Vata and Kapha, recent fever, cough, undigested residue (ama), running nose, breathlessness and pain in the flanks, and suits the days of cleansing therapy. Water that has been boiled and then cooled does not clog the tissues, stays light, and suits Pitta conditions. Water left standing overnight is best avoided because it disturbs all three doshas. Tender coconut water is sweet, oily, cooling, light, mildly aphrodisiac, kindles appetite, clears the bladder, quenches thirst and calms Pitta and Vata. In the rains, fresh rainwater is the best drink and river water the least suitable.\n\nMilk and dairy (Ksheera varga)\n\nMilk as a class is sweet both in taste and after digestion, oily, cooling, heavy, nourishing to all tissues, building of ojas (vital essence), mildly aphrodisiac, calming to Vata and Pitta, and increasing to Kapha. Cow milk promotes long life, rejuvenates, sharpens intellect, strengthens, helps breast-milk flow, eases the bowels, and is used for exhaustion, giddiness, intoxication, breathlessness, cough, extreme hunger and thirst, long-standing fever, painful urination and bleeding disorders. Buffalo milk is heavier and cooling, good for someone with very strong digestion and trouble sleeping. Goat milk is light because the goat ranges widely and eats sharp light fodder; it helps wasting, fever, breathlessness, Pitta bleeding and diarrhoea. Camel milk is slightly dry, warm, salty, light, kindles digestion and suits Vata and Kapha, worms, swelling, belly disease and piles. Human breast milk soothes Vata and Pitta and blood disorders and is used in the eyes and nose for local treatment. Ewe milk is warm, not good for the heart, raises Pitta and Kapha and can trigger hiccup and breathing trouble. Elephant milk is strengthening. Milk of single-hoofed animals like the horse is very hot, slightly sour and salty, and makes a person dull and lazy. Raw milk clogs the channels, is heavy and causes ama; properly boiled it is like nectar, and milk taken straight from a healthy udder is likewise excellent.\n\nCurd (Dadhi) is sour in taste and after digestion, absorbent (useful in diarrhoea), heavy, warming, calms Vata, and increases fat, semen, strength, Kapha, bleeding tendency, digestive fire and swelling, while whetting the appetite; it helps loss of appetite, recurrent fever, running nose, painful urination and malabsorption. Important curd rules: not at night, not heated, not with green-gram soup, and not combined with honey, ghee, sugar or sour fruit; and not eaten every single day, since daily use can bring on fever, bleeding disorders, skin disease, anaemia and dizziness.\n\nButtermilk (Takra), the churned curd with fat removed, is light, sour-astringent, kindles digestion and calms Kapha and Vata; it is valued for swelling, belly disease, piles, malabsorption, urinary trouble, poor appetite, enlarged spleen, abdominal lumps, over-use of ghee, chronic poisoning and anaemia. Whey (Mastu), the watery part, resembles buttermilk: it moves fluids through the channels, cleanses them, relieves constipation and is light. Fresh butter (Navanita) is aphrodisiac, cooling, improves complexion, strength and digestion, is absorbent, balances Vata, Pitta and blood, and helps wasting, piles, facial palsy and cough; butter churned straight from milk is absorbent and good for bleeding and eye disorders. Ghee is praised as the best of all fats: it sharpens intellect, memory and digestion, lengthens life, improves vision and vigour, suits children, the old, the delicate and those wanting children, and treats Vata and Pitta disorders, poison, insanity, wasting and fever; it is cooling and preserves youth, and responds to a thousand medicinal preparations. Old ghee is used for intoxication, epilepsy, fainting, and diseases of head, ear, eye and genitals, and it cleanses and heals wounds. Thick milk products like kilata, piyusha (first milk after calving), kurchika and morana are strengthening, increase semen, sleep and Kapha, cause constipation, are heavy and disturb the doshas. Cow milk and cow ghee are rated best, ewe milk and ewe ghee the least.\n\nSugarcane group (Ikshu varga)\n\nSugarcane juice is laxative, heavy, oily, nourishing and weight-building, increases Kapha and urine, is mildly aphrodisiac, cooling, helps bleeding disorders and is sweet. Juice crushed with the root, shoot tip and wormy parts turns impure and then causes burning, indigestion and constipation; machine-pressed juice tends to this fault. The Paundraka cane is best for its coolness and sweetness, then Vamsika, then rougher kinds that are slightly alkaline and burning. Phanita (half-cooked molasses) is heavy, clogging, mildly disturbs all doshas and increases urine. Jaggery (Guda), when well washed and purified, does not raise Kapha much and increases stool and urine; poorly made jaggery breeds worms and Kapha trouble in the tissues. Old jaggery is good for the heart; fresh jaggery raises Kapha and causes indigestion. Refined sugars in rising order of quality are brown sugar (matsyandika), sugar candy (khanda) and white crystal sugar (sita); all are aphrodisiac, good for the wasted and wounded, and help bleeding and Vata. Yavasa sugar is bitter-sweet and astringent. All sugars soothe burning, thirst, vomiting, fainting and bleeding. Among cane products, sugar is best and phanita the least.\n\nHoney (Madhu)\n\nHoney is good for the eyes, breaks up hardened masses and Kapha, quenches thirst, and calms Kapha; it helps poison, hiccup, bleeding, diabetes and urinary disease, skin disease, worms, vomiting, breathlessness, cough and diarrhoea, cleanses and quickly heals wounds, is drying and astringent-sweet, and slightly raises Vata. Crystallised honey is similar. The famous caution: honey turns harmful if heated, taken by someone very overheated, in hot weather or a hot land, or mixed into hot food. It does no harm, though, when used warm to induce vomiting or in a decoction enema, because it leaves the body before it is digested.\n\nOils and fats (Taila varga)\n\nAn oil generally carries the nature of its seed. Sesame oil is the chief of oils: it cleanses the skin, is good for the eyes, penetrates deep, is warm and reduces Kapha, suits both the obese and the thin, relieves constipation and worms, and becomes a remedy for many diseases when processed with herbs. Castor oil is bitter-pungent-sweet, laxative, heavy, deeply penetrating, hot and foul-smelling, and treats hernia, abdominal lumps, Vata-Kapha disease, ascites, intermittent fever and pains and swellings of waist, genitals, belly and back; red castor oil is even stronger. Mustard oil is pungent, hot, penetrating, reduces Kapha, semen and Vata, is light, and can cause bleeding, skin rashes, piles, ulcers and worms. Vibhitaka (aksha) oil is sweet, cooling, good for hair, heavy, and calms Pitta and Vata. Neem oil is mildly hot, bitter, anti-microbial, good for skin disease and reduces Kapha. Linseed and safflower oils are hot and aggravate Kapha and Pitta and cause skin disease. Animal fats, marrow and muscle-fat calm Vata, add strength, Pitta and Kapha, and carry the quality of the animal they come from.\n\nAlcoholic drinks (Madya varga)\n\nAs a group, wines kindle digestion, improve taste, penetrate deeply, are hot, give satisfaction and nourishment, taste slightly sweet-bitter-pungent and sour after digestion, are laxative and slightly astringent, improve voice, health, talent and complexion, are light, suit both over-sleepers and the sleepless and both lean and stout people, and clear the minute channels and calm Vata and Kapha, all when used wisely; used wrongly they act like poison. Fresh wine is heavy and raises all doshas; aged wine reduces them. Wine should not be taken hot, nor during purgation therapy, nor on an empty stomach, and very strong, very weak, very clear, very cloudy or spoilt wines should be avoided. Sura (a rice beer) helps abdominal lumps, swollen belly, piles and duodenal disease, is lubricating and heavy, calms Vata and increases fat, blood, milk, urine and Kapha. Vibhitaka sura is mildly intoxicating, light and relatively harmless. Barley sura constipates, is hard to digest and raises all doshas. Arishta (fermented decoction) is the most intoxicating, carries the qualities of its herbs, and treats duodenal disease, anaemia, skin disease, piles, dropsy, swollen belly, fever, lumps, worms and spleen disorder. Grape wine (mardvika) scrapes, is good for the heart, sweet, laxative, mildly raises Pitta and Vata, and helps anaemia, diabetes, piles and worms. Date wine is inferior to grape wine and raises Vata. Sugar wine is fragrant, sweet, heart-friendly, lightly intoxicating and light; molasses wine (gauda) increases urine, stool and flatus and is nourishing. Sidhu from sugarcane raises Vata and Pitta and reduces Kapha disease, fat, dropsy and piles. Honey wine (madhvasava) breaks up masses, penetrates deep and helps diabetes, nasal catarrh and cough. Sukta (fermented from roots and tubers) moistens blood and Kapha, drives Vata downward, is very hot and penetrating, sour, heart-friendly, appetising and laxative, cool to the touch, and helps anaemia, eye disease and worms. Asava is a similar fermented infusion.\n\nSour fermented liquids and urine\n\nDhanyamla, made by fermenting the water in which grains and pulses were washed or lightly cooked, is purgative, penetrating, hot, raises Pitta, feels cool, relieves fatigue, sharpens appetite, helps bladder pain, is heart-friendly and light, and balances Vata and Kapha; sauviraka and tushodaka, made from barley with and without husk, are similar and help worms, heart disease, lumps, piles and anaemia. Finally, the urine of cow, goat, sheep, buffalo, elephant, horse, camel and donkey is described as a medicine: it raises Pitta, is dry, penetrating, hot, pungent-salty and light, and is used against worms, dropsy, swollen belly, flatulence, colic, anaemia, Kapha and Vata, lumps, loss of taste, poisoning, leucoderma, skin disease and piles."},{"n":6,"title":"Foods and Diet (Annaswaroopa Vijnaniya)","detail":"This chapter catalogues solid foods by type and explains how each affects the body and the three doshas. It works through grains and cereals, pulses and legumes, prepared dishes, meats sorted by habitat, leafy vegetables and roots, fruits, and finally spices, salts and medicinal condiments.\n\nSpiked grains and rice (Shuka Dhanya varga)\n\nMany varieties of rice are named as fit for eating. Rice in general is sweet in taste and after digestion, oily, mildly aphrodisiac, causes only a little stool, slightly astringent, light, cooling and increases urine, and it is suitable for daily use. Among all kinds, red rice (Raktashali) is the best; it quenches thirst and balances all three doshas. Big-grained rice comes next, then kalama, and so on down the list. Certain late or inferior varieties such as yavaka are sweet but hot, heavy, oily, turn sour after digestion and raise Kapha and Pitta. Shashtika rice, the kind that ripens in sixty days, is highly praised: oily, absorbent, light, sweet, balancing to all three doshas, steadying and cooling, with a white and a blackish-white type. Other grasses grains such as kangu, kodrava, neevara and shyamaka are cooling, light, scraping, raise Vata and settle Kapha and Pitta; priyangu among them helps heal fractures and koradusha is absorbent, cooling and anti-poisonous.\n\nBarley (Yava) is dry, cooling, heavy, sweet, laxative, increases stool and flatus, is mildly aphrodisiac and steadying, and helps urinary disorders, Pitta-Kapha states, running nose, breathlessness and asthma, cough, thigh stiffness, throat disease and skin disease. Wheat (Godhuma) is mildly aphrodisiac, cooling, heavy, oily, enlivening, calms Vata and Pitta, heals fractures and wounds, is sweet, steadying, laxative and fit for daily use.\n\nPulses and legumes (Shimbidhanya varga)\n\nGreen gram, toor dal, lentils and their relatives are, as a group, constipating, astringent-sweet, absorbent, pungent after digestion, cooling and light, and they reduce fat, Kapha, blood and Pitta, and are used in external pastes and baths. Green gram (mudga) is the best, raising Vata only mildly. Round pea (kalaya) raises Vata and dryness and makes more stool. Rajamasha (a big black gram) raises Vata, is dry and heavy. Horse gram (kulattha) is hot, sour after digestion, cleanses semen, and helps urinary stones, breathlessness, running nose, cough and Kapha-Vata disease, but it worsens bleeding disorders. Flat bean (nishpava) aggravates Vata, Pitta and bleeding, increases breast milk and urine, is heavy and burning, and is not good for the eyes or semen. Black gram (masha) is oily, strengthening, raises Kapha and Pitta, increases stool, is laxative, heavy, hot, calms Vata, is sweet and strongly increases semen and sexual strength. Sesame (tila) is hot in potency yet cool to the touch, good for skin and hair, strengthening, heavy, gives little urine, pungent after digestion, sharpens intellect and digestion, and raises Kapha and Pitta. Freshly harvested grain clogs the channels, while grain a year old, or dehusked, or properly fried, is lighter.\n\nPrepared foods (Kritanna varga)\n\nRice cooked with water gives four stages: manda (the thin water-like liquid drained off first), peya (a little thicker but still liquid), vilepi (more grain, less liquid), and odana (solid cooked rice). They grow heavier in that order, so manda is the lightest and odana the heaviest. Manda moves stool and flatus, relieves thirst and exhaustion, clears residual doshas (hence its use after cleansing therapy), aids digestion and restores the tissues. Peya relieves hunger, thirst, exhaustion, weakness, belly disease and fever and kindles appetite. Vilepi holds in fluids, is heart-friendly, relieves thirst and suits those with ulcers, eye disease, the weak and those given oils before therapy. Well-washed, fully dried odana is light; odana cooked with hot-potency herb decoctions or fried grain is also light, while odana cooked with milk or meat is heavy. Meat soup (mamsa rasa) is nourishing, satisfying, aphrodisiac, good for the eyes and wound-healing. Green-gram soup suits those after cleansing therapy and those with ulcers, throat and eye disease. Horse-gram soup normalises Vata and helps abdominal lumps and groin pain. Several processed items are flagged as heavy and dosha-aggravating, including sesame cake, dried leafy vegetables and especially sprouted grains, which Ayurveda does not recommend. Puffed and flaked rice (laja, prithuka), roasted flour (saktu), vesavara (spiced minced meat or pulse) and pinyaka (oil-cake residue) each have detailed rules; roasted corn flour in particular should be taken with water, not at night, not twice a day, not alone and not after a meal. Food baked directly over coal digests more easily than steamed food.\n\nMeats (Mamsa varga)\n\nAnimals are grouped by habitat into eight kinds, which fall under three broad regions: dry-land (jangala, Vata-dominant), wetland (anupa, Kapha-dominant) and in-between (sadharana, balancing). Jangala meats such as deer, rabbit and the land birds are the best: cooling, light, mildly constipating and good in mixed-dosha fever. Rabbit (shasha) enhances appetite and is cooling. Quail and partridge meats are building, and partridge in particular increases intelligence, digestion, strength and semen. Peacock is good for the ears, voice, eyes and ageing. Wild fowl is aphrodisiac, while domestic fowl is heavy and increases Kapha. Burrow-dwellers are heavy, hot, strengthening and increase urine and semen. Big animals (mahamriga) are cooling, and carnivores among them suit long-standing piles, duodenal disease and wasting. Goat meat is a balanced choice: not too cooling, matching the body's own make-up, non-clogging and nourishing without aggravating the doshas. Sheep is the opposite and fattening. Cow meat is described as treating dry cough, exhaustion, excess hunger, intermittent fever and nasal catarrh. Buffalo is hot, heavy and sleep-inducing; pig relieves fatigue and builds strength and semen; fish mostly increase Kapha. Rules on meat: use freshly killed, uncontaminated meat of adult animals, and reject meat of dead, diseased, drowned, poisoned, very thin or very fatty animals. Organs, and the upper body of males and lower body of females, are heavier to digest.\n\nLeafy vegetables (Shaka varga)\n\nMany greens are listed. As a rule, leaves, then flowers, then unripe fruit, then stalks, then tubers grow progressively heavier. Most bitter greens are cooling, pungent after digestion, hold in fluids, raise Vata and reduce Kapha and Pitta. Jivanti is rated the best leafy green and mustard greens the worst. Specific greens: patha and its group balance all three doshas and are absorbent; kakamachi (black nightshade) balances the doshas, helps skin disease and rejuvenates; changeri (wood sorrel) is sour, kindles digestion and helps piles. Patola is heart-friendly and anti-worm; vasa cures cough and bleeding; bitter gourd (karavella) kindles digestion and reduces Kapha and Pitta. Brinjal (eggplant) is pungent-bitter-sweet, hot, reduces Kapha and Vata, kindles digestion and does not raise Pitta much. Spinach-type greens (palankya, upodika) relieve intoxication; vidari and jivanti are nourishing and rejuvenating. Ash gourd (kushmanda) is the best of the gourds, calms Vata and Pitta, cleanses the bladder and is aphrodisiac. Radish (mulaka) is medicinal when tender but all-dosha-aggravating when big and mature; cooked with fat it calms Vata. Pungent greens like holy basil (tulasi), drumstick and the mint family kindle appetite and digestion, penetrate deep and are good for the heart but can harm the eyes and semen. Garlic (lashuna) is a strong rejuvenator; onion is weaker; carrot suits Kapha-Vata piles; yam (surana) is excellent for piles.\n\nFruits (Phala varga)\n\nGrapes (draksha) are the best of fruits: aphrodisiac, good for the eyes, laxative, sweet, oily, cooling and heavy, and they soothe Vata, Pitta and blood disorders, thirst, cough, fever and respiratory conditions. Pomegranate (dadima) subdues excess Pitta and the other doshas, is sweet, heart-friendly, light, absorbent and appetising. Many building, sweet, cooling, heavy fruits such as banana, dates, jackfruit, coconut and others relieve burning, chest injury and bleeding but increase Kapha and semen. Bael (bilva) is good unripe (kindles digestion, calms Vata-Kapha) and troublesome ripe. Jamun is cooling, absorbent, drying and Vata-aggravating. Mango changes with ripeness: unripe raises Vata, blood and Pitta, ripe calms Vata but adds Kapha and semen. Sour fruits (amla, kola and the like) mostly aid digestion and move the bowels. Lakucha is rated the worst fruit.\n\nSpices, salts and medicinal condiments (Ausadha varga)\n\nAll salts are penetrating, soften the stool, calm Vata, aid digestion and raise Kapha and Pitta. Rock salt (saindhava) is the best, mildly sweet, balancing all three doshas and not burning. Sochal and bida salts aid digestion and relieve bloating and belching, while common sea salt is heavier and raises Kapha. Alkalis (kshara) are very hot, penetrating, anti-worm and digestive but harmful to the eyes, hair and semen in excess. Asafoetida (hingu) calms Vata and Kapha and relieves bloating and colic. The three myrobalans (triphala: haritaki, amalaki, vibhitaki) are great rejuvenators; haritaki alone is a far-reaching remedy, and amalaki is cooling and calms Pitta and Kapha. The warming spice groups follow: cinnamon-cardamom (trijata and chaturjata), black pepper, long pepper (pippali, not to be over-used), and ginger (good fresh and dry), with the compound groups trikatu and panchakola for digestion, and the various panchamula root groups used medicinally."},{"n":7,"title":"Protecting Food and the Pillars of Life","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis chapter, called Anna Raksha Vidhi (the method of guarding food), begins with the safety of a king's meals and the detection of poison, then moves into incompatible food combinations (Viruddha ahara), and finally lays out the three supports of a healthy life: food, sleep and a balanced sex life.\n\nThe royal physician\n\nVagbhata advises that a king keep his physician living close to the palace so the doctor can stay watchful at all hours. The reasoning is practical. The king's food and drink must be shielded from poison because his health decides the welfare of the whole kingdom. In modern terms this is simply a strong argument for food safety and for having trusted people watch over what we eat.\n\nHow poisoned food looks\n\nThe text describes signs that food has been tampered with. Boiled rice laced with poison turns unusually thick, does not boil over, takes a long time to cook, and spoils quickly after cooking. It may give off steam tinged blue like a peacock's neck, and the fumes can cause giddiness, fainting and heavy drooling. Poisoned food rapidly loses its natural colour, smell, taste and texture and turns watery with shiny specks.\n\nSide dishes dry out fast and look dirty. A reflection seen in the dish appears distorted or vanishes. Froth, lines, threads and bubbles form on the surface. Sweet syrups, puddings, vegetables and meat split into liquid and solid parts and taste wrong. Tell-tale coloured streaks appear in different foods: blue lines in meat soup, coppery lines in milk, dark lines in curd, yellow-white lines in buttermilk, watery lines on ghee, and so on. Fruit ripens or rots too fast, soft foods turn hard and hard foods turn soft, flowers in a garland split and smell odd, cloth develops stains, and metal and stone vessels lose their shine while clay pots oddly gain shine. These are, in plain terms, a long checklist of unnatural changes that warn the food is unsafe.\n\nThe poisoner and the fire test\n\nA person about to poison food often shows nervous signs: a dry, discoloured face, darting eyes, sweating, trembling, fear, stumbling speech and frequent yawning. As a test, poisoned food thrown on fire crackles, makes a single tall flame with no swirl, gives off blue peacock-coloured smoke or sometimes no flame at all, and smells foul. Animals react too: bees die, a crow's voice weakens, certain birds cry out, and a cat becomes agitated. After confirming poison this way, the food must be discarded with care.\n\nEffects of poisoned food and its treatment\n\nTouching poisoned food causes itching, burning over the body, fever, pain, eruptions, numbness, loss of nails and hair, and swelling. Treatment is washing and bathing with water medicated with anti-poison herbs, and pastes of cooling herbs such as Ushira (khus), sandalwood, Padmaka, Talisa, Kushta and Guduchi. In the mouth it causes drooling, a numb tongue and lips, burning, tingling teeth and a stiff jaw; the remedy is medicated gargles. In the stomach it brings sweating, fainting, bloating, giddiness, vomiting, burning and dark spots on the skin. In the intestines it brings multi-coloured vomiting, excess urine, purging, drowsiness, wasting, pallor and swelling of the belly. For these, vomiting and purging therapies are used, followed by nasal medicine, collyrium and medicated decoctions.\n\nFor deep or chronic poisoning (Gara visha), after clearing the gut with vomiting and purging, purified metal preparations of copper and then gold are given over a long period. The idea expressed is that the body becomes so resistant that poison slides off it like a water drop off a lotus leaf.\n\nIncompatible foods (Viruddha ahara)\n\nSome foods, when eaten together or prepared in certain ways, behave like poison in the body even though each item alone is harmless. Vagbhata treats these incompatible combinations as a form of slow poisoning. Examples he lists include: marshland meat with black gram, honey, milk or sprouted grains; fish with milk, jaggery or radish; all sour things with milk; sour fruits with milk; curd with hot foods or at the wrong time; honey mixed in equal parts with ghee; honey with hot water; equal amounts of honey and ghee together; heating honey; fish fried in oil then processed with long pepper; and ghee stored in a bronze vessel for more than ten days. Certain meats cooked over particular woods or in particular fats are described as outright deadly. Milk after eating radish or leafy greens is also discouraged.\n\nThe working definition is simple: anything that stirs up the Doshas (the body's functional energies, Vata, Pitta, Kapha) but does not clear them out of the body is incompatible. The text contrasts this with Panchakarma (cleansing therapy), where Doshas are deliberately raised but then expelled, so no harm follows.\n\nWho is less affected, and how to change habits safely\n\nPeople who exercise regularly, are used to oily food, have strong digestion, and are young and robust suffer little from incompatible foods. Those long accustomed to a food, or who eat only small amounts of it, also escape harm. The chapter gives a gentle rule for changing diet or habits: drop an unhealthy habit gradually, by a quarter at a time, and take up a healthy one gradually, with gaps of one, two and three days. Sudden switches in either direction can themselves cause disease. Easing off the bad and easing in the good builds lasting health.\n\nThe three pillars of life\n\nThree things support the body like pillars hold up a house: Ahara (food), Nidra (sleep) and Brahmacharya rightly used (a balanced, disciplined sex life). Used well, all three sustain health; misused, they undermine it.\n\nSleep (Nidra)\n\nHappiness and misery, nourishment or wasting, strength or weakness, virility or impotence, knowledge or dullness, and even life and death all depend on sleep. Three faults harm health: sleeping at the wrong time, sleeping too much, and not sleeping at all. Staying awake at night is drying to the body, daytime sleep is moistening, and a short sitting nap is neither. Daytime sleep is fine in summer, when the body is drier and nights are short, but in other seasons it tends to raise Kapha and Pitta. Day sleep suits those worn out by hard work, travel, grief, illness, the very old and the very young. It is discouraged for the obese, those with heavy Kapha, and those on oily diets. Sleeping at the wrong time causes dullness, fever, catarrh, headache and poor digestion, treated with light fasting and cleansing measures. Too much sleep is reduced by stimulating measures; too little sleep causes body aches, heaviness, yawning, fatigue and Vata disorders, and is eased by milk, nourishing food, oil massage, bathing, soothing surroundings and contentment of mind.\n\nA balanced sex life (Brahmacharya / Abrahmacharya)\n\nThe chapter gives detailed cautions on when and with whom intimacy should be avoided, and notes that moderation varies by season, more freely allowed in the cold seasons and much less in the rains and summer. Excess drains strength, causes giddiness, weak thighs, dull senses and premature ageing, while disciplined conduct supports memory, intelligence, long life and slow ageing. After intimacy, rest, bathing, cooling drinks and sleep help the body recover its vigour. The chapter closes by saying a king who entrusts his care to a learned, skilled and kind physician enjoys health, strength, fame and long life."},{"n":8,"title":"How Much to Eat, Eating Rules and Indigestion","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis chapter, called Matrashiteeya (eating the right measure), is one of the most practical in the whole text. It explains how much to eat, what happens when you eat too little or too much, the types of indigestion (Ajirna) and their treatment, the correct way and time to eat, what foods suit daily use, what should be avoided, and the role of after-meal drinks (Anupana).\n\nThe right amount of food\n\nOne should always eat the proper quantity, because the right amount keeps Agni (the digestive fire, the body's power to process food) working well. Both light foods and heavy foods need to be eaten in a measured quantity. The rule of thumb: heavy foods (oily dishes, meat, sweets) should fill only about half of your comfortable capacity, while light foods can be taken a little more but still short of full satiety. The real test of the right amount is simple, you have eaten the right quantity if the food digests comfortably without discomfort.\n\nEating too little or too much\n\nEating too little does not build strength, immunity (Ojas) or nourishment, and over time it leads to Vata disorders. Eating too much is just as bad: it suddenly aggravates all the Doshas at once. Mixed with undigested food (Ama), this produces disease. Overeating can block the movement of food and cause Alasaka, or force food out violently in both directions (vomiting and diarrhoea) causing Visuchika, a gastroenteritis-like state. These happen in people who cannot control their urge to overeat.\n\nAlasaka, Visuchika and Dandalasaka\n\nIn Alasaka the food neither comes up by vomiting nor passes down, and does not digest, it just lies lazily in the stomach. It arises in weak people with poor digestion who habitually suppress natural urges; here Vata joins Kapha to block the food, causing colicky pain without vomiting or diarrhoea. In Visuchika, sharply aggravated Vata with other Doshas causes pain all over the body as if pricked by needles. Doshas express differently: Vata brings pain, giddiness, bloating, tremor and stiffness; Pitta brings fever, diarrhoea, internal burning, thirst and collapse; Kapha brings vomiting, body heaviness, difficult speech and excess salivation. A severe form, Dandalasaka, occurs when badly vitiated Doshas spread into wrong channels and stiffen the body like a log; it is said to be very hard to treat because it kills quickly.\n\nAma Dosha, the root problem\n\nAma is food that has not been properly digested and turns toxic. It builds up from eating incompatible foods, eating too much, and eating again on top of undigested food. The text calls severe Ama almost like a poison (Amavisha) and very dangerous because the treatments the different Doshas need can oppose one another.\n\nTreatment of Alasaka, Visuchika and indigestion\n\nFor Alasaka, the lodged undigested food is cleared quickly, if the case is manageable, by inducing vomiting with warm water mixed with Vacha, rock salt and Madanaphala, followed by sweating therapy, then suppositories to move stool and gas downward, and fomentation of stiff body parts. For advanced Visuchika the texts used heat application at the heel and a day of fasting, with care like that given after purgation. A crucial warning: a person with indigestion and severe belly pain should not take painkilling drugs, because the digestive fire, already clogged with Ama, cannot handle the drug on top of the Doshas and food, and this can be fatal. Only after the food is digested, if heaviness and stiffness remain, are medicines given to digest the residue and rekindle Agni.\n\nThe core principle is Apatarpana, de-nourishing or lightening, matched to how much Ama is present: if Ama is small, simple fasting (Langhana) is enough; if moderate, fasting plus digestive measures (Pachana); if heavy, full cleansing therapy (Shodhana) to pull the Doshas and Ama out by the root. More broadly, diseases are first treated by removing their cause (Hetu Viparyaya); if they persist, by disease-specific treatment (Vyadhi Viparyaya). Once Doshas, Ama and digestion are back to normal, strengthening oleation and enema therapies restore lost vigour.\n\nTypes of indigestion (Ajirna)\n\nFour main types are described. Ama ajirna (from Kapha) brings puffy eyes and cheeks, belching that tastes of the meal just eaten, drooling, nausea and heaviness. Vishtabdha ajirna (from Vata) brings belly pain, constipation, gas and weakness. Vidagdha ajirna (from Pitta) brings thirst, fainting, giddiness, sour belching and burning. A fifth, Vilambika (from Kapha and Vata together), comes from deep Ama blocking the channels. Rasashesha ajirna is a mild leftover indigestion with low enthusiasm and chest discomfort despite clean belching. Treatment matches the type: fasting for Ama ajirna, sweating for Vishtabdha, induced vomiting for Vidagdha, and for any type, resting during the day without food, then eating only a little light food once true hunger returns.\n\nGeneral signs and other causes\n\nGeneral signs of indigestion are constipation or loose stool, exhaustion, bloating, heaviness and dizziness. Overeating is not the only cause of Ama. Food that is disliked, gas-forming, over-cooked or undercooked, hard to digest, dry, very cold, dirty, or soaked in too much water fails to digest. So does food eaten while grief-stricken, angry or over-hungry.\n\nBad eating habits (Dushta Ashana)\n\nThree harmful patterns are named. Samashana is mixing suitable and unsuitable foods together. Adhyashana is eating a fresh meal before the last one is digested. Vishamashana is eating too much or too little at the wrong time. All three can cause serious disease or even death.\n\nThe right way to eat (Ahara vidhi)\n\nEat at the proper time; eat clean, familiar, wholesome food that is warm, unctuous (a little oily or moist) and easy to digest. Eat with full attention. Include all six tastes, with sweet predominating, and eat neither too fast nor too slowly. Eat after bathing, when genuinely hungry, in a quiet place, after washing hands, feet and face. Do not criticise the food, do not chatter too much, favour some liquid food, and eat food you like, in good company, served by clean and trusted people. Avoid food fouled with grass or hair, reheated food, food that is mostly poor vegetables or inferior grains, and food that is too hot or too salty.\n\nFilling the stomach in parts\n\nA famous and simple rule: divide the stomach into three parts, fill two with solid food, one with liquids, and leave one part empty for the movement of air (Vata). In other words, never eat to the point of being stuffed; leave room so digestion can work.\n\nWhat to eat daily, and what to avoid\n\nFoods fit for daily use include rice, wheat, barley, Shashtika rice, meat of dry-land animals, green gram, young radish, Haritaki, Amla, dry grapes, sugar, ghee, pure water, milk, honey, pomegranate and rock salt. Triphala with honey and ghee at night is recommended for the eyes. Foods not to be eaten habitually include curd, certain fermented dairy products, alkalis, fermented gruels, uncooked radish, meat of weak or dried animals, black gram, sprouted grains, dried vegetables and half-cooked molasses.\n\nOrder within a meal\n\nHard, heavy, sweet, oily and slow foods, such as sugarcane, mango and sweets, are best taken at the start of the meal; opposite, lighter foods at the end; and sour and salty foods in the middle.\n\nAfter-meal drinks (Anupana)\n\nAn ideal after-drink has qualities opposite to the food but not incompatible with it; it invigorates, satisfies, helps food move and soften, and aids digestion. Cold water suits meals of barley and wheat, and follows curd, wine, honey. Warm water suits starchy foods, whey, buttermilk, fermented gruel and legume dishes. Fermented liquor suits the lean; honey water suits the obese; meat soup suits the emaciated; and milk is like nectar for those weakened by illness, long walking, speaking, intimacy, fasting or sun, and for the aged and children. After-drinks are not good in diseases above the shoulders (ear, nose, throat, eyes), in breathing disorders, cough, chest injury, hoarseness and for singers. They should be avoided by the over-hydrated and those with polyuria or ulcers. Everyone, well or sick, should avoid speaking, long walking, sleeping, sun, fire and travel immediately after drinking or eating.\n\nThe right time to eat\n\nThe ideal time to eat is after passing stool and urine, when the mind is calm, the Doshas are moving normally, belching is clean, hunger is clear, gas passes downward easily, digestion is keen, the senses are clear and the body feels light. Eating then, by the rules above, is the ideal."},{"n":9,"title":"How Substances and Medicines Act","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis chapter, called Dravyadi Vijnaneeya (knowledge of substances and their properties), is the conceptual core of Ayurvedic pharmacology. It explains what a substance (Dravya) is made of, where its qualities come from, and the five ways any food or medicine acts in the body: taste (Rasa), quality (Guna), potency (Virya), post-digestive effect (Vipaka) and special effect (Prabhava).\n\nThe substance comes first\n\nThe substance itself is treated as the most important thing, because all the other properties, taste and the rest, reside in it. Every substance is made of the five great elements (Panchabhuta): Earth (Prithvi), Water (Ap), Fire (Tejas), Air (Vayu) and Ether (Akasha). These combine inseparably, and the element that dominates gives the substance its basic nature. This is drawn from Sankhya philosophy: everything in the universe is built from these five elements in different proportions, which is why the world holds such endless variety. Each element carries a signature quality: Earth gives smell and heaviness, Water gives taste, Fire gives appearance, Air gives touch, and Ether gives sound.\n\nClassifying substances by dominant element\n\nA substance named by its dominant element: earth-dominant (Parthiva) means solid, heavy, stable and nourishing; water-dominant (Apya) means liquid, cold, moist, lubricating, binding and satisfying; fire-dominant (Agneya) means dry, sharp, hot, and causing burning, colour, lustre and digestion; air-dominant (Vayaviya) means dry, light, clear, mobile and drying, tending to cause lightness and even exhaustion; ether-dominant (Nabhasa) means subtle, clear, light, and producing hollowness and lightness. An important related rule: fire and air dominant substances tend to move upward in the body, while earth and water dominant substances tend to move downward.\n\nPrimary and secondary tastes\n\nBecause substances are made of several elements combined, no substance has only one taste, and no disease arises from only one Dosha. The taste perceived clearly is the primary taste (Rasa); a taste felt faintly or only at the end is the secondary taste (Anurasa). The qualities of a substance (like heaviness) are spoken of as belonging to its taste, simply because they coexist so closely.\n\nNothing is without use as medicine\n\nA famous statement: there is nothing in the universe that cannot be used as medicine. What matters is knowing the substance and the right purpose and method of using it. The skill of the physician lies in matching substance to need.\n\nPotency (Virya)\n\nVirya is the potency or strength through which a substance acts; the master Charaka says no action is possible without Virya, and every action happens through it. There are two main views. One view counts eight potencies, as four pairs of qualities: heavy and light, unctuous (oily) and dry, cold and hot, soft and sharp. These eight are singled out from the twenty qualities because they are the strongest, most common, and most useful in practice. The second and more widely used view holds that only two potencies truly matter, Hot (Ushna) and Cold (Sheeta), because among all elements only Fire (Agni) and Water (Soma) are the supremely powerful forces in nature.\n\nActions of hot and cold potency\n\nHot potency (Ushna Virya) tends to cause giddiness, thirst, exhaustion, sweating and burning, speeds up transformation and cooking of food, and calms Vata and Kapha. Cold potency (Sheeta Virya) is soothing and enlivening: it satisfies and refreshes, restrains and steadies, purifies the blood and calms Pitta.\n\nPost-digestive effect (Vipaka)\n\nVipaka is the change in taste that a substance undergoes at the very end of digestion, under the action of the stomach's fire (Jatharagni). There are three Vipakas. Sweet and salty tastes become sweet Vipaka. Sour stays sour. Bitter, pungent and astringent generally become pungent Vipaka. The effect of a substance after digestion mirrors the effect of the matching taste in the mouth, so a sweet taste and a sweet Vipaka act similarly.\n\nHow a substance acts, and which property wins\n\nA substance can act through its taste, its Vipaka, its qualities, its Virya, or its special effect. Whichever of these is strongest in a given substance dominates and shows its effect, suppressing the weaker properties; and when two opposite qualities meet, the stronger overpowers the weaker. When properties are equal in strength, there is a natural pecking order: Vipaka overrides Rasa; Virya overrides both Rasa and Vipaka; and Prabhava (special effect) overrides all of them. This hierarchy is how Ayurveda predicts what a substance will finally do in the body.\n\nSpecial effect (Prabhava)\n\nPrabhava is a special, unexplained action a substance shows even when its taste, quality, Virya and Vipaka are the same as another substance. For example, Danti and Chitraka share similar tastes and qualities, yet Danti purges and Chitraka does not, so purgation is Danti's Prabhava. Dry grapes have a mild purgative action that licorice, otherwise similar, lacks. Ghee and milk are alike, yet ghee increases digestive strength while milk does not, so that is ghee's special effect. Prabhava is, in effect, the unique signature action that cannot be derived from the ordinary properties.\n\nExtraordinary substances (Vichitra Pratyayarabdha)\n\nFinally, some substances act in peculiar ways because of peculiar internal combinations. Wheat and barley are both sweet and heavy, yet wheat calms Vata while barley aggravates it. Fish and milk are both sweet and heavy, yet milk is cooling and fish is heating. Lion meat and pig meat are both sweet in taste, yet lion meat has a pungent Vipaka while pig meat has a sweet one. These examples show that general rules have exceptions, and that true knowledge of a substance means knowing its actual behaviour, not just its category."},{"n":10,"title":"The Six Tastes and Their Effects","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis chapter, called Rasabhediya (rasa means taste, bheda means division), works through the six tastes one by one. It explains where each taste comes from, how you recognise it in the mouth, what it does inside the body, which of the three doshas it calms or stirs up, and the harm it causes when you overdo it. It closes with lists of common substances that carry each taste, some general rules and exceptions, and the way tastes behave in terms of heating, cooling, heaviness and dryness.\n\nWhere tastes come from\n\nAyurveda sees everything as built from five great elements: earth (prithvi), water (ap/jala), fire (tejas/agni), air (vayu) and ether/space (akasha). Each taste is produced when two of these elements combine. Sweet comes from earth and water. Sour comes from fire and water. Salt comes from water and fire. Bitter comes from ether and air. Pungent comes from fire and air. Astringent comes from earth and air.\n\nHow to recognise each taste\n\nSweet tends to stick and spread in the mouth and leaves the body feeling satisfied and the senses soothed; the text playfully notes even ants are drawn to it. Sour makes the mouth water, brings on gooseflesh, sets the teeth on edge and makes the eyes and brows screw up. Salt also increases watering of the mouth and brings a burning feeling in the cheeks and throat. Bitter cleans the mouth and dulls the tongue so other tastes are hard to notice. Pungent bites at the tip of the tongue, irritates it, draws watering from the eyes, nose and mouth and burns the cheeks. Astringent numbs the tongue, blunts taste and leaves a feeling of the throat being blocked or tightened.\n\nWhat each taste does and its doshas\n\nSweet (earth + water): familiar from birth, deeply nourishing, builds all the body tissues and strength, suits children, the elderly, the injured and the weak, improves skin, hair, the senses and ojas (vital essence and immunity), firms up the body, soothes the throat, increases breast milk and helps knit broken bones; it is oily, slow to digest and life-promoting, and it settles both Pitta and Vata and counters poison. Overused it breeds Kapha and fat disorders: obesity, breathing trouble, drowsiness, diabetes, swollen neck glands and growths.\n\nSour (fire + water): kindles digestion, pleases the heart, sharpens appetite, is oily, hot in potency yet cooling to the touch, moistening, light and easy to digest, and it moves stuck Vata downward; it raises Kapha, Pitta and the blood. In excess it slackens the body, saps strength and causes dimness of sight, giddiness, itching, pallor, herpes-type spreading skin disease, swellings, pox and fevers.\n\nSalt (water + fire): loosens stiffness, clears blocked channels and pores, boosts digestion, lubricates, promotes sweating, penetrates deep, improves taste and helps open abscesses. In excess it spoils the blood and stirs Vata, causing baldness, greying, wrinkles, thirst, skin disease, poison-like effects, spreading skin disease and loss of strength.\n\nBitter (ether + air): not pleasant on its own, but it corrects loss of appetite, worms, thirst, poison, skin disease, fainting, fever, nausea and burning; it calms Pitta and Kapha, dries up excess moisture, fat, marrow, stool and urine, is light and easy to digest, cool, drying, sharpens the mind and cleanses breast milk and throat. Overused it thins the tissues and brings on Vata disorders.\n\nPungent (fire + air): clears throat disease, rashes, skin disease, a type of indigestion (alasaka) and swelling, reduces ulcer swelling, dries up oiliness, fat and moisture, increases hunger, aids digestion, improves taste, cleanses and expels doshas, breaks up hard masses, opens the channels and reduces Kapha. In excess it causes thirst, loss of semen and strength, fainting, cramps, tremors and pain in the waist and back.\n\nAstringent (earth + air): settles Pitta and Kapha, is slow to digest, cleanses the blood, presses and heals wounds, is cold in potency, dries moisture and fat and slows the digestion of undigested food; it absorbs water, which can lead to constipation and dryness, and over-cleanses the skin. In excess it keeps food stuck undigested, causes bloating, chest-region pain, thirst, wasting, loss of virility, blocked channels and constipation.\n\nGroups of substances by taste\n\nThe chapter lists example substances for each taste. Sweet group: ghee, gold, molasses, many fruits and roots, the three Bala, the two Meda, the four Parni, licorice, Vidari, milk, sugarcane, grapes, honey and so on. Sour group: amla, tamarind, matulunga, amlavetasa, pomegranate, buttermilk, curds, mango, kapittha and others. Salt group: rock salt, sauvarchala, black salt, bida, sea salt, audbhida, romaka and alkalis. Bitter group: patola, trayanti, usira, sandalwood, bhunimba, neem, katuka, guduchi, the Mahat Panchamula, the two Vyaghri and more. Pungent group: asafoetida, black pepper, vidanga, the Panchakola, ginger and pungent leafy vegetables. Astringent group: haritaki, bibhitaki, shirisha, khadira, honey, kadamba, udumbara, pearl, coral, dates, lotus stalk and lotus flowers.\n\nGeneral rules and their exceptions\n\nSweet substances usually raise Kapha, except old shali rice, barley, green gram, wheat, honey, sugar and desert-animal meat. Sour usually aggravates Pitta, except pomegranate and amla. Salt is usually bad for the eyes, except rock salt. Bitter and pungent are usually non-aphrodisiac and raise Vata, except guduchi, patola, ginger, long pepper and garlic. Astringent is usually cold and obstructive, except haritaki.\n\nPotency, dryness and heaviness\n\nAmong potencies, pungent, sour and salt are heating (each more than the last in that order), while bitter, astringent and sweet are cooling. For dryness, bitter, pungent and astringent are drying and constipating; salt, sour and sweet are oily and help pass stool, urine and gas. For weight, salt, astringent and sweet are heavy to digest, while sour, pungent and bitter are light and easy to digest.\n\nCombinations of tastes\n\nThe six tastes can be used singly or mixed, giving sixty-three practical combinations: six single tastes, fifteen pairs, twenty triples, fifteen groups of four, six groups of five, and one group of all six. Beyond even this, tastes vary in strength (more, moderate, less) and in primary versus secondary roles, so the real number of taste-blends is effectively endless, and they should be chosen according to the state of the doshas and the medicine or therapy being planned."},{"n":11,"title":"Knowing the Doshas, Tissues and Wastes","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis chapter, Doshadi Vijnaniya (knowledge of the doshas and the rest), lays the foundation of Ayurveda: the three doshas (Vata, Pitta, Kapha), the seven body tissues (dhatus) and the three main wastes (malas). It explains what each does when healthy, what happens when each is increased or decreased, how to read those changes, how the doshas relate to the tissues, what causes increase and decrease, the role of the digestive fire in the tissues, how disease spreads, and finally ojas, the vital essence that underlies health.\n\nThe body's three pillars\n\nThe body rests on three groups. The doshas are Vata, Pitta and Kapha. The dhatus are the seven tissues: rasa (plasma or the fluid product of digestion), rakta (blood), mamsa (muscle), meda (fat), asthi (bone), majja (marrow) and shukra (reproductive fluid, male and female). The malas are the wastes: sweat, urine and faeces. Together these are described as the roots of the body.\n\nWhat the healthy doshas do\n\nVata, when normal, governs all movement, from blood flowing in vessels and air moving in the lungs to the movement of the limbs. It gives enthusiasm, drives breathing, controls all locomotion and the activity of the mind and speech, starts off the natural urges such as tears, stool, urine, sneezing, coughing, vomiting and yawning, keeps the tissues in good order and keeps the senses working. Pitta, when normal, runs digestion and metabolism, keeps body temperature steady, powers vision, generates hunger, thirst and appetite, and maintains skin colour, intelligence, courage and the body's suppleness. Kapha, when normal, gives stability, lubrication and firmness to the joints, and supports mental steadiness, the ability to hold back emotions and strain, and the quality of patience or forgiveness.\n\nWhat the healthy tissues and wastes do\n\nRasa nourishes. Rakta sustains life activity. Mamsa covers and supports the bones and enables movement. Meda lubricates. Asthi gives the body its frame and support. Majja fills the inside of the bones. Shukra enables conception and pregnancy. Among the wastes, faeces help maintain the body's strength and bulk, urine carries off liquid waste, and sweat slowly carries off moisture.\n\nWhen the doshas increase\n\nIncreased Vata brings wasting, dark discolouration, craving for warmth, tremors, bloating, constipation, loss of strength, loss of sleep, failing senses, rambling speech, dizziness and timidity. Increased Pitta brings yellowing of stool, urine, eyes and skin, excessive hunger and thirst, burning sensations and very little sleep. Increased Kapha brings weak digestion, excess salivation, laziness, heaviness, pale colour, coldness, looseness of the limbs, breathing trouble, cough and excessive sleep.\n\nWhen the tissues increase\n\nExcess rasa mimics excess Kapha. Excess rakta brings spreading skin disease, spleen disorders, abscesses, skin disease, gout, bleeding disorders, abdominal tumours, a tooth disease, jaundice, facial patches, loss of digestion, unconsciousness and redness of skin, eyes and urine. Excess mamsa enlarges the cheeks, thighs and abdomen and overgrows the neck muscles, with lumps and glandular swelling. Excess meda causes similar signs plus fatigue, breathlessness on slight effort and drooping of the buttocks, breasts and abdomen. Excess asthi overgrows bone and makes extra teeth. Excess majja brings heaviness of eyes and body, enlarged joints and stubborn ulcers. Excess shukra brings strong sexual desire and seminal stones.\n\nWhen the wastes increase\n\nExcess faeces causes a distended, gurgling, heavy abdomen. Excess urine causes severe bladder pain and a feeling of not having emptied. Excess sweat causes heavy perspiration, foul smell and itching. Other minor wastes, such as eye discharge, are judged increased by their quantity and the heaviness of their sites.\n\nWhen the doshas decrease\n\nDecreased Vata gives bodily weakness, very little speech, dulled awareness and loss of consciousness, along with signs resembling raised Kapha. Decreased Pitta gives weak digestion, coldness and loss of lustre. Decreased Kapha gives dizziness, a feeling of emptiness in Kapha's sites, heart palpitation and loose joints.\n\nWhen the tissues decrease\n\nLow rasa brings dryness, fatigue, wasting, exhaustion even without work, and intolerance of noise. Low rakta brings craving for sour and cold things, slack veins and dryness. Low mamsa brings weak senses, wasted cheeks and buttocks and joint pain. Low meda brings loss of feeling in the waist, enlarged spleen and a wasted body. Low asthi brings joint pain and loss of teeth, hair and nails. Low majja brings hollow bones, giddiness and seeing darkness. Low shukra brings delayed or bloodstained ejaculation, severe pain in the testicles and a feeling of hot fumes from the urethra.\n\nReading increase and decrease\n\nAs a rule, increase shows up as more of a thing's own qualities, and decrease as more of the opposite qualities. Wastes are judged increased when they fail to clear and accumulate, and decreased when too little is passed. Because the body is used to a certain load of waste in the gut and bladder, a shortfall in waste is actually more troubling than an excess.\n\nHow doshas and tissues are linked\n\nVata resides in the bones (asthi), Pitta in sweat and blood, and Kapha in the remaining tissues and wastes. For Pitta and Kapha, when the dosha rises its related tissue and waste rise too, and the same holds for a fall; for example when Pitta rises, sweat and blood vitiation rise with it. Vata is the exception and works inversely: when Vata falls, bone increases, and when Vata rises, bone wastes.\n\nWhat drives increase and decrease, and how to treat\n\nIncrease of doshas, tissues and wastes usually comes from over-nutrition, which later shows as raised Kapha. Decrease comes from under-nutrition, which later shows as raised Vata. So conditions from excess are treated by Langhana, lightening and reducing therapy, and conditions from deficiency by Brimhana, nourishing and building therapy. Vata again is the reverse: raised Vata is treated with nourishing therapy and low Vata with lightening therapy. The chapter also lists specific measures: excess blood by bloodletting and purgation; excess muscle by surgery, alkalis and cautery; excess fat by anti-obesity measures and low fat by anti-wasting measures; low bone by medicated enema of milk, ghee and bitters; low faeces corrected by heavy foods like ram or goat organ meat, half-cooked pulses, barley and the two kinds of masha; urine disorders by diabetes and dysuria treatments; low sweat by exercise, oil baths, sweating therapy and wine.\n\nThe tissue fire\n\nThe body's digestive fire (kayagni) has portions of itself seated in each tissue. When these tissue-fires weaken in quality, quantity or function, the tissues go out of balance, and a change in an earlier tissue passes on the same change to the next tissue in sequence.\n\nHow disease travels\n\nVitiated doshas first spoil rasa and the other tissues; then doshas and tissues together spoil the wastes, which in turn spoil the channels that carry them out. These elimination routes are the anus and urethra below, seven openings in the head, and the sweat channels; diseases arise from these spoiled channels.\n\nOjas, the essence of life\n\nOjas is the finest essence of all the tissues, the refined end-product of shukra. It sits mainly in the heart, pervades the whole body and underpins health. It is oily, watery, clear and slightly reddish-yellow. Where ojas goes, life goes; its loss is loss of life, and all aspects of health depend on it. Ojas falls through anger, hunger, worry, grief and overexertion, and classical texts add overexercise, long fasting, eating too little, drying drinks, poor food mixtures, fear, staying awake at night, injury, infection, tissue loss such as from bleeding, excess loss of Kapha, blood, semen or waste, and poison. When ojas falls, the person becomes fearful, weak, endlessly anxious without cause, uneasy in the senses, poor in complexion, negative in mind and dry; it is managed with enlivening (jivaniya) herbs, milk and meat juice. When ojas increases, there is contentment, good nourishment and strength.\n\nManaging the doshas through food and awareness\n\nRaised doshas are brought down by avoiding the foods one dislikes, and lowered doshas are built up by the foods one craves. A raised dosha tends to make a person crave dissimilar foods, and a lowered dosha similar foods, though the unwise do not notice this. Doshas, when raised, produce their signs in proportion to their strength; when lowered they produce no signs; and when normal they simply do their jobs. The same doshas that, when balanced, let the body grow and thrive, become the cause of its ruin when disturbed, so the body should be guarded from both their increase and their decrease."},{"n":12,"title":"The Sub-types of the Doshas and How Imbalance Spreads","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis chapter, Doshabhediya (division of the doshas), maps where each dosha lives in the body, breaks each of the three doshas into five sub-types with their seats and jobs, explains the qualities that make a dosha accumulate, overflow or settle, describes the three stages of imbalance and how they follow the seasons, and then turns to how disease arises: the pathways it travels, the three kinds of faulty contact that provoke doshas, the signs of each raised dosha, how a physician should examine a patient, and the ways diseases are classified and counted.\n\nWhere the doshas live\n\nVata mainly occupies the large intestine, waist, hips, ears, bones and skin, with the large intestine as its special seat. Pitta occupies the navel region, stomach and small intestine, sweat, lymph, blood, plasma, eyes and skin, with the area around the navel as its special seat. Kapha occupies the chest, throat, head, pancreas region, bone joints, stomach and small intestine, plasma, fat, nose and tongue, with the chest as its special seat.\n\nThe five types of Vata\n\nPrana sits in the head and moves in the chest and throat; it governs the will, the heart, the senses, the intellect and vision, and drives expectoration, sneezing, belching, breathing in and swallowing food. Udana is seated in the chest and moves in the nose, navel and throat; it starts speech, effort, enthusiasm, strength, colour and memory. Vyana is seated in the heart and races throughout the whole body; it runs flexion and extension, the opening and closing of the eyelids, and essentially all bodily movement. Samana sits beside the digestive fire and moves through the digestive tract; it draws in food, aids digestion, separates the useful part from the waste and moves each along its proper path. Apana sits in the large intestine and moves in the waist, bladder and genitals; it handles ejaculation, menstruation, defecation, urination and childbirth.\n\nThe five types of Pitta\n\nPachaka sits between the stomach and large intestine; made of all five elements but dominant in fire and lacking water, it is called the fire (anala) because it digests and transforms food, splitting it into essence and waste, and it supports and empowers the other four Pittas, so it is the chief. Ranjaka sits in the stomach and colours the useful part of digestion into blood, turning rasa into rakta. Sadhaka sits in the heart and runs mental functions like knowledge, intelligence and self-awareness, helping a person pursue the aims of life. Alochaka sits in the eyes and enables vision. Bhrajaka sits in the skin and brings out its colour and complexion.\n\nThe five types of Kapha\n\nAvalambaka sits in the chest and the junction of shoulders, neck and back; by its own strength and the essence of food it lubricates and nourishes, and it supports the other four Kaphas. Kledaka sits in the stomach and moistens the hard food mass to help digestion. Bodhaka sits in the tongue and enables the perception of taste. Tarpaka sits in the head and nourishes the sense organs. Shleshaka sits in the bone joints and lubricates and strengthens them. Although the doshas are present everywhere, each is most dominant in these particular sites.\n\nThree levels of imbalance: Chaya, Kopa, Shama\n\nChaya is the first stage, a mild build-up of a dosha in its own home; the body defends itself by creating a dislike for whatever would increase that dosha further. Kopa (also called prakopa) is the next stage, where the raised dosha overflows from its seat into other channels, premonitory symptoms appear and disease can begin. Shama (or prashama) is the return to normal, where the raised dosha subsides and health is restored.\n\nWhich qualities move each dosha\n\nVata: hot with dryness causes its mild build-up (chaya), cold with dryness causes its overflow (kopa), and oily and heavy with warmth brings it back to balance (shama). Pitta: cold with sharpness causes chaya, hot with sharpness causes kopa, and cold with mildness brings shama. Kapha: cold with oiliness causes chaya, hot with oiliness causes kopa, and hot with dryness brings shama.\n\nHow the seasons drive the doshas\n\nEach dosha has a season for building up, overflowing and settling. Vata accumulates in summer, overflows in the rains and settles in autumn. Pitta accumulates in the rains, overflows in autumn and settles in early winter. Kapha accumulates in winter, overflows in spring and settles in summer. The text explains that Vata builds in summer because of light, dry food and bodies, but the summer heat, being opposite to cold Vata, stops it overflowing fully. Pitta builds in the rains because water and food turn sour at the end of digestion, but the coldness of the rains checks its full overflow. Kapha builds in winter from cold, oily food, but the severe cold solidifies it so it does not overflow then. So both daily food and activity and the season influence whether a dosha rises or falls.\n\nHow imbalance spreads\n\nLike floodwater rushing fast into a city but draining away slowly, a raised dosha spreads quickly through the body from foot to head to cause disease, while restoring balance takes much longer. The doshas are the single root cause of all disease; even the mental qualities of sattva, rajas and tamas are influenced by them. When doshas are vitiated they spoil the tissues and wastes and bring on disease.\n\nThree kinds of faulty contact\n\nDoshas are provoked by three faulty forms of contact, each applying to the senses (artha), the season (kala) and action (karma). Hinayoga is too little contact, atiyoga is too much, and mithyayoga is wrong or unsuitable contact. For the senses: too little is barely hearing or living in near-darkness, too much is staring at tiny or dazzling things or loud sound for too long, and wrong is seeing frightening, too-close, too-far, disliked or abnormal objects. For the seasons: too little is a weak winter, summer or monsoon, too much is an extreme one, and wrong is weather opposite to the season, like rain or cold in summer. For action, which is of mind, body and speech: too little is hardly speaking, moving or thinking, too much is excessive speech, activity and thinking, and wrong includes suppressing urges, bad posture, sudden jumps and falls, speaking right after meals and harbouring desire, hatred or fear. These provoke the doshas, which then produce many diseases in the mind, tissues, viscera, bones and joints.\n\nThe three disease pathways\n\nThe body is divided into three routes of disease. The external pathway (bahya rogamarga) is the blood and other tissues and the skin, linked to moles, facial patches, goitre, facial ulcers, malignant growths, piles, abdominal tumours and swellings. The internal pathway (anta rogamarga) is the stomach, small and large intestine and the whole gut, the great channel (mahasrota), linked to vomiting, diarrhoea, cough, breathlessness, abdominal enlargement, fever, dropsy, piles, tumours, herpes and abscess. The middle pathway (madhyama rogamarga) is the vital organs such as head, heart and bladder, the joints, blood vessels, tendons, ligaments and nerves, linked to consumption, hemiplegia, facial paralysis, head diseases, pain and stiffness of the joints, bones and waist.\n\nThe signs of each raised dosha\n\nRaised Vata produces drooping, dilation, cutting pain, loss of sensation, weakness, pain, continuous pain, splitting pain, constriction, body-ache, shrinking, twisting, tingling, thirst, tremor, roughness, a feeling of emptiness, dryness, pulsation, a tied-up rigidity, stiffness, an astringent taste in the mouth and a bluish or crimson discolouration. Raised Pitta produces burning, redness, heat and pus or ulcers, sweating, wet inflammation, oozing, putrefaction, debility, fainting, toxicity, a bitter and sour taste in the mouth and colours other than yellowish-white and crimson. Raised Kapha produces oiliness, hardness, itching, coldness, heaviness, obstruction, a coating as if wrapped in wet cloth, stiffness, swelling, indigestion, excess sleep, white discolouration, a sweet and salty taste in the mouth and a slowing of all activity.\n\nExamining the patient\n\nThe physician recognises these signs by careful inspection and must observe the patient closely, because skill in treatment, like skill in judging gems, comes only from constant practice and study. Before deciding on the aggravated dosha and its treatment, the physician should weigh ten factors: the tissues and wastes involved, the site and the patient's locale, the patient's strength, the season and age of both patient and disease, the digestive power, the body constitution, the age, the mind and tolerance, what the patient is accustomed to, the food habits and the stage of disease. A physician who judges all this never errs; one who mistakes a serious disease for a mild one under-treats and worsens it, while over-treating a mild disease with aggressive measures also harms the patient. The physician should not be ashamed of not knowing a disease's name, since not every disease has one.\n\nKinds of disease by cause\n\nDiseases come in three kinds by cause. Karmaja arises from past or present wrong deeds, its cause often unclear, and it clears only when the effect of those deeds ends. Doshaja arises from specific causes that raise the doshas and then manifest disease, and it clears by using food, activity and medicine opposite to the cause. Doshakarmaja combines wrong deeds with disease-causing habits, often with strong symptoms, and clears only when both the dosha is calmed and the effect of past deeds is undone. Diseases are also grouped in two by cause: primary (svatantra) diseases with their own specific causes and clear features, and secondary (paratantra) diseases with no cause of their own, which are of two sorts, premonitory symptoms seen early and complications that follow later. A secondary disease usually settles when the primary one is treated, but a strong complication should be treated promptly because it troubles an already weakened body. The same vitiated dosha can travel to many parts and cause many diseases, so the physician must quickly judge the disease, its site and its particular cause.\n\nCounting the dosha combinations\n\nCombining the increase and decrease of the doshas gives sixty-two states. For increase: three single doshas, three pairs in equal measure plus six with one dosha dominant (nine for pairs), and thirteen for all three together (six with one dominant, one with all equal, six with uneven subdivisions), giving twenty-five for increase; decrease mirrors this with another twenty-five. Added together with the mixed increase-and-decrease states, the total reaches sixty-two. The sixty-third state is the one in which all the doshas are in equilibrium, and that balanced state is what is called health. With the involvement of the tissues and the varying degrees of rise and fall, the real permutations are countless, and the physician must read them through signs and symptoms with an attentive mind."},{"n":13,"title":"Treating the Doshas and Ama","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis chapter (Doshopakramaniya) explains how to treat an imbalance of each dosha (Vata, Pitta, Kapha), how the doshas combine with Ama (the sticky residue of poor digestion) to cause illness, the correct order and route for clearing them, and the right time of day and season to give medicines.\n\nCalming aggravated Vata\n\nWhen Vata is raised, the whole approach is warm, oily, grounding and comforting, because Vata is dry, cold, light and mobile. Helpful measures: oleation (sneha) taken by mouth as oil, ghee or fat and applied outside as massage; sudation or sweating therapy (sveda); gentle purification only (mild Vamana/emesis or Virechana/purgation), since aggressive cleansing itself raises Vata; foods that are sweet, sour and salty in taste and warm; oil massage (abhyanga) and simple rubbing (mardana); wrapping or covering the body or the affected part in cloth; pouring warm herbal liquids or oils over the area (seka); wine made from corn flour and jaggery; enema (basti) with warm, oily, warming-potency substances; medicated fats prepared with herbs that kindle appetite and improve digestion; and oleation enema (anuvasana) made from fatty meat juice and oil. Keeping the person calm, cared for and at ease matters too.\n\nCalming aggravated Pitta\n\nPitta is hot, sharp and sour, so it is cooled, soothed and gently cleared. Helpful measures: drinking ghee (plain or medicated, on a physician's advice); purgation (Virechana) using sweet-tasting, cooling-potency herbs; foods and medicines that are sweet, bitter and astringent; inhaling the cool, pleasant fragrance of soothing herbs; anointing the body often with camphor, sandalwood and vetiver paste; and a cooling, pleasant lifestyle: moonlit evenings, gentle music, soft cool breeze, time near clean water, ponds, gardens and fountains, and the company of agreeable, undemanding people. Milk, ghee and purgation are specifically useful.\n\nCalming aggravated Kapha\n\nKapha is heavy, cold, oily and stable, so it is treated with drying, lightening, heating and stimulating measures. Helpful measures: strong, properly conducted emesis and purgation (to be practitioner-guided); dry foods eaten in small amounts; foods that are sharp, hot, pungent, bitter and astringent; old wine; sexual activity; staying awake at night; varied exercise; dry massage (udvartana); soups of grains; honey; fat-reducing therapies and medicines; inhaling medicinal smoke; fasting; and gargling. A degree of hardship and effort suits Kapha.\n\nCombined and simultaneous dosha problems\n\nWhen two or three doshas are raised together, the individual treatments are combined sensibly. As a rough guide: Vata-plus-Pitta is managed like the summer regimen; Kapha-plus-Vata like the spring regimen (because Vata is 'yogavahi', a carrier that amplifies whichever dosha it joins); Kapha-plus-Pitta like the autumn regimen. Treat a dosha while it is only mildly accumulating (chaya) if you can. Once truly aggravated (kopa), reduce it without disturbing the others. If all three flare at once, control the strongest or most aggravated one first, still without aggravating the rest.\n\nWhat counts as good treatment\n\nA treatment that cures one problem but seeds another is not 'pure' or sound. A sound treatment relieves the illness without creating a new one.\n\nHow doshas move between gut and tissues\n\nDoshas can move outward from the gut (koshta) into the tissues, bones and vital points (shakha/marma) under the push of exercise, excess body heat, unsuitable habits, and the quick movement of Vata. They move the other way, back into the gut, when the fine channels are cleansed and opened, when the doshas increase further, when they liquefy or 'ripen', and when Vata is steadied. On moving, doshas settle somewhere and wait for a trigger; once strengthened by season or timing they flare again and spread.\n\nNative versus foreign doshas; priorities\n\nPriority in treatment goes to a dosha that has invaded another dosha's seat and is weak there, and also to a dosha strong enough to overpower the others. A 'foreign' or externally caused problem (agantu) may be treated before or after the native one, the order depending on which is more aggravated and stronger.\n\nDoshas lodged in the tissues (tiryak gata)\n\nDoshas stuck in the tissues rather than the gut trouble the patient for a long time and must not be rushed. First judge the person's strength and digestive power. Then either settle them with palliative measures, or draw them back into the gut and expel them by the nearest route (upward by emesis or downward by purgation).\n\nSigns of doshas mixed with Ama (Sama)\n\nWhen aggravated doshas combine with Ama (undigested residue), the state is 'Sama'. Signs: blocked channels, loss of strength, heaviness, sluggish Vata, laziness, weak digestion, frequent spitting or expectoration, constipation or scanty urine with waste build-up, loss of appetite, and exhaustion. The opposite, clear signs indicate 'Nirama' doshas, free of Ama.\n\nWhere Ama comes from\n\nAfter digestion the nourishing part separates from waste and becomes Rasa dhatu (the nutrient fluid). When digestive power is weak, Rasa is not formed properly, stays raw in the stomach and intestines, gets spoiled, and becomes Ama. Some teachers add that Ama can also form when greatly increased doshas intermix. Dhatus (tissues) and malas (wastes) tainted by Ama are called Sama, and the diseases arising from Sama doshas are Sama diseases.\n\nTreating Sama doshas\n\nSama doshas spread through the body and lurking in the tissues must not be force-expelled by cleansing therapies; doing so is like squeezing juice from an unripe fruit, which ruins the fruit, meaning the tissues themselves are harmed. The correct order is: first digestive and appetite-kindling herbs; then oleation and sudation (snehana, svedana); and only then, when the doshas are 'ripe', clearing them by Shodhana (emesis or purgation) at the right time and to the person's strength. Medicine by mouth clears the stomach and small intestine; medicine by nose clears the parts above the shoulders; medicine by rectum clears the large intestine. If greatly increased Sama doshas are leaving the body on their own by vomiting or purging, they should not be blocked with medicine, since stopping them causes disease. In the early stage let such natural elimination run and give the person light food; in the next stage either 'cook' the doshas with digestives or clear them by Panchakarma.\n\nSeason and timing for clearing doshas\n\nDoshas that quietly accumulate in one season are best cleared in the following transition: Vata (accumulated in summer) cleared in the month of Shravana (around August) by enema; Pitta (accumulated in the rains) cleared in Kartika by purgation; Kapha (accumulated in winter) cleared in Chaitra (around April) by emesis. The milder transitional periods between the harsh extremes of heat, rain and cold are the right windows to clear the spoiled doshas. First protect against the harsh effects of each season, then give the needed therapy, and do not let the stages of disease (kriyakala) advance unchecked.\n\nTime of day for taking medicine\n\nThere are ten timings for medicine, each suited to a condition: on an empty stomach (for strong Kapha disorders and strong patients); just before or at the start of food (Apana Vata disorders); in the middle of the meal (Samana Vata); at the end of the meal (Vyana Vata after the morning meal, Udana Vata after the evening meal); between morsels (Prana Vata); with each morsel and mixed into food (anorexia and loss of taste, given with tasty food); repeatedly through the day (poisoning, vomiting, hiccup, thirst, breathlessness, cough); before and after food (tremors, convulsions, hiccup); and at night or bedtime (head and neck disorders)."},{"n":14,"title":"The Two Kinds of Treatment","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis chapter (Dvividhopakramaniya) teaches that every Ayurvedic treatment falls, at its root, into just two kinds: nourishing and reducing. It explains what each means, their sub-methods, who needs each, how to recognise correct, excessive and insufficient treatment, and what to do when treatment has gone too far in either direction.\n\nThe two treatments\n\nBecause the body to be treated is itself of two broad types, heavy or lean, treatment is of two kinds. One is Santarpana, nourishing or enriching, also called Brimhana (stoutening, adding weight and substance). The other is Apatarpana, depleting or cleansing, also called Langhana (lightening, reducing, slimming). Nourishing works mainly through the earth and water elements, which add bulk and moisture. Reducing works mainly through fire, air and ether, which lighten and dry. Even the familiar paired actions fit this scheme: oiling (snehana) and withholding/stabilising (sthambhana) are nourishing in nature, while drying (rukshana) and sweating (swedana) are reducing. Nothing in therapy truly falls outside these two.\n\nThe two kinds of reducing (Langhana)\n\nReducing therapy, which makes the body light and thin, is itself of two kinds. Shodhana is purification, which forcibly expels aggravated doshas from the body; it has five forms: decoction enema (Niruha), emesis (Vamana), purgation of the body (Virechana), purgation of the head through the nose (Nasya/Shiro-virechana), and bloodletting (Raktamokshana). Shamana is palliative, which does not expel the doshas and does not raise the normal dosha, but simply brings an abnormal dosha back to normal. Shamana has seven forms: carminative/digestive herbs (Pachana), appetite-kindling measures (Deepana), bearing hunger or fasting (Kshut), bearing thirst and reducing fluids (Trut), physical exercise (Vyayama), exposure to sunlight (Aatapa), and exposure to breeze or wind (Maruta). Nourishing (Brimhana) also counts as a kind of Shamana, because it settles Vata and Vata-Pitta.\n\nWho needs nourishing therapy (Brimhaneeya)\n\nNourishing is for those who are depleted: emaciated by illness, by harsh medicines or therapies, by alcohol, by too much sex, or by grief; those worn by carrying heavy loads or long travel; those with chest injury or weakened by wounds; the excessively dry; the weak; people of Vata constitution; pregnant women; women who have just given birth; children; and the elderly. In summer, even people not otherwise in this list benefit from nourishing. The methods are rich and grounding: meat, milk, sugar, ghee and fats, nourishing enemas made with sweet substances and oils, good sleep, a comfortable bed, oil massage, bathing, rest, comfort and a happy, untroubled mind.\n\nWho needs reducing therapy (Langhaneeya)\n\nReducing is for those with excess or heaviness: diabetes and urinary disorders (Meha), Ama conditions, people over-oiled by excess oleation, fever, stiff thighs, skin diseases, herpes/erysipelas, abscess, spleen disorders, and disorders of the head, throat and eyes; and the obese. In winter, even others benefit from some reducing.\n\nMatching the method of reducing to the patient\n\nThe strength of the reducing method must match the strength of the patient and the disease. For the very obese and strong, with excess Pitta and Kapha, and for those with Ama conditions, fever, vomiting, diarrhoea, heart disease, constipation, heaviness, excess belching or nausea, purification (Panchakarma) is the right means of reducing. For the moderately obese and of medium strength with those same complaints, first give digestive and appetite-kindling measures, and only later the purification therapies. For those merely troubled by raised doshas, of medium strength and medium disease and able to bear strain, palliative Shamana reducing is enough: control of hunger and thirst, and for the weaker, exposure to breeze, sun and exercise.\n\nNot mixing the two carelessly\n\nSomeone who needs reducing must not be given nourishing. Someone who needs nourishing may be given only mild reducing, or sometimes both nourishing and reducing together, depending on their habitat, the season and their strength.\n\nBenefits of each\n\nNourishing done rightly gives strength, good body nourishment, and cure of the diseases it is suited to. Reducing done rightly gives keen, clear senses, expulsion of wastes, a feeling of lightness, good taste, natural hunger and thirst returning together, a clear and comfortable chest, clean belching and clear throat, easing of disease, more enthusiasm, and loss of dullness and heaviness.\n\nWhen treatment is overdone\n\nEither therapy pushed beyond what is needed causes harm, so dose and measure matter. Excess nourishing (Ati-Brimhana) can produce profound obesity, scrofula, diabetes and urinary disease, fever, abdominal enlargement, fistula-in-ano, cough, loss of consciousness, painful urination, Ama disorders and serious skin diseases. Treatment for over-nourishing uses fat-, Vata- and Kapha-reducing measures: horse gram, barley, green gram, millets and honey-water; purposeful activity and even some stress; purification and reduced sleep; daily licks of Triphala, Guduchi, chebulic myrobalan or nutgrass with honey; and specific classical formulations combining herbs such as Trikatu, Triphala, drumstick, false black pepper, ginger, turmeric and others with honey, ghee, oil and corn flour, which reduce excess fat while also helping heart disease, jaundice, skin discoloration, breathlessness, cough and hoarseness, and which sharpen thinking, intelligence and memory and rekindle weak digestion. These formulations should be prepared and used under a qualified practitioner.\n\nExcess reducing (Ati-Langhana) can produce profound emaciation, dizziness, cough, severe thirst, loss of appetite, and loss of moistness, digestive power, sleep, vision, hearing, semen, Ojas (vital essence), hunger and voice; pain in the bladder, heart, head, calves, thighs, shoulders and flanks; fever, delirium, belching, exhaustion, vomiting, cutting joint and bone pain, and blocked elimination of stool and urine. Over-reducing is corrected by nourishing: sweet, unctuous, fatty foods, comfortable living, more nourishing food and more sleep, freedom from worry and a happy mind, which fatten a lean person steadily. Meat, especially of carnivorous animals, is held to be the single best body-builder.\n\nThe hard case of obesity, and food choices\n\nThe text notes that emaciation is easier to treat than obesity, because deep obesity is stubborn, neither nourishing nor reducing easily overcoming the excess fat, weak digestion and disturbed Vata together. For the obese, heavy but non-nourishing foods are ideal (barley is the example); for the lean, light and nourishing foods (wheat is the example). Finally, although the states of the doshas and the kinds of treatment are countless, all of them still come down to these two, nourishing and reducing, just as all diseases come down to two, those with Ama and those without."},{"n":15,"title":"Groups of Cleansing Herbs (Shodhanadi Gana)","detail":"What this chapter is about\n\nThis chapter, called Shodhanadi Gana Sangraha, is a classical pharmacy catalogue. Instead of describing single herbs, Vagbhata arranges many plants and minerals into named groups (gana), each group chosen because its members share a common action. The idea is practical: a physician preparing a purification therapy or a formula does not hunt for one rare herb, but reaches for a whole family of substances with the same effect, and swaps one for another as availability allows. About thirty-three such groups are listed here. These are the raw building blocks that the next chapters (oleation, sweating, emesis, purgation and enema) draw upon.\n\nImportant: everything in this chapter is reference material for a trained Ayurvedic physician who formulates and dispenses medicines. Many of the plants named are powerful, and some are frankly toxic in the wrong hands. None of this is a shopping list for home use. Identifying, processing and combining these substances safely is specialist work.\n\nGroups tied to the five cleansing procedures (Panchakarma)\n\nThe chapter opens with the drug families used for the five main cleansing actions. The Chardana (emetic) group holds plants used to provoke therapeutic vomiting, headed by Madana fruit (Randia spinosa) alongside liquorice, neem, Kutaja and others. The Virechana (purgative) group gathers downward-cleansing agents such as Trivrit, Triphala, Snuhi latex and even milk. The Niruhana group lists the ingredients of decoction enemas. The Naavana group names substances that cleanse the head through the nose. Each of these simply tells the physician which shelf to open when a given route of cleansing is planned.\n\nGroups that calm each dosha\n\nNext come families defined by which dosha they settle. The Vataghna group (Bhadradaru, Nata, Dashamula, the two Bala and related herbs) eases Vata. The Pittaghna group (Durva, Ananta, neem, Vasa and the Sarivadi and Nyagrodhadi families) cools and settles Pitta. The Kaphaghna group (the Aragvadhadi, Arkadi, Surasadi and related families) reduces Kapha. A physician matching a formula to a patient's imbalance pulls from the appropriate family.\n\nNourishing and restorative groups\n\nNot all groups cleanse; several nourish. The Jeevaniya group (Jeevanti, the Kakoli pair, the Meda pair, Mudgaparni, Masaparni and liquorice) is a classic restorative set used to rebuild strength and vitality. The Vidaryadi group is described as good for the heart, stoutening, and helpful in wasting, abdominal masses, body ache, breathlessness and cough. The Padmakadi group promotes breast milk and is nourishing and restorative.\n\nGroups aimed at specific complaints\n\nMany groups are organised around a symptom picture. The Sarivadi group relieves burning sensation, bleeding tendencies, thirst and fever. The Parushakadi group is used for thirst and urinary complaints. The Anjanadi group addresses effects of poison and internal burning. The Patoladi, Guduchyadi and Aragvadhadi groups are used for skin disease, fever, vomiting, itching and cleaning of chronic wounds. The Asanadi and Arkadi groups are used for skin and fat disorders. The Varunadi and Virataradi and Ushakadi groups are used for abdominal masses, urinary stones and painful urination. The Rodhradi, Mustadi, Surasadi, Vatsakadi, Nyagrodhadi, Eladi, Priyangu-Ambasthadi, Shyamadi and others each carry their own indications, ranging from diarrhoea and wound-healing to cough, cold and gynaecological complaints.\n\nSubstitution and formulation\n\nVagbhata closes with two practical rules. First, if a named herb is unavailable, the physician may substitute another with the same properties, and may drop a member that does not suit the particular case. Second, these grouped drugs are not taken raw; they are worked into prepared forms such as a wet paste (kalka), a decoction (kwatha), a medicated fat (sneha) or a confection (leha), and then used by mouth, as nasal drops, as enemas or applied to the skin. In skilled hands such preparations can help even stubborn disease.\n\nSafety framing\n\nThis chapter shows how deep and deliberate classical Ayurvedic pharmacy is. It is knowledge for the dispensary, not the kitchen. Choosing a group, selecting members, judging dose, and turning plants into a safe medicine all require formal training and assessment of the individual patient. Several listed items are toxic unless properly processed. Nothing here should be gathered, combined or self-administered at home, and none of it replaces care from a qualified physician."},{"n":16,"title":"Oleation Therapy (Sneha Vidhi)","detail":"What oleation is\n\nThis chapter, Sneha Vidhi, explains Snehana, the therapy of giving measured fats to the body so that it becomes internally and externally lubricated. It is usually a preparation step, done before the deeper cleansing procedures such as therapeutic vomiting or purgation. The idea is that fat, when taken in a controlled way, softens and loosens accumulated waste (dosha) stuck in the tissues, so that cleansing can then carry it out smoothly. Oleation can also be used on its own to calm an aggravated dosha.\n\nImportant from the start: this is a medical procedure managed by a qualified Ayurvedic physician. Drinking medicated fat in therapeutic doses is very different from using a little ghee in food. It demands assessment of the person, careful dosing and daily supervision, and it is never something to attempt at home.\n\nThe fats used and their qualities\n\nClassical texts favour four fats: ghee (clarified butter), bone marrow, muscle fat and oil. Ghee is considered the finest because it is sweet, does not cause burning, is easy to digest and suits the body from birth. Ghee is seen as best for calming Pitta and oil the least so. Ghee is lightest to digest; oil is heavier, muscle fat heavier still, and marrow heaviest of all. Two fats combined are called Yamaka, three Trivrit, and all four together Mahasneha. The desirable qualities of an oleating substance are heaviness, coolness, the ability to spread, unctuousness, mildness, subtlety, softness and fluidity.\n\nWho is suitable and who is not\n\nOleation suits those about to undergo sweating or cleansing therapy, people worn down by overwork, wine or sexual activity, heavy thinkers, the elderly, children, the weak, the emaciated, those with dryness, depleted blood and semen, Vata disorders and certain eye conditions.\n\nIt is withheld from many: people with very weak or very strong digestion, the obese, the very weak, those with stiff thighs, diarrhoea, indigestion (ama), throat disease, chronic poisoning, fainting, vomiting, loss of appetite, Kapha disorders, excessive thirst, chronic alcoholism, a woman after miscarriage, and those about to receive nasal therapy, enema or purgation. Matching the fat to the person is itself a clinical decision: ghee for those needing better memory and intellect, oil for tumours, sinus, worms, Kapha and fat disorders and those wanting a leaner firmer body, and marrow and muscle fat for the depleted and for certain pains.\n\nHow it is given\n\nThe physician decides the dose by the person's digestive strength, often starting with a tiny test dose to read the digestion before fixing the real dose. A dose that digests in about six hours is small, nine hours medium, twelve hours large. Fat may be taken alone (acchapana, considered the strongest and quickest) or mixed into food (vicharana, milder). Timing matters: temperate seasons and a clear day are preferred, with adjustments for season, and fat taken before food acts on the lower body, during food on the middle, and after food on the upper body.\n\nAftercare and regimen\n\nAfter drinking fat, warm water is usually taken to aid digestion and clear the mouth. During the course the person keeps to warm water, light warm non-oily food, celibacy, and avoids suppressing natural urges, strenuous exercise, anger, grief, cold, wind, long travel, excess talking, awkward postures and daytime sleep. The course typically runs about three days for a soft bowel and up to seven for a hard bowel, or until the signs of good oleation appear. Fat continued beyond that point stops working well.\n\nReading the result\n\nSigns of proper oleation include downward movement of Vata, keen digestion, soft unformed stools, a feeling of aversion to fat and mild tiredness. The opposite signs mean the body is still too dry. Pallor and oily secretions from the nose, mouth and rectum mean the fat has been overdone. Wrong dose, wrong fat, wrong timing or wrong diet can cause complications such as swelling, piles, stupor, rigidity, itching, fever, nausea, abdominal pain and bloating; these are managed by the physician with fasting, lighter dry foods, specific drugs and measures to dry the body.\n\nShortcuts and cautions\n\nFor children, the elderly and those who cannot keep the restrictions, the texts give quick-oleation recipes using fat-rich gruels, soups and similar foods. Fats heavily salted also act fast. But certain items (jaggery, marshland meats, milk, sesame, black gram, beer, curd) are avoided in skin disease, swelling and diabetes because they worsen those conditions. Used wisely under supervision, regular oleation is said to keep digestion strong, the tissues well built and ageing slow.\n\nSafety framing\n\nEvery part of this, choosing the fat, fixing the dose, reading the daily signs and handling complications, is clinical work done only by a trained Ayurvedic physician after proper assessment. Therapeutic oleation is never a home practice and must not replace the treatment your doctor has prescribed."},{"n":17,"title":"Sweating Therapy (Sweda Vidhi)","detail":"What sweating therapy is\n\nThis chapter, Sweda Vidhi, explains Swedana, the therapy of making the body sweat on purpose using heat. It normally follows oleation (the fat therapy of the previous chapter). The reasoning is simple and sequential: oleation first softens and loosens the accumulated waste (dosha) sitting in the tissues, and sweating then melts and mobilises it, drawing it toward the digestive tract so that the cleansing procedures can expel it. Sweating relieves stiffness, cold and heaviness, and eases pain.\n\nImportant: this is a supervised clinical procedure. The heat, duration and method are all set by a qualified Ayurvedic physician for the individual, and some forms involve real heat sources and vulnerable body parts. It is not a home steam session and must never replace prescribed medical care.\n\nThe four kinds of sweating\n\nClassical texts describe four broad methods. Tapa sweda uses direct dry heat, such as a warmed cloth or plate applied to the affected part. Upanaha sweda is a warm medicated poultice bound onto the body, made from herbs, grains, fats and other ingredients and often left on and changed between day and night. Ushma sweda is steam or warm vapour directed at the body, generated from boiled grains, heated stones, sand, husk and similar materials. Drava sweda is the pouring of warm medicated liquid over the body, or having the patient sit in a tub of it, using decoctions of Vata-calming plants, meats and Dashamula mixed with suitable fats. The physician selects the method to suit the disease and the part involved.\n\nHow it is carried out\n\nSweating is given to a person who has already had oleation, who is in a draught-free room and whose previous meal is fully digested. The intensity, mild, moderate or strong, is chosen according to the disease, the patient's strength, the season and the climate. Dry sweating (without fats) is used for Kapha conditions, while fat-based sweating is used when Vata is involved, and the method is further adjusted by where the dosha sits. Crucially, certain delicate areas, the eyes, the heart region and the groin or scrotum, are given very little or no heat, because they are easily harmed.\n\nReading the result and avoiding excess\n\nWhen sweating is done correctly, cold, pain and stiffness ease and the part becomes soft; the person is then gently massaged and bathed in warm water. Over-sweating is dangerous: it can aggravate Pitta and blood, and cause thirst, fainting, weakness of voice and body, dizziness, joint pain, fever, skin discoloration and vomiting. If this happens, the physician applies cooling, holding (stambhana) measures to stop the excess, using substances that are the opposite of the heating ones. Over-done holding has its own warning signs, such as tightening skin, tremors and discoloration, so balance and judgement are essential.\n\nWho should not be sweated\n\nMany people must not be sweated, or only very mildly in an emergency: the very weak, the dehydrated and very dry, the unconscious, those bleeding or wounded, those with indigestion (ama), chronic alcoholics, people with Pitta disorders, jaundice, anaemia, urinary disorders, herpes, wasting, those who have just taken milk, curd or fat or just had purgation, and women who are pregnant, menstruating or recently delivered. Deciding this requires a physician's examination.\n\nWho benefits, and sweating without fire\n\nSweating helps conditions such as cough, breathlessness, running nose, hiccup, bloating, constipation, hoarseness, Vata disorders, body ache, stiffness of the back and flanks, locked jaw, sprains, retained urine and certain swellings. For milder needs the texts also describe sweating without a fire source, produced by a warm airtight room, heavy covers, physical exertion, wrestling and similar means.\n\nSafety framing\n\nSweating is the bridge step between oleation and actual elimination, which is why it is always set within a larger supervised plan. Picking the method, judging the heat, protecting delicate organs, screening who is unfit and catching the signs of excess are all the work of a trained Ayurvedic physician after proper assessment. This therapy must never be improvised at home and must not take the place of the treatment your doctor has prescribed."},{"n":18,"title":"Therapeutic Emesis and Purgation (Vamana-Virechana Vidhi)","detail":"What these therapies are\n\nThis chapter, Vamana-Virechana Vidhi, explains two of the main cleansing procedures of Panchakarma: Vamana, therapeutic vomiting, and Virechana, therapeutic purgation. Both are ways of expelling accumulated waste (dosha) from the body through a controlled route, vomiting upward or purging downward. They are the elimination step that follows the preparation steps of oleation (fat therapy) and sweating. Broadly, Vamana is chosen when Kapha is the main problem, and Virechana when Pitta is the main problem.\n\nImportant: these are serious clinical procedures with real risks, including, in the worst case, death from over-treatment. They are always carried out by a qualified Ayurvedic physician in a controlled setting, after full preparation and assessment, with the patient watched throughout. Nothing in this chapter is a home practice, and it must never replace the treatment your doctor has prescribed.\n\nTherapeutic emesis (Vamana)\n\nVamana is indicated classically for Kapha-dominant and certain other conditions, such as recent fever, some skin and respiratory diseases, nausea and disorders of the upper body. It is withheld from a long list of people, including pregnant women, those not prepared with oleation, the hungry, the grieving, children, the elderly, the weak, those with heart disease, wounds, bleeding tendencies, upward-moving Vata, enlarged spleen and many others.\n\nIn outline, the physician prepares the patient over the prior days with oleation and sweating, deliberately increases Kapha the day before with specific foods, ensures the previous meal is digested and the patient has rested, and then on the day gives the emetic medicine after suitable preparation. The patient sits supported, and vomiting is allowed to proceed in a measured way until the signs show the Kapha has been cleared and Pitta begins to appear. The physician reads the outcome by the number and nature of the bouts: too few (ayoga) leaves symptoms such as itching and fever; proper bouts (samyak yoga) bring calmness and a natural stop; excessive bouts (atiyoga) show frothy or blood-stained vomit and cause weakness, burning, dryness and grave danger. Afterwards the patient is comforted, may inhale medicated smoke, and follows the graded recovery diet.\n\nTherapeutic purgation (Virechana)\n\nVirechana is indicated for Pitta-dominant conditions and many others, such as abdominal masses, piles, jaundice and liver disease, chronic fever, certain eye and urinary disorders, gout and bleeding disorders of the upper body. It is withheld in recent fever, poor digestion, bleeding from the lower body, diarrhoea, foreign bodies, after enema, in those with naturally hard bowels or excessive prior oleation, and in the emaciated.\n\nAfter emesis and recovery, the patient is again prepared with oleation and sweating, and then given the purgative medicine in the morning once the Kapha time has passed. The drug and dose are matched to the person's bowel nature (a soft bowel may purge even with milk, a hard bowel resists even strong purgatives) and to the dosha (sweet and astringent drugs for Pitta, pungent for Kapha, unctuous-hot-salty for Vata). The physician again reads the result by the bouts and the material passed, watching for inadequate purgation, proper purgation, and over-purgation (which can bring watery meat-washing-like discharge, rectal prolapse, thirst, dizziness and sunken eyes).\n\nThe recovery regimen (Samsarjana Karma)\n\nBoth procedures leave the digestive fire weak, so recovery is gradual. The patient is stepped back onto normal food through a graded sequence of thin gruel, thick gruel, plain soup, prepared soup and finally meat juice, over a number of meals that depends on how strong the cleansing was. The chapter compares the digestive fire to a small spark that must be fed gently until it grows strong again. This staged diet is essential to recovery and is itself part of the medical plan.\n\nDosing wisdom and safety\n\nThe texts stress caution: weak patients, the emaciated, and those whose bowel nature is unknown should be given mild purgatives in small, repeated doses rather than one large dose, because a large dose given once risks death. Dry patients with strong Vata may need an enema first. Those with poison, trauma, skin disease, jaundice, anaemia or diabetes need only mild oleation before purgation. Done properly, purification brings clarity of mind, strong senses, stable tissues, keen digestion and slower ageing; done on a body not first prepared with oleation and sweating, it can break the person like bending a dry stick.\n\nSafety framing\n\nEvery stage, deciding which procedure fits, screening who is unfit, preparing the body, choosing and dosing the medicine, reading the bouts, catching over-treatment and running the recovery diet, is skilled clinical work performed only by a trained Ayurvedic physician after proper assessment, in a setting where the patient can be watched and complications handled. Therapeutic vomiting and purgation are powerful and potentially dangerous. They must never be attempted at home, never self-dosed, and never used in place of the care your doctor has prescribed."},{"n":19,"title":"Enema Therapy (Basti)","detail":"What Basti Is\n\nBasti means medicated enema, where liquid medicine is introduced into the body through the rectum (and in special cases through the urinary or genital passage). The name comes from the animal bladder that physicians of old used as the holding bag for the fluid. This chapter of the Ashtanga Hridaya treats enema as the single most powerful tool against disordered Vata, the biological air principle that governs all movement in the body. The classical texts rate it so highly that some call it half of all treatment, and a few call it the whole of treatment, because when Vata is set right much else falls into place.\n\nWhy It Matters for Vata\n\nVata is seen as the force that carries things through the body and also clears out accumulated waste, excess Kapha and Pitta. When Vata is badly disturbed, there is said to be no remedy more effective than enema, whether the trouble sits in the limbs, the digestive tract, the vital centres, or the region above the shoulders. Blood-letting is mentioned in the same breath as the matching strong treatment for blood disorders.\n\nThe Main Types\n\nBasti is classically grouped in three forms. Niruha, also called Asthapana, is a decoction enema: a herbal decoction blended with salt, honey, fat and herbal paste, used mainly to cleanse and expel. Anuvasana, also called Sneha Basti, is a fat or oil enema using medicated oils and ghee, used to lubricate and nourish. Uttarabasti is given through the urethra or, in women, the vaginal and uterine route, for disorders of the bladder and reproductive organs. A very gentle low-dose oil enema called Matra Basti is also described.\n\nWho It Is For\n\nDecoction enema is classically indicated in abdominal tumours, bloating and fullness, gout, spleen enlargement, simple diarrhoea, abdominal pain, long-standing fever, blocked flatus, urine and stool, scrotal enlargement, urinary stones, absent menstruation and severe Vata disorders. Oil enema suits the same patients, especially those with strong digestion, a dry constitution and Vata disease. The text is equally careful to list who should NOT receive it, such as the very weak and emaciated, those with chest injury, recent or Ama-type diarrhoea, vomiting, breathlessness and cough, piles, hiccups, ascites, diabetes, skin disease, and pregnant women in later months. Age-based dosing and nozzle sizes are given in fine detail.\n\nHow It Is Classically Done\n\nThe chapter describes elaborate preparation: oleation and sweating first, then light meals, correct timing by season and time of day, the exact nozzle length and bore for each age, how the bag is filled and warmed, the direction and gentle single-stroke pressure of administration, and detailed aftercare, including how long the material may safely stay inside and what to do if it does not return. Decoction enema is given on an empty stomach; oil enema is given soon after food. Structured courses are named by their length and pattern, such as the 30-enema Karma Basti, the 15-enema Kala Basti and the 8-enema Yoga Basti, always alternating oil and decoction so the body is both lubricated and purified.\n\nSafety\n\nEvery form here is a formal clinical procedure. The classical rules on candidate selection, dosing, timing and the narrow window for the material to return show how much skill and judgement the treatment demands. It is performed only by a trained Ayurvedic physician, usually with assistants, after examining the person, the disorder and the medicines. It is never something to attempt at home, and it does not replace modern medical care."},{"n":20,"title":"Nasal Therapy (Nasya)","detail":"What Nasya Is\n\nNasya means putting medicine into the nostrils. The classical reasoning is simple and striking: the nose is described as the doorway to the head, so medicine placed there spreads upward and reaches the whole region of the head and neck. For this reason Nasya is the treatment of choice for disorders of the parts above the shoulders, the area ordinary oral medicines reach less directly.\n\nThe Three Kinds\n\nThe chapter divides Nasya into three broad actions. Virechana Nasya is the cleansing or purgative type, which expels stuck Doshas from the head and is used in headache, heaviness and stiffness of the head, certain eye and throat diseases, swellings, gland enlargement, worm trouble, growths, skin disease, seizure disorders and chronic nasal catarrh. Brimhana Nasya is the nourishing type, using rich materials such as meat juice, and is used for Vata headaches, a headache that worsens through the day, loss of voice, dryness of the nose and mouth, difficulty speaking, difficulty opening the eyes and a stiff shoulder or arm. Shamana Nasya is the soothing type, used for skin discolouration of the face, hair and eye complaints, prepared with gentler liquids such as milk or even water.\n\nFurther Subdivisions\n\nOil-based Nasya is split by dose into Marsha, the higher dose, and Pratimarsha, a very low daily dose of just a drop or two. Pratimarsha is prized because it is so gentle that it can be used from childhood to old age, at almost any time, without the dietary restrictions or risks of the stronger forms. Strong head-purging methods include Avapeeda, made from paste or fresh juice, and Dhmana, a medicated powder blown into the nose through a tube.\n\nDose, Timing and Who Should Avoid It\n\nThe text gives careful dose measures counted in drops, and lays out when to give Nasya by season and time of day, for example in the morning for Kapha and in the evening for the healthy. It also lists many people who should not receive it, such as those who have just eaten, bathed, drunk alcohol or water, had blood-letting, those with acute rhinitis, breathlessness or cough, recently delivered mothers, and it sets age limits, generally between about seven and eighty years. Signs of correct, insufficient and excessive therapy are described so the physician can judge the result.\n\nAnu Taila and Benefits\n\nA famous medicated nasal oil called Anu Taila is described, made by repeatedly cooking sesame oil with a long list of herbs and finishing with goat milk. Regular, correct nasal therapy is said to strengthen the neck, shoulders, face and senses, keep the voice clear and even delay greying of the hair.\n\nSafety\n\nAll of this is classical clinical knowledge, not a home routine. Choosing the right type, material, dose, season and timing, and judging when to stop, calls for training. Nasya is carried out by or under a qualified Ayurvedic physician. The strong purgative and powder forms in particular are never to be self-administered, and none of this replaces proper medical care."},{"n":21,"title":"Medicated Smoke Inhalation (Dhumapana)","detail":"What Dhumapana Is\n\nDhumapana means drawing in the smoke of medicinal herbs through a special pipe. The chapter opens by saying that a person who wishes to look after their health may inhale herbal smoke regularly to treat, and to help prevent, disorders of the region above the shoulders that arise from excess Kapha and Vata. It is a therapy aimed squarely at the head, throat, nose and chest.\n\nThe Three Strengths\n\nThe smoke is graded by strength. The soft or lubricating smoke, called Snigdha or Mridu, is for Vata. The medium smoke, Madhya, also called the soothing or Shamana type, is for mixed Vata and Kapha. The strong smoke, Teekshna, also called the purifying or Virechana type, is for Kapha. The physician chooses the grade to match the disturbance.\n\nRight and Wrong Times\n\nThe text is precise about timing. Mild smoke is taken after sneezing, yawning, passing stool or urine, intimacy, minor surgery, laughing or tooth cleaning. Medium smoke suits the end of the night, after meals and after nasal therapy. Strong smoke suits waking from sleep, after nasal therapy, after applying collyrium, after a bath and after vomiting. Done at the wrong time or in excess, smoke is said to cause serious trouble, including bleeding disorders, blindness, deafness, intense thirst, fainting and confusion, for which a cooling regimen is the remedy.\n\nWho Must Avoid It\n\nMany people are warned off smoke therapy, including those with bleeding disorders, those freshly purged, people with ascites, diabetes, certain eye disease, upward-moving Vata, bloating, a grave throat disease, those just given an enema, those who have just taken fish, alcohol, curd, milk, honey or fat, the head-injured, the anaemic and those who stayed awake at night.\n\nThe Apparatus and Method\n\nThe smoking pipe is made of the same kind of material as the enema nozzle, with three chambers, straight, and of a set length for each grade of smoke. A medicated wick is prepared by soaking a reed, wrapping it in cloth layers, coating it with herbal paste, drying it in shade, removing the reed, smearing it with oil, then lighting it inside the tube. The person sits straight and alert, inhales through one nostril while closing the other, or through the mouth as the case needs, and importantly lets the smoke out only through the mouth, never the nose, since releasing it through the nose is said to harm the eyes. A separate earthen-pot arrangement is described for a cough-relieving smoke. Herbal blends are listed for each of the three strengths.\n\nBenefits and Safety\n\nHabitual, correct smoke therapy is said to protect against cough, breathlessness, rhinitis, voice disorders, bad odour of the nose and mouth, hair trouble and weakness of the ears, mouth and eyes. All the same, the many cautions, the exact timing rules and the real harm from misuse show this is a controlled clinical therapy. It is done only by or under a trained Ayurvedic physician, is not a casual habit, is not to be self-administered, and does not replace medical care. It has nothing to do with recreational smoking."},{"n":22,"title":"Mouth, Head and Ear Therapies (Gandushadi)","detail":"What This Chapter Covers\n\nThis chapter gathers together a family of local therapies for the mouth, face, head and ears. It takes its name from Gandusha, the practice of holding liquid medicine in the mouth, but it goes on to cover mouth gargling, coating the inside of the mouth with herbs, applying herbal paste to the face, pouring and holding oil on the head, and filling the ears with oil.\n\nGandusha and Kavala\n\nGandusha means filling the mouth completely with a medicated liquid and holding it still, without swishing, for a set time until the mouth fills with watery Kapha or the nose and eyes begin to run. Kavala is the related practice of taking a smaller amount of liquid and actively moving it around the mouth, that is, gargling. Gandusha is classically grouped in four kinds by purpose: the lubricating type with oils for Vata, the soothing type with bitter, astringent and sweet herbs for Pitta, the purifying type with hot, pungent and salty herbs for Kapha, and the healing type with astringent and bitter herbs. A wide range of liquids is used, including sesame oil, ghee, milk, honey with water, fermented preparations, meat juice and fermented grain wash, chosen to match the condition and always kept comfortable to hold.\n\nEveryday Mouth Care\n\nSome of these practices are presented as ordinary daily upkeep rather than strong treatment. Holding or swishing plain comfortably warm water lightens and cleanses the mouth. Daily holding of sesame oil or meat soup is called good routine care. Sesame-paste water, warm or cool, eases tingling or shaky teeth. Honey held in the mouth clears sliminess, helps mouth ulcers and relieves burning and thirst. Fermented grain wash removes bad taste, coating and odour. These gentle, non-medicated daily rinses and oil-holding can reasonably be treated as simple self-care. Anything using strong, medicated, hot or purifying ingredients is a different matter and stays physician-guided.\n\nMouth Coating and Facial Paste\n\nPratisarana is rubbing herbs, as paste, thickened decoction or powder, onto the inside of the mouth for Kapha-type mouth disorders. Mukhalepa is applying herbal paste to the face in three styles, to clear Doshas, to counter poison, or to improve complexion, applied warm or cold by Dosha, in set thicknesses, and removed as soon as it dries. The chapter even lists six seasonal facial recipes.\n\nOil Therapies of the Head and Ears\n\nMurdha Taila, oiling of the head, is given in four forms of increasing power: Shiro Abhyanga, oiling with gentle massage, for dryness and itching; Shiro Pariseka or Shirodhara, pouring oil in a steady stream, for scalp ulceration, headache and burning; Shiro Pichu, keeping an oil-soaked cloth on the scalp, for hair fall and cracking; and Shiro Basti, holding a pool of oil on the head within a fitted leather band, the strongest, for facial palsy, loss of scalp sensation, sleeplessness, dryness of nose and mouth and grave head disease. Karna Purana is filling the ears with medicated oil to settle ear pain and disease. Oiling the head is said to prevent hair fall, greying and matting, ease scalp dryness and Vata head disorders, and strengthen the voice, jaw and head.\n\nSafety\n\nThe gentle daily rinses, oil-holding and plain-water gargles described here can be used as mild self-care within reason. But the strong, medicated, hot or purifying gargles, the mouth coatings, the facial pastes, and especially the head-oil procedures such as Shirodhara and Shiro Basti and ear-oiling for disease are clinical therapies. They involve correct materials, timing, thickness, holding times and aftercare, and are carried out by or under a qualified Ayurvedic physician. None of these is a replacement for proper medical care."},{"n":23,"title":"Eye Drops and Collyrium (Aschotana and Anjana)","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis is a clinical chapter of classical Ayurveda that describes two external eye treatments used by the trained physician: Aschotana (instilling medicated liquid drops into the eye) and Anjana (applying a medicated paste, gel or powder, a kind of collyrium, to the inner surface of the lids). These are specialist eye-care procedures from an ancient surgical tradition, not home remedies. In a modern setting any eye complaint belongs with a qualified eye doctor.\n\nAschotana (medicated eye drops)\n\nThe text presents drops as broadly useful across many eye complaints, to ease symptoms such as pain, a pricking feeling, itching, a sense of friction, watering, burning and redness. The temperature of the drops was matched to the imbalance being treated: warm for Vata, lukewarm for Kapha, and cool for conditions linked to Pitta or to vitiated blood. The classical method had the patient lie in a draught-free place while the physician held the lids open and released a measured number of drops just above the inner corner of the eye, then wiped the eye clean and applied gentle warm or cold compresses depending on the disorder. The emphasis throughout is on correct dose and temperature: drops that are too hot, too cold, too strong, too much or too little were each said to cause their own harm, which is exactly why judgement of a trained practitioner was required.\n\nAnjana (collyrium)\n\nAnjana was applied only after the body had been suitably prepared and when the disease was judged 'ripe' and localized to the eye. The text names three types by purpose: Lekhana (scraping or reducing, from pungent, sour, salty, astringent and heating herbs), Ropana (healing, from bitter herbs), and Prasadana (soothing and clarifying, from sweet, cooling herbs). A counter-collyrium (Pratyanjana), usually a mild cooling one, was used to settle the eye if a strong collyrium irritated it. Different applicator rods and forms (pill, semi-solid gel, powder) were chosen for mild, moderate or severe imbalance.\n\nTiming, after-care and precautions\n\nTiming mattered: the classical texts advised against applying collyrium at night, during sleep, at midday or when the eyes were tired from sun glare, and favoured morning or evening. After application the eye was gently moved and the lids lightly massaged to spread the medicine, then washed at the right time so that no residue remained to irritate the eye. The chapter lists many states in which Anjana should not be given at all, for example in fear, anger, grief, fever, headache, indigestion, after a head bath, after loss of sleep, when hungry or thirsty, or when the person has just undergone purification therapy. This long list of cautions underlines that these were careful clinical decisions.\n\nImportant note\n\nEverything here is historical and educational. These are clinical eye procedures that in classical practice were performed only by a trained physician. They must never be attempted at home, and nothing here replaces modern eye care. Anyone with an eye problem should see a qualified doctor."},{"n":24,"title":"Eye Nourishing Therapies (Tarpana and Putapaka)","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis clinical chapter describes two specialist eye-strengthening procedures of classical Ayurveda: Tarpana (bathing the eye in medicated ghee or fat held in place over the eye) and Putapaka (instilling a concentrated juice prepared from meat and herbs by a special roasting method). Both are nourishing eye therapies carried out by a trained physician in a controlled setting. They are presented here only for education and are not something to attempt at home.\n\nTarpana (eye bathing with medicated fat)\n\nTarpana was classically considered for tired, dry, stiff or rough eyes, for clouded or weak vision, and after the acute redness, pain, watering and swelling of an eye disorder had settled. Before it, the person was prepared by cleansing of head and body. The method, in broad terms, built a small retaining wall of barley-and-black-gram paste around the eye socket and poured warm, melted medicated ghee into this 'well' so the closed eye was bathed; in certain conditions muscle fat was used instead of ghee. The person then opened the eyes slowly and the fat was retained for a set counted time that varied by which part of the eye was affected and by the dominant imbalance. Afterwards the fat was drained, medicated smoking was advised, and the person was told to avoid looking at bright light. The texts describe signs of a well-done, an inadequate and an excessive treatment, and set out how often it should be repeated depending on the disorder.\n\nPutapaka (concentrated eye instillation)\n\nBecause the eye can feel fatigued after Tarpana, Putapaka was used to restore its strength. A paste of meat and herbs was rolled into a ball, wrapped in leaves, coated in mud, dried and roasted in fire until red-hot, then cooled, and the juice squeezed out was instilled into the eye much like Tarpana. Three forms are described by aim: Snehana (nourishing, for Vata), Lekhana (reducing or scraping, for Vata-Kapha), and Prasadana (soothing, for weakness and for Pitta or blood disorders). Retention times and temperature (lukewarm or cold) were matched to the type.\n\nAfter-care and cautions\n\nAfter either therapy the person followed supportive food and conduct for roughly twice the number of treatment days and, at night, rested a soft pad of fragrant flowers over the eyes. The chapter is clear that these therapies should not be given to anyone unfit for nasal cleansing therapy, and it closes by stressing how precious sight is and how much effort protecting the eyes deserves.\n\nImportant note\n\nThese are historical, physician-only eye procedures involving heated fats, roasting and precise timing. They were performed only by a trained physician and must never be attempted at home. Nothing here replaces modern eye care; any eye problem should be seen by a qualified doctor."},{"n":25,"title":"Blunt Surgical Instruments (Yantra Vidhi)","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis is a surgical chapter of classical Ayurveda cataloguing the Yantras, the blunt instruments and appliances used by the surgeon. Unlike sharp instruments that cut, blunt instruments grasp, hold, probe, lift, extract, suck, squeeze and support. The chapter is educational and describes tools of an ancient operating tradition; everything here belongs to trained surgical practice, not to home use.\n\nPurpose of the instruments\n\nThe text explains that blunt instruments were used to extract foreign bodies lodged in different parts of the body, to inspect conditions such as piles and rectal fistula, to assist in applying sharp instruments, caustic alkali and cautery, and to protect healthy parts during procedures like enema. It notes that instruments come in many shapes and sizes and should be fabricated thoughtfully, often with mouths shaped like the beaks of birds or jaws of animals and named accordingly.\n\nThe main families of instruments\n\nThe chapter describes a long list, including: cruciform grasping instruments (Swastika) for pulling foreign bodies stuck in bone; forceps (Samdamsha) with and without a catch for skin, vessels, tendons, muscles, and for tiny objects or eyelashes; toothed forceps (Muchundi) for deep fleshy remnants; flat-armed instruments (Tala) for the ear canal; and hollow tubular instruments and probes (Nadi Yantra) of many openings and sizes for inspecting channels, draining and suction, including specialized tubes for viewing the throat, the rectum and piles (Arsho Yantra), nasal growths, the vagina and wounds, and for ascites. It also covers a finger-guard, horn and gourd appliances used for cupping-style suction to draw out vitiated blood or pus, a pot appliance, rod-like instruments (Shalaka) for probing and extraction, a set of hooks (Shanku) including one for impacted delivery and others for removing bladder stones or teeth, cotton-tipped rods for cleaning sinuses, and smooth cylindrical stones (Jambavaushtha) used alongside alkali and cautery. Accessory aids (Anu Yantra) such as magnet, rope, cloth, hammer and even the hands, teeth and the forces of time and digestion are listed as adjuncts used judiciously.\n\nFunctions and the key instrument\n\nThe text summarizes the actions these tools perform: extracting, twisting out, filling, clearing a passage, bringing parts together, binding, pressing, suction, lifting, pushing down, shaking, breaking, turning over and straightening. It singles out the heron-mouthed grasping instrument (Kankamukha) as the most versatile and indispensable of the blunt instruments.\n\nImportant note\n\nThis is a historical, educational survey of surgical instruments. Any procedure using them is a surgical act for a trained surgeon in a proper setting. None of this should ever be attempted at home, and it is not a substitute for modern medical or surgical care."},{"n":26,"title":"Sharp Surgical Instruments (Shastra Vidhi)","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis surgical chapter of classical Ayurveda describes the Shastras, the sharp instruments used by the surgeon for cutting, incising, puncturing, scraping and suturing, together with their proper qualities, defects, how to hold them, how to store them, and the non-metallic accessory agents (including leeches). It is presented for education. Every tool and action here belongs to trained surgical practice and is never a home procedure.\n\nQualities of a good sharp instrument\n\nThe text says there are twenty-six sharp instruments, made by skilled smiths from well-refined steel. A good instrument should be well shaped, pleasant to look at, firm to hold, keen enough to split a hair, with a well-hammered edge, and sized appropriately, with the cutting edge a set fraction of the whole length. Having two or three of each, suited to the operation site, was advised.\n\nThe instruments and their uses\n\nAmong the many named instruments, the chapter describes round-tipped knives for scraping and excising lid cysts and tonsils; razor-like scalpels and lancets for incising, splitting and draining swellings, both shallow and deep-seated; serpent-mouth and heron-beak shaped blades and scissors for cutting nasal and ear polyps and for draining; sharp probes for exploring sinuses; needle-tipped and grain-shaped blades for puncturing veins and the abdomen; an axe-shaped blade for cutting a vein lying on bone; a special rod for couching a cataractous lens; a finger-knife for throat-passage work; a hooked blade for holding the uvula or pterygium; a saw for bone; scissors for tendons, threads and hair; brushes with spikes and a churner for scraping skin patches and removing vitiated nasal blood; a dental lancet for scraping tartar; needles of different shapes for suturing flesh, joints and vital parts; and awls and ear-piercing blades. The chapter also lists the non-metallic accessory instruments: leeches, caustic alkali, fire, glass, nail and stone.\n\nFunctions, defects, handling and storage\n\nIt summarizes the actions of sharp instruments (extracting, splitting, suturing, probing, scraping, scratching, pounding, excising, breaking, puncturing, churning, grasping and cauterizing), lists eight defects to avoid (bluntness, chipping, being too thin or too thick, too short or too long, crookedness and a rough edge), and gives the correct finger-grip for each kind of action. It describes a well-made instrument wallet with padded compartments to keep the tools safe and clean.\n\nLeeches and bloodletting by cupping or incision\n\nA large part of the chapter deals with medicinal leeches for drawing off vitiated blood: how to tell safe from poisonous leeches, how to apply them, how to make them detach and disgorge, and how to care for them. It also covers scratch-incision (Pracchana) to draw out locally pooled blood, and the use of horn and gourd for suction, noting which method suits blood vitiated by which dosha, and the after-care of the bled site.\n\nImportant note\n\nThis is historical and educational. Sharp-instrument surgery, leeching and bloodletting are clinical procedures for a trained surgeon in a proper setting. They must never be attempted at home and are not a substitute for modern surgical or medical care. Nothing here is a promised cure, and no one should delay proper treatment because of it."},{"n":27,"title":"Venesection and Bloodletting (Siravyadha)","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis surgical chapter of classical Ayurveda explains Siravyadha, the deliberate opening of a vein to let out blood believed to be vitiated. It describes the signs of healthy versus vitiated blood, who should and should not undergo the procedure, how the site was chosen, the method and its after-care, and the warning signs of too little or too much bleeding. It is presented for education only. Venesection is a clinical procedure that was performed by a trained physician and must never be attempted at home.\n\nHealthy versus vitiated blood\n\nThe text first describes the qualities of healthy blood (balanced taste, neither too hot nor cold, a clear red colour) and explains that blood disturbed mainly by Pitta and Kapha was thought to cause a range of disorders, so curable complaints that failed to settle with other measures were sometimes attributed to vitiated blood and treated by letting it out.\n\nWho should not undergo it\n\nThe chapter is careful about safety. It lists many people for whom venesection was considered unsuitable, for example the very young and the elderly, pregnant and recently delivered women, and those weak, anaemic, feverish, with diarrhoea, breathing trouble, swelling or recent oleation and purification therapy. It also advised against the procedure in extreme weather. This long list of exclusions shows how much judgement the procedure required.\n\nMethod and site\n\nThe person was first prepared with oleation and sweating and seated in a described posture, and the limb or part was bound above the chosen point to make the vein stand up. Specific sites were matched to the disorder being treated, and a vein free of vital spots was chosen. The physician then opened the vein with the appropriate fine instrument, using only a small opening on fleshy parts and a controlled cut over bone. Signs of a proper opening (a steady flow that stops when the binding is released), of an inadequate one, and of an excessive one (forceful flow, hard to stop) are described, along with reasons blood may fail to flow, such as fear, fainting, a loose binding, a blunt instrument or lack of prior sweating.\n\nHow much, and after-care\n\nA strict upper limit on the volume drawn was emphasized, because excessive bleeding was said to risk serious harm or death; oil massage and nourishing drinks were the remedy for over-bleeding. The colour and quality of blood from Vata, Pitta or Kapha vitiation are described so the physician could judge progress. Afterwards the binding was released slowly, the site washed, dressed with an oil-soaked swab and bandaged, and the person kept to light, easily digested food while the body recovered. Methods to stop bleeding that would not settle, including cautery of the vein ends, are given. The chapter ends by describing the healthy, well-nourished state of a person whose blood is not vitiated.\n\nImportant note\n\nThis is historical and educational. Bloodletting by opening a vein is a clinical surgical procedure; in classical practice it was done only by a trained physician, with strict limits and after-care. It must never be attempted at home and is not a substitute for modern medical care. Nothing here is a promised cure, and no one should delay proper treatment or stop prescribed medicine because of it."},{"n":28,"title":"Removing Foreign Bodies (Shalya Aharana)","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis surgical chapter of classical Ayurveda deals with Shalya Aharana, locating and removing foreign bodies lodged in the body. In the era it was written, war wounds from arrows and sharp weapons made this an important branch of surgery. The chapter describes how a hidden foreign body is recognized, how its position and shape are judged, which ones should and should not be removed, and the many techniques and instruments used to extract them. It is educational only; all of this is surgery for a trained surgeon and never a home procedure.\n\nRecognizing a foreign body\n\nThe text explains that a wound holding a foreign body tends to show bluish discolouration, swelling, pain, repeated bleeding and soft, non-healing tissue. It then describes how the signs differ by the tissue the object sits in: skin, muscle, between muscles, tendons and nerves, veins, body channels, arteries, joints, bones, the abdomen and vital spots each produce their own pattern of symptoms and their own kind of discharge, which helped the surgeon work out where the object lay.\n\nLocating and judging it\n\nSeveral methods for pinpointing a concealed object are given, such as watching for redness, pain and tenderness after oil, fomentation or massage, noting where placed ghee melts or sandal paste dries quickly, and slimming the body so hidden sites declare themselves. The shape of the invisible object was inferred from the shape of the wound.\n\nWhich to remove and which not\n\nForeign bodies that could be cut down to and had broad extractable blades were removed, while the chapter warns against removing those whose removal would cause sudden death, those truly lost or absorbed by the body, and those causing no trouble. Direction mattered: objects entering from above or below were drawn out the way that opposed their entry, and those entering sideways were removed by cutting conveniently.\n\nTechniques of extraction\n\nThe chapter gives a rich set of methods: removal by hand; grasping with animal-faced instruments and forceps; loosening objects in veins and tendons with rod-like instruments before extraction; sprinkling cool water for a chest-lodged object; holding the patient firmly or using strong attendants for a bone-lodged arrow; and, famously, the bow-and-horse method, where the arrow's feathered end is tied to a bent bow fastened to a horse's bridle so the horse's sudden movement pulls it free. Tubular instruments, hammers to free a stuck arrow, and magnets for iron arrowheads are described. Objects in the lower gut were removed by inducing purgation; gas, poison, milk or fluids acting as foreign matter by suction with a horn. Specific tricks are given for objects in the throat (a thread-tied sponge or lotus stalk, a hair-ball swallowed then vomited, lac-smeared food for a lac object, a heated rod for metal), for water in the lungs from near-drowning, and for water or insects in the ear. The chapter notes which materials dissolve in body heat and which do not, and that objects set deep in flesh may need the area ripened before extraction by excision or probing.\n\nImportant note\n\nThis is historical and educational. Finding and removing a foreign body is a surgical procedure for a trained surgeon in a proper setting. It must never be attempted at home and is not a substitute for modern emergency or surgical care. Nothing here is a promised cure, and anyone with an embedded foreign body or injury should seek proper medical help at once."},{"n":29,"title":"Surgical Operations (Shastrakarma)","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis surgical chapter of classical Ayurveda sets out Shastrakarma, the operation of incising and draining abscesses and swellings, along with the stages of a swelling, pre-operative preparation, the operation itself, the qualities of a good surgeon, suturing of fresh wounds, dressings and bandages, and detailed after-care of the wound. It is presented for education. All of it is surgery performed by a trained surgeon, never a home procedure.\n\nStages of a swelling\n\nThe text explains that an inflammatory swelling passes through three stages: unripe (Ama, raw, no pus, same colour as skin), ripening (Pachyamana, changing colour, red, tense, painful, with fever and other distress), and ripe (Pakva, softened, wrinkled, with pus movement felt on touch). It describes how Vata, Pitta, Kapha and vitiated blood each colour the picture, and notes that a slow-ripening Kapha swelling can mislead. The key teaching is timing: opening an unripe swelling causes harm (bleeding, pain, spreading damage), while neglecting a ripe one lets pus burn inward; a surgeon who gets either wrong is called careless.\n\nBefore the operation\n\nIn the weak, the frightened, children, or when pus is pointing on a vital spot or joint, bursting the swelling with caustic alkali (Darana) was preferred; otherwise it was cut open (Patana). The person was generally given liked food beforehand, or strong wine to withstand pain if accustomed to it, with important exceptions (obstructed delivery, stone, mouth and abdominal disease) where neither food nor wine was given.\n\nThe operation and the surgeon\n\nKeeping all equipment ready, the surgeon seated the patient, incised the swelling in the direction of the hair, avoiding vital parts, deep enough to reach the pus, then removed the instrument quickly; for a large area several spaced incisions were made and the interior thoroughly cleared so nothing harmful remained. The desired qualities of a surgeon are listed: courage, swift action, very sharp instruments, no sweating, no trembling and no confusion. Certain sites (forehead, brow, gums, shoulders, abdomen, axillae, lips, cheeks, throat, groins) required a curved or horizontal incision to avoid cutting vessels and tendons.\n\nAfter the operation\n\nThe chapter gives thorough after-care: reassuring the patient, washing the wound with a drug decoction, drying it, fumigating with medicated smoke, placing a medicated wick inside, applying a flour-and-ghee plaster and bandaging in the correct direction. Detailed guidance follows on diet to favour and to avoid, on conduct (avoiding day-sleep, strenuous activity, keeping awake at night, and sexual activity, which could worsen or even endanger), on protecting the wound, on the properties a good wick and bandage cloth should have, and on when to re-dress.\n\nFresh wounds, suturing and bandages\n\nIt describes immediate suturing of recent, wide, traumatic wounds and those on fleshy immovable parts, and which wounds should not be sutured (those emitting gas, holding a foreign body, or caused by alkali, poison or fire). It covers preparing the wound before suture, the materials used, spacing, inducing a little bleeding to aid healing, the fifteen named bandage shapes and where each applies, and how tight or loose to bind by site, season and dosha, with the caution that failing to bandage lets a wound get contaminated and heal slowly. It ends with long-term care, foods to avoid during healing, and leading a disciplined life for several months until full strength returns.\n\nImportant note\n\nThis is historical and educational. Incising and draining abscesses, suturing and the related wound care are surgical procedures for a trained surgeon in a proper setting. They must never be attempted at home and are not a substitute for modern surgical or medical care. Nothing here is a promised cure, and no one should delay proper treatment or stop prescribed medicine because of it."},{"n":30,"title":"Cautery and Alkali (Kshara and Agnikarma)","detail":"What this chapter covers\n\nThis surgical chapter of classical Ayurveda describes two powerful chemical and thermal procedures: Kshara (caustic alkali) and Agnikarma (thermal cautery or branding). Both destroy or seal tissue and were considered strong measures. The chapter sets out their uses and the many situations where they must not be used, how the alkali was prepared in three strengths, how each was applied, the signs of correct, inadequate and excessive burning, and the after-care. It is educational only. These are hazardous clinical procedures for a trained physician or surgeon and must never be attempted at home.\n\nKshara (caustic alkali)\n\nThe text praises alkali as highly versatile, able to incise, excise and reach places instruments cannot, and usable both applied to the surface and, in some conditions, taken as a drink. Classical surface uses included warts and moles, external piles, certain skin lesions, fistula, growths and foul sinus ulcers; drinkable forms were described for some internal complaints. A long list of contraindications is given, for example aggravated Pitta or blood, fever, diarrhoea, heart and head disease, anaemia, pregnancy and menstruation, infancy and old age, and many delicate body sites such as vital spots, joints, vessels, nerves, testes and the eyes (except the lids). Preparation is described in detail: plant parts were burnt to ash, the ash dissolved, filtered and cooked, and various minerals added to produce a mild, medium or strong alkali. Ten ideal qualities of a good alkali are listed (neither too strong nor too mild, smooth, able to act then settle on its own, not too painful). Application involved cleaning or scoring the site, protecting surrounding tissue, placing the alkali for a counted time, then wiping it off and soothing the burn with ghee, honey and cooling pastes. Signs of proper burning (a dark, jambu-fruit colour and a depressed site) and of over-burning (bleeding, fainting, serious damage, even death at sensitive sites) are given, with the caution that alkali in unskilled hands is like a deadly weapon but in skilled hands can relieve dreaded disease.\n\nAgnikarma (thermal cautery)\n\nThe chapter calls fire even more powerful than alkali, because conditions treated by cautery were said not to recur and because it could help where drugs, alkali and ordinary surgery had failed. It was applied to skin, muscle, vein, tendon, joint and bone using heated agents such as a lighted wick, metal rods, honey, fats and jaggery, chosen by the depth of tissue to be burned. Uses included certain growths, haemorrhoids, fistula, foul ulcers and bleeding points. Contraindications mirror those of alkali, and also wounds holding a foreign body or pooled blood and severely injured people. Four grades of burning are described, from inadequate (Tuccha), through improper (Durdagdha) and proper (Samyak), to excessive (Atidagdha), each with its own signs and its own corrective after-care. The chapter closes by naming the knife, alkali and fire as the chief weapons of death, to be used only with the greatest care.\n\nImportant note\n\nThis is historical and educational. Caustic alkali and thermal cautery destroy tissue and can cause serious harm; in classical practice they were used only by a trained physician or surgeon, with strict selection and after-care. They must never be attempted at home and are not a substitute for modern medical or surgical care. Nothing here is a promised cure, and no one should delay proper treatment or stop prescribed medicine because of it."}],"remedies":[{"problem":"The three doshas (Vata, Pitta, Kapha) and how they keep you well","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata, Pitta, Kapha","signs":"For anyone wanting to understand why they feel the way they do","guidance":"Ayurveda sees the body as run by three working forces. Vata governs all movement (breath, circulation, nerve signals, elimination) and is tied to air and space. Pitta governs transformation, heat and digestion and is tied to fire. Kapha governs structure, moisture and steadiness and is tied to water and earth. Health is simply these three staying in balance; illness is any one becoming too high or too low. They sit throughout the body but each has a base: Kapha from the chest up, Pitta between chest and navel, Vata below the navel. Understanding which force is disturbed is the first step before choosing food, routine or any remedy, because everything in this system works by steadying the dosha that is off.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Qualities of Vata and how to settle it","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Dryness, cold hands and feet, lightness, restlessness, irregular habits","guidance":"Vata carries six qualities: dry (ruksha), light (laghu), cold (sheeta), rough (khara), subtle or able to slip into small spaces (sookshma), and mobile (chala). Because like increases like, dry cold windy conditions, raw and dry foods, and constant movement all push Vata up and can show as dry skin, cracking joints, gas, anxiety and broken sleep. The way back is the opposite set of qualities: warmth, oiliness, steadiness and routine. Warm cooked moist food, oil massage, rest and a regular daily rhythm all counter Vata's dry, cold, moving nature.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Qualities of Pitta and how to settle it","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"Excess heat, acidity, sharp hunger, irritability, inflammation","guidance":"Pitta carries these qualities: slightly oily (sasneha), sharp and penetrating (teekshna), hot (ushna), light (laghu), foul-smelling (visra), flowing (sara), and liquid (drava). Heat, sharpness and sourness raise it, often showing as acidity, burning, skin flare-ups, loose heat in the gut and a short temper. Since opposites calm a dosha, cooling, calming and slightly drying measures help: cooling foods, sweet-bitter-astringent tastes, avoiding excess sour, salty and pungent food, and staying out of fierce heat. The aim is to take the edge off Pitta's hot, sharp, liquid nature.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Qualities of Kapha and how to settle it","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"Heaviness, sluggishness, congestion, weight gain, slow digestion","guidance":"Kapha carries these qualities: oily (snigdha), cold (sheeta), heavy (guru), slow and dense (manda), smooth (shlakshna), slimy or sticky (mrutsna), and stable or unmoving (sthira). Heavy, cold, oily and sweet things increase it, showing as sluggish digestion, mucus and congestion, weight gain and a dull, attached feeling. The opposite qualities lighten it: warmth, dryness, stimulation and movement. Lighter warm food, the pungent-bitter-astringent tastes, exercise and keeping active all help bring down Kapha's heavy, cold, stable nature.","chapter":1},{"problem":"The seven body tissues (Dhatus)","category":"General","dosha":"—","signs":"For understanding how food becomes body","guidance":"From digested food the body builds seven tissues in order, each feeding the next: Rasa (the first nourishing fluid, broadly like plasma and lymph), Rakta (blood), Mamsa (muscle), Meda (fat), Asthi (bone and cartilage), Majja (marrow), and Shukra (reproductive tissue and its secretions in both men and women). Together with the wastes they are called Dushyas, meaning the things the doshas act on and spoil in disease. Strong, well-formed tissues depend on good digestion, because each tissue is made from the one before it. This explains the Ayurvedic focus on digestion as the root of overall nourishment and strength.","chapter":1},{"problem":"The three wastes (Malas) and why they matter","category":"Detox","dosha":"—","signs":"For understanding regular elimination","guidance":"The body makes three main wastes: Pureesha or Shakrut (faeces), Mootra (urine), and Sweda (sweat). Though called wastes, they each do a job while present, and their regular, easy passage is a sign of health. Problems with any of them point to disturbed doshas or weak digestion. This is the basis for the later teaching that natural urges should never be forced or held back: smooth elimination keeps the inner system clean and balanced.","chapter":1},{"problem":"The six tastes (Rasa) and their effect on the body","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Vata, Pitta, Kapha","signs":"For planning a balanced plate","guidance":"Food acts through six tastes: sweet (Madhura), sour (Amla), salty (Lavana), bitter (Tikta), pungent (Katu) and astringent (Kashaya). In that order they give progressively less nourishing energy, so sweet is the most strengthening and astringent the least. Each taste also moves the doshas. Sweet, sour and salty reduce Vata but build Kapha. Bitter, pungent and astringent reduce Kapha but raise Vata. Astringent, bitter and sweet reduce Pitta, while sour, salty and pungent raise it. A balanced meal uses the tastes to steady whichever dosha is prone to rise, rather than piling on tastes that worsen it.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Potency (Veerya) and post-digestive effect (Vipaka) of foods","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"—","signs":"For understanding whether a food heats or cools you","guidance":"Beyond taste, every food has a potency, either heating (Ushna Veerya, like pepper) or cooling (Sheeta Veerya, like milk). As a rule the astringent, bitter and sweet tastes tend to cool, while sour, salty and pungent tend to heat. After the first stage of digestion the taste changes into a final effect called Vipaka, of three kinds: sweet Vipaka (usually from sweet and salty foods), sour Vipaka (from sour foods), and pungent Vipaka (from bitter, pungent and astringent foods). Choosing foods by their heating or cooling nature, not taste alone, is how Ayurveda matches diet to a person's current state and the season.","chapter":1},{"problem":"The digestive fire (Agni) and its four types","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata, Pitta, Kapha","signs":"Appetite that is irregular, too strong, too weak, or just right","guidance":"Digestion is run by Agni, and its pattern follows the doshas. Vishama Agni (Vata-driven) is irregular, with appetite that swings between strong and weak. Teekshna Agni (Pitta-driven) is sharp, with a fierce appetite and very fast digestion that can leave you hungry again quickly. Manda Agni (Kapha-driven) is dull, with weak appetite and slow, heavy digestion. Sama Agni (from balanced doshas) is the ideal: steady appetite with food digesting fully at the proper time. Knowing your Agni type guides how much and how often to eat, since the goal is to steady digestion toward the balanced Sama state.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Knowing your body type (Prakruti)","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata, Pitta, Kapha","signs":"For understanding your inborn constitution","guidance":"Each person has a fixed constitution set at conception, much as poison is natural to a poisonous creature. Some people run more Vata, some more Pitta, some more Kapha, some are balanced across all three, and some are two-dosha mixes. The text traditionally rates a balanced tridosha type as the most resilient, Kapha-dominant as good, Pitta-dominant as middling and Vata-dominant as more delicate, with two-dosha types seen as less steady. This is about natural tendencies and resilience, not a person's value. Knowing your type helps you anticipate which dosha is most likely to rise in you and adjust food and routine before trouble starts.","chapter":1},{"problem":"The rule of like increases like, opposites reduce","category":"Prevention","dosha":"Vata, Pitta, Kapha","signs":"The single principle behind all Ayurvedic choices","guidance":"The core law of this whole system is that similar qualities increase a thing and opposite qualities reduce it. Dryness is a Vata quality, so dry cold weather raises Vata and dries the skin, while oil, being the opposite of dryness, brings it back down. The twenty qualities of matter come in ten opposite pairs (heavy/light, slow/sharp, cold/hot, oily/dry, smooth/rough, solid/liquid, soft/hard, stable/moving, subtle/gross, non-slimy/slimy). Whenever something is disturbed, you apply its opposite quality. This one rule lets you reason about any food, activity or climate: ask which qualities it adds, and whether those are what you need more of or less of.","chapter":1},{"problem":"What makes you healthy or sick (time, senses, action)","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","signs":"For understanding the root causes of illness","guidance":"Three factors decide health: time (kala), contact between the senses and their objects (artha), and our actions (karma). When our use of these is balanced and appropriate, we stay well. When it is too little, too much, or plain wrong, disease follows. For example, too much sun, too little sleep, or misusing the senses all tip the balance. In short, health is dosha balance and disease is dosha imbalance. The practical lesson is that most illness traces back to how we handle timing, our senses and our daily conduct, which is exactly why Ayurveda puts such weight on daily and seasonal routine.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Caring for the mind (mental doshas and their remedy)","category":"General","dosha":"—","signs":"Agitation and restlessness, or dullness and lethargy","guidance":"The mind has its own two doshas. Rajas drives activity and, in excess, agitation and restlessness. Tamas brings dullness, inaction and lethargy. Just as the body falls ill from imbalance, so does the mind. The text's remedy for mental imbalance is threefold: Dhee (sharpening understanding and discernment), Dhairya (building courage, patience and steadiness), and Atmavijnana (self-knowledge or self-realisation). This is educational, traditional guidance for cultivating a steady mind, and sits alongside, not in place of, modern mental-health care.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Two kinds of treatment: Shamana and Shodhana","category":"Detox","dosha":"Vata, Pitta, Kapha","signs":"For understanding how Ayurveda treats imbalance","guidance":"Treatment comes in two broad forms. Shamana (pacifying) gently brings a disturbed dosha back to balance and is suited to early stages. Shodhana (purification) actively expels the excess dosha from the body and is used when the imbalance is advanced. The classic pairings are: for Vata, Basti (medicated enema) purifies while oil pacifies; for Pitta, Virechana (purgation) purifies while ghee pacifies; for Kapha, Vamana (therapeutic vomiting) purifies while honey pacifies. These purification therapies are serious clinical procedures meant to be done under a trained practitioner, not at home. Educational context only.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Signs of good health and the four aims of life","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","signs":"For understanding why long life and health matter","guidance":"Ayurveda treats a long, healthy life as the foundation that lets a person pursue the four real aims of living: Dharma (living rightly), Artha (earning fairly), Kama (meeting legitimate desires), and Moksha (spiritual freedom). Good health shows up as balanced doshas, steady digestion (Sama Agni), easy regular elimination of the three wastes, well-nourished tissues, restful sleep and a calm, clear mind. Because none of life's aims can be pursued when you are unwell, the text urges putting steady effort into protecting health, and names humility and willingness to learn (Vidheya) as the first quality of anyone who wants to live well.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Waking time and the start of the morning routine","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"—","signs":"For anyone wanting a healthier morning rhythm","guidance":"A healthy person should rise during Brahma Muhurta, the last part of the night before dawn, roughly between 3 am and 6 am and ideally about 45 minutes before sunrise. This quiet, fresh hour is traditionally said to protect life and health and to give the day a calm, clear start. The very first things on waking are to empty the bladder and bowels, and then to clean the teeth, before moving on to the rest of the routine. Building the day around an early, consistent waking time is the foundation that the whole daily regimen rests on.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Cleaning the teeth (Dantadhavana)","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"Daily oral care for a healthy person","guidance":"After relieving yourself, clean the teeth. The traditional method uses a fresh chewing-twig from an astringent, pungent or bitter plant such as Arka (Calotropis), banyan, Khadira (Acacia catechu), Karanja (Pongamia) or Arjuna. The twig should be about as thick as the tip of your little finger and about twelve finger-widths long, with one end chewed soft into bristles. Brush so that the teeth are cleaned without injuring the gums. The astringent-pungent-bitter taste is chosen deliberately because it helps clear the sticky Kapha and coating that build up in the mouth overnight. A modern soft brush achieves the same goal of gentle, thorough daily cleaning.","chapter":2},{"problem":"When to skip vigorous tooth brushing","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"—","signs":"Indigestion, vomiting, cough, breathlessness, fever, mouth ulcers, eye/ear/head trouble, facial paralysis, heart disease","guidance":"Vigorous twig-brushing is to be avoided while you are in certain unwell states, because the strong action can aggravate them. These include indigestion, vomiting, breathlessness, cough, fever, facial paralysis, severe thirst, ulcers in the mouth, heart disease, and disorders of the eyes, head or ears. During such times gentle care is better than forceful brushing. The underlying lesson is to adjust even daily habits to the body's current condition rather than following them mechanically.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Eye care with collyrium (Anjana)","category":"Eyes/Head","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"Daily eye care; eyes prone to Kapha congestion","guidance":"Traditionally a healthy person applies a special antimony-based collyrium called Sauveera Anjana to the eyes each day, because the eyes are seen as fiery in nature and therefore easily troubled by cold, heavy Kapha. Once a week a stronger preparation, Rasanjana (an aqueous extract of Berberis aristata), is used to help drain excess Kapha secretions from the eyes. This is described as part of the classical daily routine and is given here for educational understanding of how the text cares for the eyes; it is not a prescription for self-treatment.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Nasal drops, gargling, smoke and betel after eye care","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"Overnight congestion in the nose, mouth and throat","guidance":"After eye care the routine continues in order: Nasya, instilling drops into the nose using milk, herbal decoction or medicated oil; then Gandusha, holding or gargling warm water, milk, herbal decoction or oil in the mouth; then Dhuma, inhaling medicinal herbal smoke; and finally chewing betel leaf (Tambula). Together these care for the nose, mouth and throat and clear the heavy Kapha that accumulates overnight, leaving the head and senses feeling light and clear. These are classical practices; the everyday takeaway is the value of warm-water gargling and keeping the nasal passages clear each morning.","chapter":2},{"problem":"When to avoid betel leaf chewing (Tambula)","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"—","signs":"Wounds, bleeding problems, dryness, red eyes, poisoning, fainting, intoxication, tuberculosis","guidance":"Chewing betel leaf is part of the classical routine, but the text names clear exceptions where it should be avoided because it can worsen the condition: open wounds, bleeding disorders, excessive dryness of the body, redness of the eyes, poisoning, repeated fainting spells, intoxication, and tuberculosis. As with tooth-cleaning, the principle is to match the practice to the state of the body and leave it out when the body is already strained.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Daily oil massage (Abhyanga)","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Dryness, aches and pains, fatigue, poor sleep, dull skin, early ageing","guidance":"Oil massage done every morning is one of the most valued daily practices. It slows ageing, relieves tiredness and eases the aches and pains that come from excess Vata. It also sharpens eyesight, nourishes the body tissues, lengthens life, brings sound sleep, and improves the tone and texture of the skin. Give special attention to three areas: the head, the ears and the feet. Important exceptions: avoid Abhyanga when Kapha is aggravated (for example during heavy congestion), immediately after a purification therapy like Panchakarma, and during indigestion. Warm oil suited to the season works best, applied unhurriedly before bathing.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Exercise (Vyayama) and how much is right","category":"Energy","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"Low energy, sluggish digestion, excess fat, poor body tone","guidance":"Exercise brings lightness, improves work capacity, strengthens digestion, burns fat and gives the body a firm, well-shaped form. The correct dose is to work only up to about half your capacity (ardha shakti), stopping when effort first begins to show rather than pushing to exhaustion. It is especially suited to the cold season and spring (roughly December to May), and should be kept mild in hotter times. It belongs mainly to those who are strong and eat rich, oily food. People with Vata or Pitta disorders, children, the elderly and anyone with indigestion should not do it. After exercising, the body should be gently massaged all over to settle it. Moderation, matched to season and strength, is the whole principle.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Harm from over-exercise and overexertion","category":"Energy","dosha":"Vata, Pitta","signs":"Exhaustion, breathlessness, wasting, feeling weak even at rest","guidance":"Pushing exercise too hard is actively harmful. It can cause excessive thirst, wasting of the body, severe breathlessness, bleeding disorders, deep exhaustion, a sense of weakness even without any work, cough, fever and vomiting. The text warns that a person who constantly over-exercises, stays up late night after night, walks very long distances, has excessive sex, or laughs and talks to excess, wears themselves out, like a lion that destroys itself even after defeating an elephant. The message is to respect the half-strength limit and to treat rest, sleep and restraint as part of staying strong, not the opposite of it.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Dry powder massage (Udvartana) for Kapha and fat","category":"Detox","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"Heaviness, excess fat, sluggishness, poor skin tone","guidance":"Udvartana is massage with dry herbal powders instead of oil. It calms aggravated Kapha and helps dissolve and burn fat, which is why Ayurvedic centres use it as part of anti-obesity care. It also firms and steadies the limbs and improves the skin's strength and complexion. Where oil massage is soothing and best for Vata dryness, powder massage is stimulating and drying, making it the better choice when the body feels heavy, congested or sluggish with Kapha. It is typically done before bathing.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Bathing (Snana) and the right water temperature","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"—","signs":"For refreshing the body and supporting digestion","guidance":"Bathing strengthens digestion, acts as a tonic for vigour, lengthens life, and lifts enthusiasm and strength. It clears away dirt, sweat, waste, tiredness, excessive thirst, burning sensation and germs. One specific rule matters: warm water poured over the body gives strength, but hot water over the head harms the hair and the eyes, so wash the head with cooler water while the body can take warm. Bathing is best avoided by those with facial paralysis, diseases of the eyes, mouth or ears, diarrhoea, flatulence, a blocked or running nose, or indigestion, and by anyone who has just eaten.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Eating only after the last meal has digested","category":"Digestion","dosha":"—","signs":"Heaviness, bloating, irregular appetite from eating too soon or too much","guidance":"A simple but central rule of the daily routine: eat only after the previous meal has fully digested, and even then eat in a measured, limited amount (Jeerne hitam mitam cha adyat). Eating again before the last meal is digested burdens the digestive fire and breeds imbalance, while overeating overwhelms it. Waiting for genuine hunger, the sign that the earlier food has been processed, and then stopping before you are completely full, keeps digestion steady and the tissues well nourished. This single habit protects Agni, which the text treats as the root of overall health.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Do not suppress or force natural urges","category":"Prevention","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Holding in urine, stool, or other urges; or straining when there is no urge","guidance":"Two paired rules govern the body's natural urges. First, never force out an urge that is not there, such as straining to urinate or pass stool when there is no real need (Na vegan neerayet); forcing disturbs the downward Vata. Second, never hold back an urge that has genuinely arisen, attend to it promptly instead of staying busy with other tasks (Na vegito anya karyaha). Smooth, timely elimination keeps the inner system clean and the doshas, especially Vata, in order. The text adds a related point: seek treatment for any illness as early as possible rather than letting it settle in.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Good conduct of speech (right, kind, measured words)","category":"General","dosha":"—","signs":"For calmer relationships and a steadier mind","guidance":"The text treats speech as part of daily health. Speak at the right occasion (Kale bruyat), speak kind and pleasant words (Hitam bruyat), and speak sparingly, only as much as needed (Mitam bruyat). Avoid arguing and avoid untruth. Be the first to greet and open conversation, keep a pleasant face and good character, and be gentle and courteous in word and action. Do not broadcast insults you have suffered or your grievances against superiors. This disciplined, warm speech is listed among the practices that, over time, bring long life, good reputation and peace of mind.","chapter":2},{"problem":"The ten acts to give up (body, speech, mind)","category":"General","dosha":"—","signs":"For cultivating a clean conscience and steady mind","guidance":"The chapter names ten harmful acts to abandon, grouped by body, speech and mind. Of the body: violence or cruelty (Himsa), stealing (Steya), and wrongful sexual conduct (Anyatha-kama). Of speech: back-biting (Paishunya), harsh words (Parusha), lying (Anruta), and divisive talk that breaks friendships (Sambhinna-alapa). Of the mind: ill-will and the wish to harm (Vyapada), envy of others' good fortune (Abhidya), and perverse, faultfinding views and faithlessness (Drg-viparyaya). Giving these up is presented as mental and moral hygiene, the counterpart to physical hygiene, and part of what keeps a person well and free of inner turmoil.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Kindness, generosity and an even mind","category":"General","dosha":"—","signs":"For emotional steadiness in good times and bad","guidance":"The code of good conduct centres on compassion and balance. Help those who lack a livelihood, who are ill, or who are stricken with grief. Treat even the smallest creatures with respect, and honour physicians, elders and guests. Never turn away or abuse a beggar. Be helpful even to those who do not help you, even to foes. Above all, keep an even mind in both calamity and prosperity (Sampat-vipatsu eka-manaha), and do not envy others' wealth or happiness. The text sums up the essence of right conduct as compassion toward all beings, generosity, control of body, speech and mind, and treating others' interests as your own.","chapter":2},{"problem":"The middle path and care of the senses","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","signs":"For avoiding the harm of extremes","guidance":"A recurring instruction is to keep to the middle course in all things and avoid extremes. The senses should neither be strained and overworked nor pampered and indulged. Stop the activity of body, speech and mind before you reach the point of exhaustion, rather than pushing to depletion. Do not throw yourself into any undertaking empty of the aims of Dharma, Artha and Kama, and never act against Dharma. Deal with each person according to their nature, in the way most likely to please them. This balance, moderate effort, moderate restraint, is itself treated as preventive medicine for both body and mind.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Personal hygiene and grooming","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"—","signs":"Daily cleanliness and grooming","guidance":"Keep yourself clean and well groomed. Trim the hair, nails and moustache regularly. Keep the feet, ears, nose, eyes, urethra and anus clean. Bathe every day, wear light pleasant scents, and dress in clean, neat clothes that are agreeable rather than flashy. When going out, carry an umbrella against sun and weather, wear footwear, and look ahead about four arm-lengths on your path. If urgent work takes you out at night, go equipped with a stick, a head-covering and a companion for safety. This everyday cleanliness and sensible precaution is part of the routine that protects health.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Places, postures and situations to avoid","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","signs":"For avoiding avoidable harm and accidents","guidance":"The chapter lists many sensible cautions. Do not cross rivers by swimming on your arms, walk straight into a large fire, travel in an unsafe boat, climb a tree of doubtful strength, or ride a vehicle in bad condition. Do not sneeze, laugh or yawn without covering the mouth, nor blow the nose or scratch the ground needlessly, nor squat on the heels for long. Do not gaze at the sun for long or keep staring at tiny, dazzling, dirty or unpleasant objects. Avoid harsh draughts, fierce sun, dust, dew and whirlwinds, and awkward postures while sneezing, eating, sleeping or coughing. Avoid the company of wild, horned and biting animals and of mean, cunning people, and avoid quarrels with good people. Do not stay overnight at road junctions, recreation grounds or near burial places. Much of this is practical safety and self-respect dressed as daily discipline.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Timing: what to avoid at dusk and dawn","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"—","signs":"For aligning activities with the right time of day","guidance":"The junctions of the day, the meeting of night and sunrise and of day and dusk, are treated as sensitive times. The text advises against taking food, having sex, sleeping, studying or revising at these junctions. These transitional hours are better kept for quiet, settled activity. The broader teaching is that timing matters as much as the action itself: the same act can help or harm depending on when it is done, which is why the daily routine fixes certain things to certain parts of the day.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Rest and sleep without overstraining","category":"Sleep","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Exhaustion from late nights and overexertion","guidance":"Sleep and rest are protected within the routine. The text warns that staying awake late night after night, like over-exercising or over-indulging, wears the body down and leads to breakdown. It teaches stopping the activity of body, speech and mind before exhaustion sets in, so that rest comes before depletion rather than after collapse. Sound sleep is also listed as one of the benefits of daily oil massage. In short, regular adequate sleep and timely rest are treated as active supports for health, especially for keeping Vata steady, not as time taken away from productive life.","chapter":2},{"problem":"The reward of a disciplined daily routine","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","signs":"For motivation to keep a steady routine","guidance":"The chapter closes with its promise. A person who keeps reflecting on how each day and night is passing, and who steadily chooses the right path, never becomes a victim of sorrow. Whoever follows this routine of good conduct and daily care gains long life, health, wealth, good reputation and lasting well-being. The point is that the many small habits, waking early, caring for the body, moderate exercise, balanced food, timely rest, kind speech and steady conduct, add up over time into a resilient, contented life. Consistency, not any single practice, is what carries the benefit.","chapter":2},{"problem":"What to eat and do in early winter (Hemanta)","category":"Seasonal","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Cold season, roughly mid-November to mid-January; strong appetite, early morning hunger, long nights","guidance":"In early winter the cold traps body heat inside, so your digestive fire runs strong and will start to feed on your own tissues if you underfeed it. Eat heartily and lean on sweet, sour and salty foods: meat soups enriched with fat, dairy and sugarcane preparations, fresh grains, wheat and black-gram dishes. Start the day with warm oil massage (abhyanga) using a Vata-pacifying oil, giving extra attention to the head and forehead, then do gentle exercise to about half your strength, bathe, and warm yourself with sunshine and a fire. Use warm water for washing, dress in warm layers, keep your feet covered, and stay in warm rooms. Intimacy is well supported this season. The theme is nourish, warm and oil the body so cold and dryness cannot take hold.","chapter":3},{"problem":"Late winter care (Shishira)","category":"Seasonal","dosha":"Vata","signs":"The coldest, driest part of the year, roughly mid-January to mid-March; strength still high","guidance":"Late winter follows the same plan as early winter, just applied more firmly, because this is the coldest and driest stretch and it falls inside the sun's drying half of the year. Keep the body warmer, more oiled and better nourished than before. Continue sweet, sour and salty foods and heavier, warming meals, keep up the oil massage, stay in warm surroundings, cover the feet, and avoid cold and dry exposure. Think of it as winter care turned up a notch to match the sharper cold and dryness.","chapter":3},{"problem":"Spring regimen (Vasanta)","category":"Seasonal","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"Spring, roughly mid-March to mid-May; heaviness, sluggish digestion, colds and congestion as the weather warms","guidance":"In spring the Kapha that quietly piled up over winter melts in the warming sun, floods the body, dulls digestion and can bring on illness. Your job is to clear the excess. Favour light, dry, Kapha-reducing foods, such as older barley, wheat and honey, and avoid heavy, cold, oily, sour and sweet foods along with daytime sleep, since all of those feed Kapha. Use dry powder massage and regular exercise. Under proper guidance, gentle Kapha-clearing therapies like therapeutic vomiting (Vamana) and nasal cleansing (Nasya) may help. Bathe and apply light cooling pastes like sandalwood and camphor, and spend pleasant midday hours in fragrant, breezy gardens.","chapter":3},{"problem":"Summer regimen (Greeshma)","category":"Seasonal","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"Summer, roughly mid-May to mid-July; fierce heat, thirst, low energy, tiredness","guidance":"Summer sun is at its harshest, Kapha keeps dropping and Vata climbs, so energy runs low. Eat sweet, light, cool, slightly oily and liquid foods, and cut back on salty, pungent and sour foods, heavy exercise and sun exposure. Cold grain drinks with a little sugar after a cool bath, cold milk, and water kept cool in earthen pots all suit the season. Keep alcohol to a minimum and well diluted, because too much brings swelling, weakness, burning and confusion. By day stay in cool, shaded, breezy places near water and fragrant cooling mists; at night sleep in the open moonlight in light clothing, with sandalwood paste and gentle fanning. It is best to avoid sexual activity in the heat. The whole aim is to stay cool, hydrated and lightly nourished.","chapter":3},{"problem":"Monsoon / rainy-season care (Varsha)","category":"Seasonal","dosha":"combination","signs":"Rainy season, roughly mid-July to mid-September; weak appetite, bloating, all three doshas easily disturbed","guidance":"During the rains your digestive fire is at its weakest, already worn down by summer and further upset by damp, cold winds, dirty rainwater and sourness in the air, which stir up all three doshas at once. Protect and rekindle digestion and keep the doshas settled. Eat old grains, well-spiced meat soups and light dishes that are mildly sour, salty and oily with a touch of honey, and drink only rain or deep-well water that has been well boiled. Stay dry and comfortable: use perfumes, keep clothes pleasantly scented, live in the drier upper part of the house, and travel by vehicle rather than walking through the wet. Avoid river water, heavy chilled drinks, daytime sleep, overexertion and sun exposure. Under proper guidance, cleansing therapies and a decoction enema (Asthapana Basti) may be used to steady the doshas.","chapter":3},{"problem":"Autumn regimen (Sharath)","category":"Seasonal","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"Autumn, roughly mid-September to mid-November; returning heat after the rains, acidity, skin and heat-related flare-ups","guidance":"In autumn the body has grown used to the cool rains and then meets the sun's returning heat, so the Pitta that built up during the monsoon flares. Settle it with bitter, sweet and astringent tastes in light, easy meals, such as rice, green gram, amla and gentle meats. A classic seasonal drink is Hamsodaka, water warmed by the day's sun and cooled by the night's moon over many clear nights, considered pure and balancing and neither clogging nor drying. Spend cool evenings in the moonlight with a light sandalwood paste. Avoid rich curds, oils and fats, strong liquor, overeating, harsh sun, daytime sleep and the eastern breeze. Under guidance, purgation and bitter medicated ghee can help clear excess Pitta.","chapter":3},{"problem":"Matching tastes and food qualities to the season","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","signs":"Anyone wanting a simple rule of thumb for seasonal eating","guidance":"A quick seasonal taste guide: emphasise sweet, sour and salty tastes in the two winters and the rains; bitter, pungent and astringent in spring; mostly sweet in summer; and sweet, bitter and astringent in autumn. For the quality of food, keep it dry and low in fat during autumn and spring, cool during summer and the tail end of the rains, and warm in the other seasons. The ideal is to include all six tastes every single day for steady health, and simply give extra weight to the tastes the current season calls for rather than dropping any taste entirely.","chapter":3},{"problem":"Why the body weakens in some seasons: Adana, Visarga and digestive fire","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"combination","signs":"Anyone noticing their energy and digestion shift with the time of year","guidance":"The year has two halves that explain much of how you feel. In the sun's northern, drying course (Adana Kala, covering late winter, spring and summer) the sun and wind steadily pull moisture and strength out of the earth and the body, so energy tends to run lower and the harsher tastes dominate. In the southern, moistening course (Visarga Kala, covering the rains, autumn and early winter) rain, cloud and cool winds restore moisture, so strength tends to be higher and the gentler building tastes dominate. Your digestive fire tracks this too: strongest in the cold seasons, weakest in summer and the rains. Practically, eat heavier and nourish more when strength and digestion are high in winter, and eat lighter, cooler and simpler when they are low in summer and the monsoon.","chapter":3},{"problem":"Easing between seasons (Ritu-sandhi transition weeks)","category":"Seasonal","dosha":"combination","signs":"The last week of one season and the first week of the next, when weather is changing over","guidance":"The final seven days of one season and the opening seven days of the next form a transition seam. During these two weeks, wind down the routine of the season that is ending bit by bit and bring in the new season's routine gradually, rather than switching everything overnight. The body needs time to adapt, and a sudden change, dropping old habits abruptly or jumping straight into new foods and routines, creates a mismatch (asatmya) that can itself make you unwell. Go slow and let your diet, clothing and activity shift step by step as the weather turns.","chapter":3},{"problem":"Natural urges you must never suppress","category":"Prevention","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Everyday life, whenever the body signals a natural need; especially relevant to people who routinely hold back due to work or habit","guidance":"There are thirteen natural urges the body should be allowed to release and never force back: passing gas, stool and urine, sneezing, thirst, hunger, sleep, cough, the heavy breathing after exertion, yawning, tears, vomiting, and ejaculation. Each one is the body clearing or regulating something, and blocking it pushes Vata, the force that governs downward flow, out of its normal path, which is why suppression tends to cause bloating, pain, headaches, constipation, breathing and heart complaints and general discomfort. The practical rule is simple: attend to these needs when they arise rather than holding them in. When problems have already developed from holding back gas, stool or urine, supportive measures include oil massage, warm sitz or tub baths, sweating therapy, enema and foods that gently loosen the bowels; thirst is eased with cooling drinks, hunger with a light warm meal, lost sleep with rest and massage, and so on, each matched to the urge that was blocked.","chapter":4},{"problem":"Harm from holding in gas, stool and urine (and how it is managed)","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Habitually delaying the toilet or holding back gas; bloating, abdominal pain, constipation, difficulty urinating","guidance":"When you hold back gas, Vata that should move downward is forced upward, bringing abdominal bloating and distension, pain, tiredness, constipation, trouble passing urine, dimmed vision and weakened digestion. Holding back stool can add cramping calf pain, runny nose, headache, a tight heavy chest and foul breath, while holding back urine can bring body pain, urinary stones and pain in the bladder and genital area. The guidance is to never postpone these needs. If complaints have set in, helpful measures are oil massage (abhyanga), warm tub or sitz baths, sweating therapy, suppositories, enema (basti), and foods that ease the bowels. For urine specifically, taking ghee before meals and a larger dose once the earlier meal has digested (a practice called Avapidaka Sneha) is advised. The common thread is restoring Vata's natural downward movement.","chapter":4},{"problem":"Suppressed sneeze, yawn, belch, cough or heavy breathing","category":"Prevention","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Stifling a sneeze, yawn, belch or cough, or holding your breath after exertion; headache, neck stiffness, chest tightness, worsening cough","guidance":"Stifling a sneeze can bring headache, weak senses, neck stiffness and facial palsy; it is relieved by measures that let the sneeze come out and settle Vata, such as inhaling medicated smoke, nasal drops, briefly looking toward bright light, and massage with oleation and sweating. Holding back a yawn causes similar trouble and needs the same Vata-balancing care. Suppressing a belch can cause loss of appetite, tremors, constipation, chest tightness, bloating, cough and hiccups. Holding back a cough only worsens it and can lead to breathing difficulty, poor appetite, heart trouble and wasting, so it should be allowed and treated with normal cough care. Suppressing the heavy breathing after hard work can cause abdominal masses, heart trouble and fainting, and is best handled with simple rest and Vata-settling measures. The overall lesson is to let these reflexes happen and to calm Vata rather than fight the body.","chapter":4},{"problem":"Suppressing thirst, hunger or sleep","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Skipping meals, ignoring thirst, or pushing through tiredness; weakness, weight loss, dizziness, heaviness of head","guidance":"Ignoring thirst can lead to dryness and wasting, weakness, deafness, fainting, confusion and heart trouble, so respond with cooling measures like cool drinks and a cool bath. Ignoring hunger can cause body ache, loss of appetite, weakness, weight loss, colicky pain and dizziness; the right response is a light, warm, lightly oiled meal, not a heavy one, because fasting lowers digestive power and raises Vata and a big meal afterward weakens digestion further, while warm oily food soothes the stomach and keeps the digestive fire steady. Ignoring sleep brings confusion, a heavy head and eyes, laziness, yawning and body ache, and is set right by rest and gentle massage. The practical takeaway is to eat, drink and rest when your body asks, and to answer each need in a mild, measured way rather than overcorrecting.","chapter":4},{"problem":"Suppressing tears, vomiting or ejaculation","category":"Prevention","dosha":"combination","signs":"Forcibly holding back weeping, the urge to vomit, or ejaculation","guidance":"Holding back tears can cause a blocked or running nose, eye disorders, painful heart conditions, neck stiffness, poor appetite, dizziness and abdominal masses; gentle comfort such as rest, sleep and warm friendly conversation helps. Holding back vomiting mainly disturbs Pitta and can bring spreading skin diseases, rashes and other skin disorders, eye problems, itching, anaemia, fever, cough, breathing difficulty, nausea and facial pigmentation; care focuses on clearing Pitta through gargling, medicated smoke, eating dry food and then inducing vomiting, exercise, bloodletting for skin issues, and purgation. Holding back ejaculation can cause dribbling of semen, groin pain, swelling, fever, cardiac distress, blocked urine, body ache, hernia, stone formation and impotence; supportive care includes nourishing foods and rice, massage and immersion baths of the lower abdomen, bladder cleansing, medicated-milk treatment, and normal marital relations. In each case the point is not to fight these natural releases.","chapter":4},{"problem":"Urges you should hold back (greed, envy, anger)","category":"General","dosha":"—","signs":"Anyone wanting steadier mental and emotional health","guidance":"While the body's physical urges should be allowed to flow, the mental and moral urges are the ones to deliberately rein in. Hold back greed, envy, hatred, jealousy, and unlawful or excessive craving and attraction. Mastering the senses and restraining these impulses is treated as a genuine part of staying well, not just good manners, because unchecked they drive wrong actions that lead to suffering and illness. In everyday terms, let the body do what it naturally needs, but govern the mind with self-control, patience and an even temper.","chapter":4},{"problem":"Clearing doshas at the right time (Shodhana / Panchakarma) and recovering after","category":"Detox","dosha":"combination","signs":"Seasonal dosha buildup; feeling drained or depleted after cleansing therapy","guidance":"Waste and doshas should be cleared at their proper seasons before they accumulate, because stored toxins can aggravate and, at worst, become dangerous. There is a key difference between approaches: doshas only pacified by fasting and digestive therapies can flare up again later, while doshas truly expelled by purification therapy (Shodhana, the Panchakarma procedures) tend not to return. A good seasonal rhythm is to expel what builds in winter during spring, what builds in summer during the rains, and what builds in the rains during autumn. After proper cleansing, rebuild gradually with nourishing foods like fast-maturing rice, wheat, green gram, meat and ghee, plus medicines that support the heart and kindle digestion, along with oil massage, powder massage, herbal baths and enemas. Done well, this restores health, ease, clear thinking, good vision, strong senses, vigour and longevity. These intensive therapies are not for everyone: those in severe pain and thirst, the severely weakened, habitual urge-suppressors, and very advanced cases are not suitable candidates.","chapter":4},{"problem":"Everyday conduct that keeps you free of disease","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","signs":"Anyone wanting a simple long-term plan for staying healthy and avoiding both internal and external illness","guidance":"The secret of lasting health is a steady, sensible daily life. Eat wholesome food and keep wholesome routines every day, weigh the good and bad of things before acting, and do not become overly attached to sensory pleasures. Control the senses, learn from past experience, and understand your environment, the season and your own nature so you can adapt. Follow good conduct and healthy habits, keep a calm and peaceful mind, practise charity, honesty, patience and fairness, and keep good company. Clear the doshas at their proper seasons so seasonal illness never takes hold. Remember too that some illnesses come from outside, from microbes and unseen agents, poisons, violent weather, fire, injuries, fractures, and even strong emotions like desire, hatred and fear, so care for both inner balance and outer safety. This is educational guidance for healthy living, not a promise of cure.","chapter":4},{"problem":"Rainwater — the best water and how to judge it","category":"Drinks","dosha":"combination","signs":"general drinking water, best in the rainy season","guidance":"Fresh rainwater caught in a clean vessel is treated as the finest of all waters. Having met sunlight, moonlight and wind, it is light to digest, thin, cooling, almost tasteless and soothing to the heart and mind. A traditional quality test is to pour it over boiled rice on a clean silver plate; good water neither makes the rice soggy nor changes its colour. Drink it fresh, as it loses its virtues on standing, and remember its goodness still depends on the season and place where it fell. In the rains it is the best drink, better than river water. In modern conditions, pollution has taken away much of rainwater's natural purity, so judgement is needed.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Water to avoid — contaminated and unsafe water","category":"Prevention","dosha":"combination","signs":"stagnant, dirty or out-of-season water","guidance":"Refuse water that is muddy or full of algae, weeds and fallen leaves, water shut away from sun and wind, a mixture of stale and fresh, water that is thick and hard to digest, frothy, wormy, naturally warm, or so icy it sets the teeth on edge. Out-of-season rain, the very first rains, and any water fouled by spiders, saliva, urine or droppings are all unsafe. Stagnant river or pond water that has lost its movement is said to breed intestinal worms and to foster diseases of the heart, throat and head, so moving, clean, sun-and-wind-exposed water is always preferred.","chapter":5},{"problem":"River and well water — how the source changes the water","category":"Drinks","dosha":"combination","signs":"choosing drinking water by its source","guidance":"Water takes on the character of the land it flows through. Sweet, clean rivers running west into the Arabian Sea are considered wholesome, while east-flowing rivers are less suitable. Mountain streams that churn and tumble over rocks stay healthy, but once they stagnate they can cause worms and swellings. Water from different hill ranges carries different faults, ranging from piles and belly swelling to skin disease and anaemia, and sea water disturbs all three doshas except in the autumn month when it is briefly considered safe. Well and pond water simply mirrors the surrounding terrain, so the setting matters as much as the source.","chapter":5},{"problem":"How much water to drink and when","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","signs":"weak digestion, swelling, diarrhoea, piles, anaemia","guidance":"People with weak digestion, abdominal tumours, anaemia, a swollen belly, diarrhoea, piles, duodenal disease or dropsy should drink very little plain water or none. Even healthy people are advised to keep water modest in every season except summer and autumn. Around meals the timing matters: sipping a little during the meal is the balanced habit, drinking heavily after a meal tends to add weight, and drinking a lot before a meal tends to thin the body and weaken it. The practical message is to drink with the meal in small amounts rather than loading up before or after.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Cold water — when it helps","category":"Drinks","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"hangover, fainting, burning heat, thirst, bleeding","guidance":"Cold water is soothing in heat-driven and Pitta states. It settles the morning-after feeling of alcohol, relieves exhaustion, fainting, vomiting, fatigue, giddiness and strong thirst, calms burning sensations and sun heat, helps bleeding tendencies and Pitta-dominant conditions, and is used to counter the effects of poison. It is the natural choice when the body feels overheated or overwrought.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Warm and boiled water — the digestive aid","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","signs":"sluggish digestion, cold, cough, bloating, Vata-Kapha","guidance":"Hot water kindles appetite, helps digestion, is kind to the throat, stays light, and cleanses the bladder. It relieves hiccup, bloating, aggravated Vata and Kapha, recent fever, cough, a build-up of undigested residue, running nose, breathlessness and pain in the flanks, and it suits the days of cleansing therapy. Water that is boiled and then cooled does not clog the tissue channels and remains light, which makes it a good choice in Pitta conditions. Water left standing overnight is best avoided, as it is said to disturb all three doshas.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Tender coconut water","category":"Drinks","dosha":"combination","signs":"thirst, heat, Pitta and Vata imbalance","guidance":"Tender coconut water is sweet, lightly oily, cooling, light to digest and mildly strengthening. It quenches thirst, calms both Pitta and Vata, gently kindles appetite and helps clear the urinary bladder. It is a gentle, refreshing drink, well suited to hot weather and to conditions where the body runs warm.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Milk — general qualities and who it suits","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","signs":"depletion, weakness, Vata-Pitta states","guidance":"Milk as a class is sweet both on the tongue and after digestion, oily, cooling and heavy. It nourishes all the body tissues, builds vital essence (ojas), is mildly aphrodisiac, and calms Vata and Pitta while increasing Kapha. This makes it restorative for the weak, the depleted and those with dryness or heat, but less suited to people who are already heavy, congested or slow in digestion. Milk is always better boiled, since raw milk tends to clog the channels and cause undigested residue.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Cow's milk","category":"Energy","dosha":"combination","signs":"exhaustion, chest injury, chronic fever, low vitality","guidance":"Cow's milk is the most prized milk. It promotes long life, rejuvenates and slows ageing, sharpens the intellect, strengthens the body, helps breast-milk flow and eases the bowels. It is used for exhaustion, giddiness, intoxication, breathlessness and cough, severe hunger and thirst, long-standing fever and painful urination, is good for someone emaciated after a chest injury, and helps calm bleeding disorders. Boiled and taken warm, it is a broadly restorative daily nourishment for depleted people.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Buffalo, goat and camel milk — which milk for whom","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","signs":"strong digestion and insomnia; wasting and fever; worms and swelling","guidance":"Different milks suit different people. Buffalo milk is heavier and cooling and suits a person with very strong digestion who sleeps poorly. Goat milk is light to digest, because the goat roams and eats sharp, light fodder, and it helps wasting, fever, breathlessness, Pitta-type bleeding and diarrhoea. Camel milk is slightly dry, warm, salty and light, kindles digestion and is useful in Vata and Kapha states, worms, swelling, belly disease and piles. Ewe milk is warm, hard on the heart and raises Pitta and Kapha, and milk of single-hoofed animals like the horse is very hot and dulling, so both are used sparingly.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Curd (Dadhi) — benefits and strict rules","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","signs":"poor appetite, recurrent fever, diarrhoea, malabsorption","guidance":"Curd is sour in taste and after digestion, absorbent, heavy and warming, and it calms Vata. It increases fat, semen, strength, Kapha, bleeding tendency, digestive fire and swelling, and whets the appetite, so it helps loss of appetite, recurrent fever, running nose, painful urination and malabsorption. But the rules around it are firm: do not eat curd at night, do not heat it, do not take it with green-gram soup, and never combine it with honey, ghee, sugar or sour fruit. It should also not be eaten every single day, because daily use can bring on or worsen fever, bleeding disorders, skin disease, anaemia and dizziness.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Buttermilk (Takra) for digestion","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"poor appetite, loose motions, piles, bloating, after too much ghee","guidance":"Buttermilk is curd that has been churned with the fat removed. It is light, sour and astringent, kindles digestion and calms both Kapha and Vata. It is a go-to for swelling, belly disease, piles, malabsorption and loose motions, urinary trouble, poor appetite, enlarged spleen, abdominal lumps, the heaviness that follows eating too much ghee, chronic low-grade poisoning and anaemia. Being light and digestive, it is one of the most useful everyday drinks for a sluggish gut. The watery whey (mastu) left over is similar: it keeps fluids moving through the channels, cleanses them, relieves constipation and is light.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Fresh butter (Navanita)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","signs":"wasting, loose motions, cough, bleeding, dull complexion","guidance":"Fresh butter is aphrodisiac and cooling, improves complexion, strength and digestive power, is absorbent so it helps diarrhoea, and balances Vata, Pitta and the blood. It is used for wasting conditions, piles, facial palsy and cough. Butter churned directly from milk, rather than from curd, is particularly absorbent and useful in bleeding disorders and eye diseases. It is a gentler, cooling fat compared with the deeper action of ghee.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Ghee (clarified butter) — the best of fats","category":"Energy","dosha":"combination","signs":"low intellect and memory, Vata-Pitta disease, wasting, poor eyesight","guidance":"Ghee is praised as the finest of all fatty substances. It improves intellect, memory, digestive power, longevity, vigour and eyesight, and suits children, the elderly, the delicate and those who wish for children. It helps disorders of Vata and Pitta origin, poison, insanity, wasting and fever, and is cooling and youth-preserving. A key idea is that ghee takes on and carries the power of whatever herbs it is cooked with, so it becomes the base for countless medicinal preparations. Old ghee is reserved for intoxication, epilepsy, fainting and diseases of the head, ear, eye and genitals, and it both cleanses and heals wounds.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Thick milk products — kilata, piyusha, kurchika","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"need for building up, but prone to heaviness","guidance":"Concentrated dairy such as kilata (the solid from heated curd or buttermilk), piyusha (the first milk for about three days after calving), kurchika and morana are strengthening and increase semen, sleep and Kapha. The trade-off is that they cause constipation, are heavy to digest and tend to disturb the doshas, so they are rich building foods rather than everyday ones. Overall, cow's milk and cow's ghee are rated the best dairy, while ewe's milk and ghee are rated the least.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Sugarcane juice and its products","category":"Energy","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"weakness, bleeding tendency, low body weight","guidance":"Fresh sugarcane juice is laxative, heavy, oily, nourishing and weight-building, cooling, mildly aphrodisiac, increases urine and Kapha, and helps bleeding disorders. For best quality it should be chewed or pressed cleanly, because juice crushed together with the root, shoot tip and wormy parts becomes impure and then causes burning, indigestion and constipation. Among its solid products, well-purified jaggery does not raise Kapha much and increases stool and urine, old jaggery is good for the heart, and fresh jaggery raises Kapha and causes indigestion. Refined sugars improve in quality from brown sugar to sugar candy to white crystal sugar, and all sugars soothe burning, thirst, vomiting, fainting and bleeding.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Honey (Madhu) — benefits and the rule never to heat it","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"congestion, cough, wounds, bleeding, excess Kapha","guidance":"Honey is good for the eyes, scrapes and breaks up hardened masses and excess Kapha, relieves thirst and calms Kapha. It helps poison, hiccup, bleeding, diabetes and urinary disease, skin disease, worms, vomiting, breathlessness, cough and diarrhoea, and it cleanses wounds and helps them close and heal quickly. It is drying and astringent-sweet and slightly raises Vata. The famous and strict rule is never to heat honey: it is said to turn harmful when heated, when taken by someone who is very overheated, in hot weather or a hot climate, or mixed into hot food. The one exception is using it warm to induce vomiting or in a decoction enema, because then it leaves the body before it is digested.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Sesame oil and other oils","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Vata","signs":"dry skin, constipation, worms, Vata states, both thin and obese","guidance":"An oil generally carries the nature of the seed it comes from, and sesame oil is considered the chief of all oils. It cleanses and detoxifies the skin, is good for the eyes, penetrates deep into the tissues, is warm and reduces Kapha, and uniquely suits both the obese and the thin. It relieves constipation and worms, and when processed with herbs it becomes a base for treating many diseases. Other oils have their own uses: mustard oil is pungent, hot and penetrating and reduces Kapha but can irritate; neem oil is bitter, mildly hot and antimicrobial for skin disease; vibhitaka oil is sweet, cooling and good for hair; and linseed and safflower oils are hot and tend to aggravate Kapha and Pitta.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Castor oil (Eranda taila)","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","signs":"constipation, abdominal lumps, hernia, Vata-Kapha pain and swelling","guidance":"Castor oil is bitter, pungent and sweet in taste, laxative, heavy, deeply penetrating, hot and strong-smelling. It is a classic Vata remedy, used to produce purgation, ease pain and reduce swelling. It helps hernia and scrotal enlargement, abdominal lumps, Vata-Kapha diseases, ascites and intermittent fever, and relieves pain and swelling of the waist, genitals, abdomen and back. The red-seed variety of castor oil is even more penetrating and heating. Because it works deeply on Vata, it is valued across many conditions but used with care given its strength.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Alcoholic drinks (Madya) — general qualities and cautions","category":"General","dosha":"combination","signs":"used judiciously it helps; misused it harms","guidance":"As a group, wines kindle digestion, improve taste, penetrate deeply and are hot, give satisfaction and nourishment, and taste slightly sweet, bitter and pungent, turning sour after digestion. They are laxative and slightly astringent, said to improve voice, health, talent and complexion, are light, and suit both the over-sleeping and the sleepless and both lean and stout people, clearing the fine channels and calming Vata and Kapha. All of this holds only when they are used wisely; used wrongly they act like poison. Fresh wine is heavy and raises all the doshas, while aged wine reduces them. Wine should not be taken hot, nor during purgation therapy, nor on an empty stomach, and anything very strong, very weak, very clear, very cloudy or spoilt should be avoided.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Types of wine — sura, arishta, grape and sugarcane wines","category":"General","dosha":"combination","signs":"various digestive and tissue conditions","guidance":"Each fermented drink has its own leaning. Sura (a rice beer) helps abdominal lumps, swollen belly, piles and duodenal disease, is lubricating and heavy and increases fat, blood, milk, urine and Kapha. Arishta, a fermented herbal decoction, is the strongest and carries the power of its herbs, treating duodenal disease, anaemia, skin disease, piles, dropsy, fever, lumps, worms and spleen disorder. Grape wine (mardvika) is heart-friendly, sweet, laxative and helps anaemia, diabetes, piles and worms. Sidhu from sugarcane raises Vata and Pitta but reduces Kapha disease, fat and dropsy. Honey wine (madhvasava) breaks up masses and helps diabetes, nasal catarrh and cough. Date wine is the weakest of these and raises Vata.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Sour fermented grain liquids (Dhanyamla, Sauviraka)","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","signs":"poor appetite, fatigue, bladder pain, Vata-Kapha","guidance":"Dhanyamla is made by fermenting the water in which grains and pulses were washed or lightly cooked. It is purgative, penetrating and hot, raises Pitta, feels cool to the touch, relieves fatigue and exhaustion, sharpens appetite, eases bladder pain, is heart-friendly and light, and balances Vata and Kapha. It is also used as a medicated enema fluid. Sauviraka and tushodaka, prepared from barley with and without husk, are similar and help worms, heart disease, abdominal lumps, piles and anaemia. These sour liquids were common digestive tonics in older times.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Rice — qualities and which variety is best","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","signs":"daily staple, thirst, dosha balance","guidance":"Rice as a food is sweet in taste and after digestion, oily, mildly aphrodisiac, light to digest, cooling, slightly astringent, increases urine and causes only a little stool, which makes it suitable for daily eating. Among the many kinds, red rice (Raktashali) is considered the best because it quenches thirst and balances all three doshas; big-grained rice comes next, then kalama, and so on down the traditional list. Rice that ripens in sixty days (Shashtika) is especially valued as oily, absorbent, light, steadying, cooling and tridosha-balancing. Late or inferior varieties tend to be hot, heavy and sour after digestion and raise Kapha and Pitta.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Millets and grass grains (kangu, kodrava, shyamaka)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"need for light, drying grains; Kapha-Pitta states","guidance":"Grains from grass-like plants such as kangu, kodrava, neevara and shyamaka are cooling, light to digest and scraping in action; they raise Vata somewhat but settle Kapha and Pitta, which makes them useful light staples for heavy or congested conditions. Among them priyangu helps knit fractures and is nourishing though heavy, while koradusha is strongly absorbent, cooling and regarded as anti-poisonous. Because they are drying and Vata-raising, they suit Kapha types better than those who are already dry or windy.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Barley (Yava)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"urinary disorders, cough, asthma, excess Kapha and fat","guidance":"Barley is dry, cooling and heavy, sweet in taste, laxative, and it increases the bulk of stool and flatus while being mildly aphrodisiac and steadying for the body. It reduces fat and helps urinary disorders, Pitta-Kapha imbalances, running nose, breathlessness and asthma, cough, stiffness of the thighs, throat disease and skin disease. Its drying, reducing nature makes it a classic grain for Kapha-heavy and overweight conditions, though its heaviness and dryness mean Vata-prone people should take it prepared with care.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Wheat (Godhuma)","category":"Energy","dosha":"Vata","signs":"depletion, dryness, fractures and wounds, Vata-Pitta states","guidance":"Wheat is mildly aphrodisiac, cooling, heavy, oily and enlivening. It calms both Vata and Pitta, helps knit fractures and heal wounds, is sweet, steadies the body and gently moves the bowels, and is considered fit for daily eating. Being nourishing and grounding, it suits dry, depleted and Vata-type constitutions, while its heaviness means it is less ideal for those with sluggish digestion or heavy Kapha.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Pulses and legumes — general qualities","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"need to reduce fat and Kapha; tendency to constipation and gas","guidance":"Green gram, toor dal, lentils and their relatives share a common nature: they tend to constipate and produce gas, are astringent-sweet in taste, absorbent, pungent after digestion, cooling and light to digest. As a group they reduce fat, Kapha, blood and Pitta, and are even used as external pastes and in bathing. This makes them good for Kapha and Pitta reduction, but their drying, gas-forming tendency means Vata-prone people should cook them well with spices and fats. Fresh-harvested grain clogs the channels; grain a year old, dehusked or properly fried is lighter and easier to digest.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Green gram, black gram and other specific pulses","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","signs":"choosing the right dal for the constitution","guidance":"Among the pulses, green gram (mudga) is the best and raises Vata only slightly, which is why it is the standard dal for the sick and the recovering. Round pea (kalaya) raises Vata and dryness and makes more stool, and rajamasha is dry, heavy and Vata-raising. Black gram (masha) is the opposite extreme: oily, strengthening, heavy, hot, sweet, laxative, it calms Vata and strongly builds semen and sexual strength, but it raises Kapha and Pitta and increases stool, so it is a building food for the depleted rather than an everyday one. Horse gram is hot and reducing. The fruits of kakandola and atmagupta (kapikacchu) resemble black gram in action.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Horse gram (Kulattha)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"urinary stones, cough, asthma, Kapha-Vata; avoid in bleeding","guidance":"Horse gram is hot in potency and sour after digestion. It cleanses semen and is used for urinary stones, breathlessness and asthma, running nose, cough and Kapha-Vata diseases, and its soup specifically normalises the downward movement of Vata and helps abdominal lumps and pains of the groin region. The important caution is that it increases bleeding disorders, so it should be avoided in conditions like heavy menstrual bleeding or any Pitta-type haemorrhage.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Sesame seed (Tila)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Vata","signs":"weak hair and skin, low strength, Vata depletion","guidance":"Sesame is hot in potency yet feels cool to the touch, is good for the skin and hair, strengthening and heavy, gives little urine, turns pungent after digestion, sharpens intellect and digestive fire, and raises Kapha and Pitta. It is a building, grounding seed well suited to Vata depletion and to nourishing the hair and skin, but its heaviness and heating action mean it should be used in moderation by Pitta and Kapha types. Linseed (uma) and safflower seeds are similar, oily and hot, but are noted as not good for vision or semen.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Rice preparations — manda, peya, vilepi, odana","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","signs":"recovery, after cleansing therapy, weak digestion, fever","guidance":"Rice cooked with water passes through four stages of increasing thickness and heaviness: manda is the thin water-like liquid drained off first, peya is a little thicker but still liquid, vilepi has more grain and less liquid, and odana is solid cooked rice. They get heavier in that order, so manda is lightest and best for the most fragile digestion. Manda moves stool and flatus, relieves thirst and exhaustion, clears leftover doshas and is the ideal food right after cleansing therapy. Peya relieves hunger, thirst, weakness, belly disease and fever and rekindles appetite. Vilepi holds in fluids, is heart-friendly and suits ulcers, eye disease, the weak and those given oils before therapy. These thin gruels are the backbone of convalescent diet.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Soups — meat soup, green-gram soup, horse-gram soup","category":"Energy","dosha":"combination","signs":"convalescence, wounds, throat and eye disease, Vata trouble","guidance":"Meat soup (mamsa rasa) is nourishing, satisfying, aphrodisiac, good for the eyes and helps heal ulcers and wounds, making it a strong restorative broth for the depleted. Green-gram soup is light and wholesome, especially good for those recovering from cleansing therapy and for ulcers and diseases of the throat and eyes. Horse-gram soup normalises the downward movement of Vata and is used for abdominal lumps and pains of the groin. Light soups like these are a gentle way to nourish and medicate at the same time.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Processed and fried foods, and sprouted grains — what to limit","category":"Prevention","dosha":"combination","signs":"heaviness, poor digestion, weak eyes","guidance":"Several processed items are flagged as hard to digest and dosha-aggravating, including sesame cake, dried leafy vegetables, germinated or sprouted grains and certain fried dough balls; sprouted grains in particular are not recommended because they are heavy, harm the eyes and disturb the doshas. Oil-cake residue (pinyaka) causes giddiness, dryness and indigestion and dulls vision. Roasted corn flour (saktu) is nourishing and strengthening but has strict rules: take it with water, not at night, not twice a day, not on its own without other food, not after a meal, and never in a hard-to-chew or excessive amount. As a general cooking note, food baked directly over coal digests more easily than steamed food.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Flaked and puffed rice (laja, prithuka) and roasted flour","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"thirst, vomiting, diarrhoea, diabetes, obesity","guidance":"Puffed or parched paddy (laja) relieves thirst, vomiting and diarrhoea, helps diabetes and obesity, reduces Kapha, cough and Pitta, kindles appetite, is light to digest and cooling, which makes it a useful light food in heat and congestion. Flaked paddy (prithuka), made by boiling and pounding paddy, is the opposite: heavy to digest, strengthening, Kapha-increasing and constipating. Roasted barley flour (dhana) stays long in the stomach and can cause indigestion, is dry, satisfying and scraping. These forms differ sharply, so the preparation matters as much as the grain.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Jangala (dry-land) meats — the lightest, healthiest meats","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","signs":"convalescence, mixed-dosha fever, need for light protein","guidance":"Meats are grouped by habitat, and the dry-land (jangala) group, which includes deer, rabbit and the ground-scratching and pecking birds, is considered the best. These meats are cooling in potency, light to digest and mildly constipating, and they are especially good in mixed-dosha fevers where Pitta is high. Rabbit (shasha) in particular enhances appetite and is cooling. Because they are the lightest meats, they are the preferred animal foods for the sick, the recovering and anyone needing easily digested protein.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Poultry and game birds — partridge, peacock, fowl","category":"Energy","dosha":"Vata","signs":"low strength, poor intellect, weak digestion and semen","guidance":"The quail and partridge group are building meats: slightly hot, heavy, oily and body-strengthening. Partridge (tittiri) is rated especially good for increasing intelligence, digestive power, strength and semen, improving complexion and calming Vata-dominant mixed-dosha states. Peacock, though generally not rated highly, is said to benefit the ears, voice, eyes and healthy ageing. Wild fowl is aphrodisiac, whereas domesticated fowl is heavier and raises Kapha. Black partridge (krakara) sharpens intellect and digestion and is good for the heart. These are nourishing meats for depletion rather than light everyday ones.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Goat, sheep, cow, buffalo and pig meat compared","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","signs":"choosing meat by its heaviness and dosha effect","guidance":"Goat meat is the balanced choice: not too cooling, matching the make-up of the human body, so it does not clog the channels or aggravate the doshas, and it nourishes and adds healthy weight. Sheep meat is the opposite in nature and simply fattening. Cow meat is described as treating dry cough, exhaustion, excess hunger, intermittent fever, chronic nasal catarrh and emaciation from independent Vata increase. Buffalo meat is hot, heavy, and induces sleep, strength and bulk. Pig relieves fatigue and builds taste, semen and strength. Fish generally increase Kapha, and some kinds disturb all three doshas. The overall rule is to use freshly killed, clean meat of adult animals and to reject meat of dead, diseased, drowned, poisoned, very thin or very fatty animals.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Leafy greens — general rule and the best and worst","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","signs":"choosing vegetables by heaviness and dosha","guidance":"Among plant parts, leaves are lightest, then flowers, then unripe fruit, then stalks, then tubers, which are heaviest. Most leafy greens, especially the bitter ones, are cooling, pungent after digestion, hold in body fluids, raise Vata and reduce Kapha and Pitta. Jivanti is rated the best of the leafy vegetables, being good for the eyes, balancing to all doshas, sweet and cooling, while mustard greens (sarshapa) are rated the worst because they bind stool and urine and raise all three doshas. Cooking greens with oil or ghee and taking out their juice reduces their tendency to aggravate the doshas.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Bitter and medicinal greens (patola, vasa, bitter gourd, kakamachi)","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"worms, cough, bleeding, skin disease, poor digestion","guidance":"The bitter greens are cooling and reducing. Patola (pointed gourd) is good for the heart, anti-worm, sweet after digestion and improves taste. Vasa cures vomiting, cough and especially bleeding disorders. Bitter gourd (karavella) is bitter, kindles digestion and strongly reduces Kapha and Pitta. Black nightshade (kakamachi) balances the three doshas, helps skin disease, is mildly aphrodisiac and rejuvenating and moves the bowels. Wood sorrel (changeri) is sour, kindles digestion and helps duodenal disease and piles. These greens are chosen for Kapha and Pitta reduction, digestion and worm and skin problems.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Brinjal (eggplant)","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"sluggish digestion, Kapha-Vata, poor appetite","guidance":"Brinjal is pungent, bitter and slightly sweet, hot in potency and mildly alkaline. It reduces both Kapha and Vata, kindles the digestive fire and improves taste and appetite, and notably it does not aggravate Pitta much. This makes it a warming, appetising vegetable well suited to weak digestion and Kapha-Vata sluggishness.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Ash gourd and the gourd family","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"bladder trouble, Pitta-Vata heat, need for a cooling vegetable","guidance":"Ash gourd (kushmanda) is rated the best of the creeper vegetables. It calms both Vata and Pitta, cleanses the urinary bladder and is aphrodisiac, making it a prized cooling and strengthening food. Tender gourds of the cucumber-melon family soothe Pitta and are cooling when young but turn opposite when ripe. Cucumber (trapusa) is diuretic. Many gourds raise Kapha and Vata and sit heavy, so they are best eaten young and well cooked.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Radish (Mulaka)","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","signs":"tender radish for digestion; mature radish aggravates","guidance":"Radish is a vegetable whose value depends entirely on its age. Tender radish, before it develops a strong taste, is slightly alkaline, balances all the doshas, is light, hot in potency, and cures abdominal lumps, cough, respiratory conditions, ulcers, diseases of the eye and throat, hoarseness, bloating and nasal catarrh. Large, mature radish is the opposite: pungent, hard to digest, aggravating to all three doshas and clogging. When cooked with fats it calms Vata, while dried radish increases the doshas. So choose it young and cook it with oil or ghee.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Pungent kitchen greens — holy basil, drumstick, mint family","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"poor appetite, cough, bad breath, Kapha states","guidance":"Green pungent herbs such as holy basil (tulasi), drumstick (sigru), surasa and their relatives are absorbent when fresh, cause some burning during digestion, are pungent and drying, hot in potency, good for the heart and mind, and they kindle hunger and taste and destroy worms. The caution is that they can harm vision and semen and cause a slight rise in the doshas. Holy basil (tulasi) specifically relieves hiccup, cough, poison, asthma, flank pain and bad breath. These are warming, appetising seasonings rather than bulk foods.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Garlic (Lashuna), onion and yam for piles","category":"Energy","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Kapha-Vata disease, skin disease, weakness, piles","guidance":"Garlic is strongly penetrating, hot, pungent in taste and after digestion, laxative, good for the heart and hair, heavy, aphrodisiac, oily, appetising, bone-knitting, strengthening and a recognised rejuvenator; it treats leucoderma, skin disease, abdominal lumps, piles, diabetes, worms, Kapha-Vata disease, hiccup, nasal catarrh, asthma and cough, but it strongly vitiates blood and Pitta, so Pitta types should be careful. Onion (palandu) is a milder, weaker version that raises Pitta less. Carrot (grinjanaka) suits piles of Kapha-Vata origin but not Pitta-dominant people, and yam (surana) kindles digestion, reduces Kapha and is especially good for piles.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Grapes (Draksha) — the best fruit","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"combination","signs":"thirst, cough, fever, Vata-Pitta-blood disorders, weakness","guidance":"Grapes are considered the finest of all fruits. They are aphrodisiac, good for the eyes, gently move urine and stool, sweet in taste and after digestion, oily, slightly astringent, cooling and heavy. They soothe disorders of Vata, Pitta and blood, improve the taste in the mouth, and relieve intoxication, thirst, cough, fever, breathlessness, hoarseness, injury to the lungs and chronic respiratory disease. This broad, gentle, restorative action is why grapes top the fruit list.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Pomegranate (Dadima)","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"excess Pitta, poor appetite, loose motions, heart weakness","guidance":"Pomegranate subdues greatly increased Pitta in particular, and also soothes the other doshas, while being sweet. The sour variety still does not raise Pitta much, is only mildly hot and calms Vata and Kapha. All pomegranate is good for the heart, light to digest, oily, absorbent (so it checks loose motions) and stimulating to appetite and digestion. It is an unusually balancing fruit, suiting Pitta conditions yet gentle on the other doshas.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Sweet building fruits and fruits by ripeness (banana, mango, bael, jamun)","category":"Energy","dosha":"Vata","signs":"wasting, burning, bleeding; and ripeness-dependent effects","guidance":"A large group of sweet, cooling, heavy, oily fruits such as banana, dates, jackfruit and coconut build the body, relieve burning, chest injury and bleeding, and are sweet after digestion, but they sit long in the stomach and increase Kapha and semen. Ripeness changes a fruit's action sharply: bael (bilva) is good unripe (kindles digestion, calms Vata and Kapha) but troublesome when ripe; mango is Vata-, blood- and Pitta-raising when unripe and Vata-calming but Kapha- and semen-increasing when ripe. Jamun is cooling, absorbent, drying and strongly Vata-aggravating. Sour fruits like amla and kola mostly aid digestion and move the bowels. Lakucha is rated the worst fruit, raising all the doshas.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Salts — rock salt and the others","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","signs":"poor digestion, bloating, belching, constipation","guidance":"All salts are penetrating, soften and ease the movement of stool, calm Vata, aid digestion, and tend to raise Kapha and Pitta. Rock salt (saindhava) is the best of them: mildly sweet, aphrodisiac, good for the heart and mind, balancing to all three doshas, light, not overly hot and non-burning, which is why it is the preferred salt for daily and medicinal use. Sochal salt (sauvarchala) is light, digestive, fragrant and clears belching and constipation; bida salt moves Kapha and Vata both up and down and relieves bloating, colic and heaviness; while common sea salt is heavier and raises Kapha more.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Triphala and the myrobalans (haritaki, amalaki, vibhitaki)","category":"Prevention","dosha":"combination","signs":"general rejuvenation, eye disease, skin disease, Kapha and blood","guidance":"The three myrobalans together make Triphala, regarded as a leading rejuvenator that helps eye disease, heals wounds, clears skin disease, excess tissue moisture, obesity, diabetes and aggravated Kapha and blood. Haritaki on its own is astringent, sweet after digestion, drying, light, appetising and digestive, sharpens the mind, best preserves youth, is hot in potency and laxative, lengthens life and treats a very wide range of Kapha-Vata disorders. Amalaki is similar but cooling and calms Pitta and Kapha, and vibhitaki (aksha) is cooling, good for hair and pungent after digestion. Together they are a cornerstone of preventive and rejuvenating diet.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Warming spices — pepper, long pepper, ginger, trikatu","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"weak digestion, cough, asthma, obesity, nasal catarrh","guidance":"The warming spices are the main digestive and Kapha-reducing condiments. Black pepper (maricha) is pungent in taste and after digestion, reduces Kapha and is light. Long pepper (pippali) when dry is aphrodisiac, pungent, sweet after digestion, reduces Vata and Kapha and helps asthma and cough, and is laxative, but it should not be used in excess or for long periods without a proper rejuvenation regimen. Ginger (nagara, and fresh ardraka) kindles appetite, is aphrodisiac, absorbent, heart-friendly, relieves constipation, improves taste, is light, sweet after digestion, oily, hot and reduces Kapha and Vata. Pepper, long pepper and ginger together form trikatu, used for obesity, asthma, indigestion, cough, filariasis and nasal catarrh, and the panchakola group is excellent for appetite, lumps and bloating.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Asafoetida (Hingu) and the panchamula root groups","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","signs":"bloating, colic, Vata-Kapha obstruction","guidance":"Asafoetida (hingu) calms Vata and Kapha, relieves distension of the abdomen and colic, is pungent in taste and after digestion, enhances taste, hunger and digestion and is light, though it slightly raises Pitta. For deeper treatment, Ayurveda groups several roots into panchamula sets: the greater and lesser panchamula (which together form the dashamula) and the middle, life-giving (jivana) and grass (trina) panchamulas. Broadly, the greater and lesser root groups reduce Kapha and Vata, the jivana group is good for the eyes, aphrodisiac and calms Pitta and Vata, and the trina group reduces Pitta. These are medicinal condiments and decoction bases rather than everyday foods.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Incompatible food combinations (Viruddha ahara)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","signs":"For anyone eating two clashing foods together or oddly prepared food; worse in the weak, sedentary and those with poor digestion","guidance":"Some foods are safe on their own but harmful when combined, because together they stir up the Doshas without letting the body clear them. Treat such combinations like a mild poison. Avoid pairing milk with anything sour (sour fruit, curd, buttermilk) and with fish; avoid fish with jaggery or radish; do not mix honey and ghee in equal amounts, do not take honey with hot water, and never heat honey. Skip milk right after eating radish or leafy greens, and do not store ghee in a bronze vessel for more than about ten days. The practical signal is this: if a mixture or a method of cooking makes you bloated, acidic or unwell even though each item alone agrees with you, treat it as incompatible and stop. Strong digestion, regular exercise, youth and small accustomed quantities reduce the harm, but they do not make a bad combination good.","chapter":7},{"problem":"Safe food, and spotting food that has gone bad or been tampered with","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","signs":"Before eating any prepared food, especially food handled by others","guidance":"Guarding food is the heart of this chapter. Watch for unnatural changes: food that loses its normal colour, smell, taste and texture too fast, turns oddly thick or watery with shiny specks, grows froth, threads or bubbles, or shows strange coloured streaks. Fruit that ripens or rots too quickly, soft foods that turn hard, and foul or sharp smells are all warnings. If in doubt, do not eat it. The ancient fire and animal tests were simply ways to confirm suspicion before eating. The modern takeaway is to keep food clean and covered, eat it fresh, trust the people who prepare it, and throw away anything that looks, smells or tastes off rather than risk it.","chapter":7},{"problem":"Changing a diet or habit safely (gradual accustomization)","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","signs":"When giving up a long-standing unhealthy habit or starting a new healthy one","guidance":"Do not swing suddenly from one extreme to another, because an abrupt change in either direction can itself trigger illness. Drop an unhealthy food or habit a little at a time, roughly a quarter of the amount in each step, and bring in a new healthy one slowly too, adding it over spaced days. This gentle tapering lets the body adjust. Over time the bad influence fades and the good habit takes hold, and the result is steady, robust health rather than the shock and relapse that come from going cold turkey.","chapter":7},{"problem":"Healthy sleep (Nidra) — timing and amount","category":"Prevention","dosha":"combination","signs":"Poor sleep, daytime drowsiness, or sleeping at odd hours; waking tired","guidance":"Sleep is one of the three pillars of health, and happiness, nourishment, strength and clarity of mind all depend on it. Three faults harm you: sleeping at the wrong time, sleeping too much, and not sleeping enough. Sleep at night, at a regular hour, as much as you genuinely need. Night waking dries the body, while daytime sleep tends to moisten it and raise Kapha and Pitta, so avoid day naps except in summer or if you are genuinely exhausted, ill, very old or very young. If you lost a night's sleep, you may rest the next morning for about half that time, without first eating. For too little sleep, use milk, nourishing meals, oil massage, a warm bath, calm surroundings and a contented mind; for too much sleep, use lighter food, activity and stimulating routines.","chapter":7},{"problem":"The three pillars of life (food, sleep, balanced sex life)","category":"General","dosha":"—","signs":"General wellbeing and longevity","guidance":"The body is held up by three supports, like a house on pillars: Ahara (food), Nidra (sleep) and a balanced, disciplined sex life. When all three are used wisely they sustain health; when any is neglected or overused, health falls. Eat well and at the right time, sleep properly at night, and keep intimacy in moderation, which the text says should be freer in the cold seasons and much more restrained in the rains and heat. Overindulgence drains strength and ages the body early, while balance preserves memory, energy and long life. After intimacy, rest, bathing and cooling drinks help restore vigour.","chapter":7},{"problem":"Suspected food poisoning — first care","category":"General","dosha":"combination","signs":"Burning, vomiting, giddiness, bloating, diarrhoea or skin eruptions after eating suspect food","guidance":"Classical Ayurveda treated poisoning in stages by where it acts. Contact with the skin causes itching, burning and eruptions, eased by washing and cooling herbal pastes such as sandalwood and khus. In the mouth it numbs the tongue and burns, eased by medicated gargles. In the stomach and intestines it causes vomiting, bloating, giddiness, loose motions and weakness, for which the texts used controlled emesis and purgation to clear the toxin, followed by soothing, strengthening measures. This is educational background only. Real suspected poisoning is an emergency: stop eating the food, keep it for identification, and get prompt medical help rather than attempting these old therapies yourself.","chapter":7},{"problem":"How much to eat (the right quantity of food)","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","signs":"Everyone, at every meal; especially helpful for those who feel heavy, bloated or sleepy after eating","guidance":"Eat the measured amount that keeps your digestive fire (Agni) working well, because the right quantity is what lets food digest cleanly. As a guide, heavy foods like oily dishes, meat and sweets should fill only about half of your comfortable capacity, while light foods can be a bit more but still stop short of feeling full. The surest test is the result: you ate the right amount if the meal digests without discomfort, heaviness or sleepiness. The best practical rule is to divide the stomach into three parts, fill two with solid food and one with liquid, and leave the last part empty so there is room for movement and digestion. In short, stop before you feel stuffed.","chapter":8},{"problem":"Eating too little or too much","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","signs":"Low energy and slow wasting from under-eating; heaviness, acidity and gut upsets from over-eating","guidance":"Both extremes harm you. Eating too little fails to build strength and immunity, leaves the body under-nourished, and over time triggers Vata problems such as weakness and aches. Eating too much suddenly disturbs all three Doshas at once; mixed with undigested food it creates Ama (toxic residue) and can block the gut (causing a stuck, lazy digestion) or force food out violently as vomiting and diarrhoea. The fix is self-control at the table: eat a moderate, measured amount, do not push past comfortable fullness, and if you have eaten too much, lighten the next meal or fast briefly rather than piling more food on top.","chapter":8},{"problem":"Indigestion (Ajirna) — types and what to do","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","signs":"Bloating, heaviness, belching, nausea, loose stool or constipation, dullness and dizziness after meals","guidance":"Indigestion comes in a few forms. Kapha-type (Ama ajirna) brings puffy eyes and cheeks, drooling, nausea, heaviness and belching that tastes of the meal; its remedy is fasting until the gut clears. Vata-type (Vishtabdha) brings belly pain, gas, constipation and weakness; its remedy is warmth and sweating therapy (a warm bath, hot water bottle, light fomentation). Pitta-type (Vidagdha) brings thirst, giddiness, sour burning belches; here lightening and cooling help, and the classics used controlled emesis. For any type, the general advice is gentle: rest quietly, do not eat while still full, drink warm water, and only eat again, in a small, light amount, once genuine hunger returns. A key safety point from the text: do not take painkillers for the belly pain of indigestion, because the clogged digestive fire cannot cope with the drug on top of the undigested food.","chapter":8},{"problem":"Undigested food toxins (Ama) and how to clear them","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","signs":"Coated feeling, heaviness, dullness, bad breath, poor appetite after irregular or clashing eating","guidance":"Ama is food left half-digested that turns toxic inside you. It builds up from eating incompatible foods, overeating, and eating again before the last meal is digested. The remedy is matched to how much Ama there is. If it is little, simple fasting (Langhana) is enough, just skip a meal and let the gut catch up. If it is moderate, fast and add digestive support such as warm water, ginger and light spiced food (Pachana). If it is heavy and entrenched, a supervised cleansing (Shodhana) is needed to remove it at the root. The guiding principle is lightening, not forcing food down. Once appetite and lightness return, ease back gently with small, warm, easy-to-digest meals before returning to normal food.","chapter":8},{"problem":"Bad eating habits (Dushta Ashana)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","signs":"Irregular eaters, grazers, those who snack before the last meal digests or eat at random hours","guidance":"Three habits are singled out as harmful enough to cause serious disease. Samashana is mixing suitable and unsuitable foods in one meal. Adhyashana is eating a fresh meal before the previous one has digested, the classic cause of Ama. Vishamashana is eating too much or too little, or at the wrong, irregular times. The cure is rhythm and simplicity: eat at regular times, let one meal fully digest (real hunger is the signal) before the next, and keep meals coherent rather than a jumble of clashing items. Also avoid eating when grief-stricken, angry or over-hungry, since a disturbed mind and extreme hunger both spoil digestion.","chapter":8},{"problem":"Rules for a healthy meal (Ahara vidhi)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"—","signs":"For everyone wanting food to nourish rather than trouble them","guidance":"Eat clean, familiar, wholesome food that is warm, lightly unctuous and easy to digest. Include all six tastes with sweet predominating, and eat neither too fast nor too slowly. Eat only when genuinely hungry, after washing hands, feet and face, in a calm quiet place and in good company, with full attention on the meal rather than screens or arguments. Do not criticise the food and do not talk too much while eating. Favour some liquid or moist food. Avoid reheated food, food fouled with grass or hair, food that is mostly poor vegetables or inferior grains, and anything too hot or too salty. Within the meal, take heavy sweet oily foods first, sour and salty items in the middle, and lighter foods last.","chapter":8},{"problem":"The right time to eat","category":"Digestion","dosha":"—","signs":"Deciding when a meal will actually digest well","guidance":"The ideal moment to eat is when the body shows it is ready: after you have passed stool and urine, when the mind is calm, belching is clean without sour taste or smell, hunger is clearly felt, gas moves downward easily, the senses feel clear and the body feels light. Eating at that point lets the digestive fire do its job. Eating before the previous meal has cleared, or when upset, rushed or not truly hungry, invites indigestion. So let real hunger and these body signals, not the clock or the company, decide when to eat.","chapter":8},{"problem":"After-meal drinks (Anupana) — what to drink with food","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","signs":"Choosing what to drink after eating, by body type and meal","guidance":"A good after-drink has qualities opposite to the meal but not clashing with it; it helps food move, soften and digest, and leaves you satisfied. Cold water suits meals of wheat and barley and follows curd, honey or wine. Warm water suits starchy, heavy, legume and buttermilk-based meals. For practical body types, honey water suits the overweight, meat soup or nourishing drinks suit the thin and depleted, and milk acts like a tonic for those weakened by illness, long travel, speaking, fasting or sun, and for the old and young. But skip heavy after-drinks if you have throat, nose, ear or eye complaints, cough, breathing trouble or hoarseness. And whether well or ill, do not immediately sleep, walk far, sit in sun or fire, or travel right after eating or drinking.","chapter":8},{"problem":"How a substance acts: taste, quality, potency, post-digestive effect and special effect","category":"General","dosha":"combination","signs":"Anyone trying to understand why a food or herb has the effect it does","guidance":"Ayurveda explains the action of any food or medicine through five layers. Rasa is the taste you perceive (sweet, sour, salty, pungent, bitter, astringent). Guna is its quality (heavy or light, oily or dry, hot or cold, and so on). Virya is its potency, mainly whether it is heating or cooling. Vipaka is the taste it turns into after digestion. Prabhava is a special, unique action that cannot be explained by the other four. When these point in different directions, the strongest one decides the effect, and there is a natural order: potency (Virya) outranks taste and Vipaka, and special effect (Prabhava) outranks everything. Knowing these layers is what lets a practitioner predict whether something will warm or cool you, lighten or nourish you, and calm or stir a particular Dosha.","chapter":9},{"problem":"Hot versus cold potency (Virya) of foods and herbs","category":"Energy","dosha":"combination","signs":"Choosing warming or cooling foods for your constitution or condition","guidance":"Potency (Virya) is the single most practical property, and the common view reduces it to two, heating (Ushna) and cooling (Sheeta), because fire and water are the two great forces of nature. Heating substances speed up digestion and transformation, can cause thirst, sweating and a burning feeling if overused, and they calm Vata and Kapha. Cooling substances soothe, refresh and steady the body, settle Pitta and calm the blood. In daily use this means: if you run hot, are acidic or Pitta-aggravated, favour cooling foods; if you are cold, sluggish or Kapha- or Vata-aggravated, favour warming foods and spices. Judging a food by its heating or cooling potency, rather than only its taste, is the key to matching food to your body.","chapter":9},{"problem":"Post-digestive effect (Vipaka) — what food becomes after digestion","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","signs":"Understanding the lasting effect of a food after it is digested","guidance":"Vipaka is the taste a food transforms into at the end of digestion, and it often matters more than the taste on your tongue. The rule is simple: sweet and salty foods become sweet after digestion (nourishing, building, grounding), sour foods stay sour, and bitter, pungent and astringent foods become pungent (lightening, drying, stimulating). This is why a food can taste one way yet have a different long-term effect on the body. When you want to know whether a food will ultimately nourish and build you or lighten and dry you, look to its Vipaka, not just its immediate taste.","chapter":9},{"problem":"Special effect (Prabhava) and why similar foods can act differently","category":"General","dosha":"—","signs":"When two foods or herbs seem alike but behave differently","guidance":"Sometimes two substances share the same taste, qualities, potency and post-digestive effect, yet one does something the other simply does not. That unexplained, unique action is called Prabhava, a special effect that overrides all the ordinary properties. Classic examples: Danti purges while the similar Chitraka does not; dry grapes gently purge while licorice does not; ghee boosts digestive strength while milk, otherwise similar, does not. The lesson is that general categories only take you so far. You must know a substance's actual, observed behaviour, because its signature effect can surprise you even when everything on paper looks the same.","chapter":9},{"problem":"The five elements and the nature of every substance","category":"General","dosha":"combination","signs":"Understanding why foods and herbs have the properties they do","guidance":"Ayurveda teaches that everything, food, herb or medicine, is built from five great elements in different proportions: Earth, Water, Fire, Air and Ether. Whichever element dominates gives the substance its nature. Earth-dominant things are heavy, solid and nourishing; water-dominant things are liquid, cooling, moist and binding; fire-dominant things are hot, sharp and digestive; air-dominant things are light, dry and mobile; ether-dominant things are subtle and light. Fire- and air-rich substances tend to move energy upward in the body, while earth- and water-rich ones move it downward. This framework is the reason the same six tastes can produce such varied effects, and it underlies every other rule about how foods and medicines work.","chapter":9},{"problem":"Nothing is without medicinal use","category":"General","dosha":"—","signs":"General principle of Ayurvedic treatment","guidance":"A central teaching of this chapter is that there is no substance in the world that cannot serve as medicine; what decides whether it helps or harms is knowing the substance well and using it for the right purpose, in the right way and amount. The same item can be food, poison or medicine depending on the person, the dose and the context. The practical message is humility and precision: do not divide things rigidly into good and bad, but learn each substance's taste, potency and effect, and apply it thoughtfully to the right condition.","chapter":9},{"problem":"Sweet taste (Madhura) - effects and when it harms","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"Sweet feels sticky and spreading in the mouth and leaves the body contented and the senses soothed.","guidance":"Sweet taste is formed from the earth and water elements. It is the taste we know from birth and the most nourishing of all. It builds every body tissue and increases strength, which is why it suits children, the elderly, the injured and the weak. It improves the skin, hair, the sense organs and ojas, the vital essence tied to immunity. It firms up the body, soothes the throat, increases breast milk and helps broken bones mend. Being oily and slow to digest, it promotes long life. In the doshas it calms both Pitta and Vata and helps counter poison. Its danger lies in excess: too much sweet food builds Kapha and fat, leading to obesity, breathing problems, heaviness and drowsiness, diabetes, swollen neck glands and abnormal growths. This is educational background from a classical text, not a treatment plan.","chapter":10},{"problem":"Sour taste (Amla) - effects and when it harms","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"Sour makes the mouth water, raises gooseflesh, sets the teeth on edge and makes the eyes and brows pucker.","guidance":"Sour taste comes from the fire and water elements. It kindles the digestive fire, pleases the heart and sharpens the appetite. It is oily, hot in its internal potency yet cooling when applied to the skin, where it relieves burning. It moistens the body, is light and easy to digest, and it nudges stuck Vata to move downward in its natural direction. In the doshas it raises Kapha, Pitta and the blood. Used in excess it slackens the body and drains strength, and can bring dimness of vision, giddiness, itching, a pale anaemic look, spreading herpes-type skin disease, swellings, pox-like eruptions, thirst and fevers. Note the general rule that sour usually aggravates Pitta, with pomegranate and amla being the gentle exceptions. Shared for understanding only, not as medical advice.","chapter":10},{"problem":"Salt taste (Lavana) - effects and when it harms","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Salt increases watering in the mouth and brings a burning feeling in the cheeks and throat.","guidance":"Salt taste is formed from the water and fire elements. It loosens stiffness and rigidity, clears blockages in the body's channels and pores, strengthens digestion, lubricates the tissues, encourages sweating and penetrates deep into the body. It improves the flavour of food and helps abscesses open and drain. In balance it is useful, but in excess it spoils the blood and stirs up Vata, which can show as baldness, premature greying, wrinkled skin, thirst, skin diseases, poison-like effects, spreading skin disease and an overall loss of bodily strength. A classical note adds that salts are generally unfriendly to the eyes, with rock salt as the exception. This is traditional teaching, offered for learning rather than as a prescription.","chapter":10},{"problem":"Bitter taste (Tikta) - effects and when it harms","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"Bitter cleans the mouth and dulls the tongue so that other tastes are hard to perceive.","guidance":"Bitter taste arises from the ether and air elements. Though it is not pleasant by itself, it is genuinely corrective: it helps with loss of appetite, worms, thirst, the effects of poison, skin diseases, fainting, fever, nausea and burning sensations. In the doshas it calms both Pitta and Kapha. It dries up excess moisture, fat, muscle-fat, marrow, stool and urine, so it is strongly drying. It is light and easy to digest, cool in potency, sharpens intelligence and helps cleanse breast milk and the throat. Its weakness is that in excess it thins and depletes the body tissues and tends to provoke Vata disorders, which is why very bitter, very drying diets do not suit already dry or depleted people. Educational information from an ancient source, not a cure.","chapter":10},{"problem":"Pungent taste (Katu) - effects and when it harms","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"Pungent bites the tip of the tongue, irritates it, draws watering from eyes, nose and mouth, and burns the cheeks.","guidance":"Pungent taste is formed from the fire and air elements. It clears throat ailments, allergic rashes, skin diseases, a form of indigestion called alasaka and various swellings, and it reduces the swelling around ulcers. It dries up oiliness, fat and excess water, increases hunger, aids digestion, improves taste, cleanses the system and helps expel aggravated doshas, dries the moisture in food, breaks up hard lumps and opens the body's channels. In the doshas it reduces Kapha. Overused, it causes thirst, depletion of semen and strength, fainting, muscle cramps, tremors and pain in the waist and back. The text also notes that bitter and pungent generally raise Vata and reduce sexual vigour, with guduchi, patola, ginger, long pepper and garlic as exceptions. Offered for learning, not as treatment.","chapter":10},{"problem":"Astringent taste (Kashaya) - effects and when it harms","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"combination","signs":"Astringent numbs the tongue, blunts taste and leaves a feeling of the throat being blocked or tightened.","guidance":"Astringent taste comes from the earth and air elements. It settles both Pitta and Kapha. It is slow to digest, cleanses the blood, presses and helps heal wounds, is cold in potency, and dries up moisture and fat. It tends to slow the digestion of food still undigested. Because it absorbs water, it can firm the stool to the point of constipation and dryness, and it can over-cleanse the skin. In excess it keeps food stuck and undigested, causing bloating, pain in the chest region, thirst, wasting, loss of virility, blocked channels and constipation. A general rule notes astringents are usually cold and obstructive, with haritaki as the exception. This is background from a classical health text, shared for understanding and not as medical guidance.","chapter":10},{"problem":"How tastes behave - potency, dryness and weight","category":"General","dosha":"—","signs":"","guidance":"Besides their direct effects, the six tastes also behave in predictable ways that guide how they are used. In terms of heating and cooling, pungent, sour and salt are heating in potency, each stronger than the one before, while bitter, astringent and sweet are cooling, again in increasing order. In terms of dryness, bitter, pungent and astringent are drying and tend to constipate, whereas salt, sour and sweet are oily and help the body pass stool, urine and gas. In terms of weight, salt, astringent and sweet are heavy and slow to digest, while sour, pungent and bitter are light and digest easily. The tastes can be used alone or blended, giving sixty-three practical combinations in all, and because tastes also vary in strength and in primary versus secondary roles, the real range of blends is effectively endless. Classical teaching chooses among them based on the state of the doshas and the goal of the food or medicine. Shared for learning only.","chapter":10},{"problem":"Qualities and functions of Vata, and what raises it","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Raised Vata shows as wasting, dark skin tone, craving warmth, tremors, bloating, constipation, weakness, poor sleep, failing senses, rambling speech, dizziness and timidity. Low Vata shows as weakness, very little speech and dulled awareness.","guidance":"Vata is the principle of all movement. When it is healthy it drives the flow of blood and air, moves the limbs, gives enthusiasm, runs breathing, controls the mind and speech, starts off natural urges like tears, stool, urine, sneezing and yawning, keeps the tissues in order and keeps the senses alert. Its home tissue is the bone, and unusually it relates to bone in reverse: when Vata rises, bone wastes, and when Vata falls, bone builds up. Vata increases mainly with under-nutrition, dryness, cold and too much movement or activity, and decreases with over-nutrition and nourishment. For this reason classical teaching treats raised Vata with building, nourishing (Brimhana) measures rather than reducing ones, which is the opposite of how Pitta and Kapha excess is handled. This is educational background from a classical text, not a treatment plan.","chapter":11},{"problem":"Qualities and functions of Pitta, and what raises it","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"Raised Pitta shows as yellowing of stool, urine, eyes and skin, strong hunger and thirst, burning sensations and very little sleep. Low Pitta shows as weak digestion, coldness and loss of lustre.","guidance":"Pitta is the principle of digestion, heat and transformation. When healthy it runs digestion and metabolism, holds body temperature steady, powers vision, creates hunger, thirst and appetite, and maintains skin colour, intelligence, courage and bodily softness. Its home is in sweat and blood, and it moves with them: when Pitta rises, the spoiling of sweat and blood rises too, and the same in reverse. Pitta tends to increase with over-nutrition that later shows as raised Kapha in general terms, but Pitta specifically is stirred by heat and sharp, sour, salty influences. Conditions from excess Pitta are treated with lightening, cooling (Langhana) measures, and deficiency with nourishing ones. A classical specific is that excess blood, closely tied to Pitta, is managed by bloodletting and purgation. Shared for understanding only, not as medical advice.","chapter":11},{"problem":"Qualities and functions of Kapha, and what raises it","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"Raised Kapha shows as weak digestion, excess salivation, laziness, heaviness, pale colour, coldness, loose limbs, breathing trouble, cough and too much sleep. Low Kapha shows as dizziness, a sense of emptiness, heart palpitation and loose joints.","guidance":"Kapha is the principle of structure, stability and lubrication. When healthy it gives the body firmness, lubricates and binds the joints, and supports mental steadiness, patience and the capacity to hold back emotion and strain. Its home is the remaining tissues and the wastes, and it moves with them in the usual direction, rising and falling together. Kapha increases mainly with over-nutrition, which is why excess tends to appear as the heavy, cold, sluggish signs above. Conditions from excess Kapha are treated with lightening, reducing (Langhana) measures that cut heaviness and moisture, while deficiency is built back with nourishing measures. This is traditional teaching offered for learning, not a prescription.","chapter":11},{"problem":"The seven tissues (dhatus) - roles and signs of excess or deficiency","category":"General","dosha":"—","signs":"Excess tissue shows as more of its own qualities; deficiency shows as the opposite. For example, low rasa brings dryness and fatigue; excess rakta brings skin disease and bleeding disorders.","guidance":"The body has seven tissues, each with its own job. Rasa (plasma) nourishes; rakta (blood) sustains life activity; mamsa (muscle) covers and supports and enables movement; meda (fat) lubricates; asthi (bone) gives the frame; majja (marrow) fills the bones; shukra (reproductive fluid) enables conception. When a tissue increases it produces more of its own nature: excess blood brings spreading skin disease, bleeding disorders, jaundice and redness; excess muscle enlarges cheeks, thighs and abdomen; excess fat brings breathlessness and drooping; excess bone overgrows bone and teeth; excess marrow brings heaviness and stubborn ulcers; excess reproductive fluid brings strong desire and seminal stones; excess rasa mimics raised Kapha. When a tissue decreases, the opposite qualities appear, such as dryness and wasting with low rasa, craving for sour and cold with low blood, joint pain and lost teeth with low bone. A change in an earlier tissue tends to pass the same change to the next in sequence. Educational information from an ancient source, not a cure.","chapter":11},{"problem":"The three wastes (malas) - roles and signs of excess or deficiency","category":"Digestion","dosha":"—","signs":"Excess faeces causes bloating, gurgling and heaviness; excess urine causes bladder pain and a feeling of incomplete emptying; excess sweat causes heavy perspiration, foul smell and itching. Too little waste causes dryness, pricking pain and emptiness at its sites.","guidance":"The body has three main wastes, each with a healthy purpose. Faeces help maintain the body's bulk and strength; urine carries off liquid waste; sweat slowly carries off moisture. When they increase, the signs reflect too much of each: a distended, noisy, heavy abdomen from faeces, severe bladder pain and a sense of not having emptied from urine, and heavy, foul, itchy perspiration from sweat. When they decrease, the little that forms is hard to perceive and must be inferred from dryness, pricking pain, emptiness and tightness at the sites where waste is made and released. Because the body is used to carrying a normal load of waste in the gut and bladder, a shortfall in waste is actually more troublesome than an excess. Classical specifics include correcting low faeces with heavier foods and boosting low sweat with exercise, oil baths and sweating therapy. Shared for learning, not as treatment.","chapter":11},{"problem":"How to tell which dosha, tissue or waste is out of balance","category":"General","dosha":"—","signs":"Increase is read as more of a thing's own qualities; decrease as more of the opposite qualities; wastes increased when they fail to clear, decreased when too little passes.","guidance":"Ayurveda reads imbalance by qualities, not just quantity. A dosha, tissue or waste that has increased reveals itself by showing more of its own natural qualities, while one that has decreased shows the opposite qualities. Wastes are judged increased when they build up and fail to clear, causing obstruction, and decreased when only small amounts pass. Increase of doshas, tissues and wastes generally traces back to over-nutrition, which later presents as raised Kapha, while decrease traces back to under-nutrition, which later presents as raised Vata. This guides treatment: excess is reduced with lightening (Langhana) measures and deficiency is built with nourishing (Brimhana) measures, with Vata as the standing exception that is treated the opposite way. The underlying idea is that the very doshas which keep the body growing when balanced become the cause of its decline when disturbed, so health means guarding against both their rise and their fall. This is background from a classical health text, shared for understanding and not as medical guidance.","chapter":11},{"problem":"Ojas - the vital essence, its loss and increase","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","signs":"Falling ojas shows as fearfulness, weakness, constant causeless worry, uneasy senses, poor complexion, negative thoughts and dryness. Rising ojas shows as contentment, good nourishment and strength.","guidance":"Ojas is the subtle essence of all seven tissues, the most refined product of shukra. It sits mainly in the heart but pervades the whole body and is the basis of health; where ojas is, life is, and its loss is the loss of life. It is described as oily, watery, clear and slightly reddish-yellow. Ojas is depleted by anger, starvation, worry, grief and overexertion, and classical texts add overexercise, long fasting, under-eating, drying drinks, poor food combinations, fear, staying awake at night, injury, infection, tissue loss such as from bleeding, excessive loss of Kapha, blood, semen or waste, and poison. When ojas falls the person becomes fearful, weak, endlessly anxious for no reason, uneasy in the senses, poor in colour, negative and dry. Classical management uses enlivening (jivaniya) herbs, milk and meat juice. When ojas is strong there is contentment, nourishment and strength. This is educational material from a classical text, not medical advice.","chapter":11},{"problem":"Prana Vata - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata","signs":"","guidance":"Prana is the first of the five sub-types of Vata. It is seated in the head and moves through the chest and throat. It governs the higher functions: the will, the heart, the sense organs, the intellect and vision. On the physical side it drives expectoration, sneezing, belching, the drawing in of the breath and the swallowing of food. Because it rules breathing and the mind, it is the most vital of the Vata sub-types. This is educational background from a classical text, not a treatment plan.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Udana Vata - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata","signs":"","guidance":"Udana is the second sub-type of Vata. Its seat is the chest, and it moves through the nose, navel and throat. It is the upward-moving air behind expression and vitality: it starts off speech, powers effort and enthusiasm, and maintains strength, colour and complexion and memory. In simple terms, it is the force behind how a person speaks, exerts themselves and holds their vigour. Shared for understanding only, not as medical advice.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Vyana Vata - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata","signs":"","guidance":"Vyana is the third sub-type of Vata. It is seated in the heart and races throughout the entire body at great speed. It carries out all forms of movement: bending and straightening the limbs, the opening and closing of the eyelids, and generally every bodily action. Because it pervades everywhere so quickly, nearly all the body's activities are regulated by Vyana. This is traditional teaching offered for learning, not a prescription.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Samana Vata - seat and role","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","signs":"","guidance":"Samana is the fourth sub-type of Vata. It sits beside the digestive fire and moves through the kostha, the alimentary tract. Its job is tied to digestion: it receives food into the stomach, helps cook and digest it, separates the nourishing part from the waste part, and then moves each of these along its proper path. In effect it is the air that keeps the digestive process orderly. Educational information from an ancient source, not a cure.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Apana Vata - seat and role","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","signs":"","guidance":"Apana is the fifth sub-type of Vata. It is seated in the large intestine and moves through the waist, bladder and genitals. It is the downward-moving air that handles elimination and reproduction: ejaculation, menstruation, defecation, urination and childbirth. Because it governs the natural downward urges, its balance matters for regular elimination and reproductive health. Shared for learning, not as treatment.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Pachaka Pitta - seat and role","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"","guidance":"Pachaka is the first of the five sub-types of Pitta. It sits between the stomach and the large intestine. Though made of all five elements, it is dominant in the fire element and lacks the water element, which is why it is called anala, fire. Its work is digestion: it cooks the food and divides it into essence and waste. It also lends its grace and power to the other four Pittas, supporting their functions. For this reason Pachaka is considered the chief among the Pittas. This is educational background from a classical text, not a treatment plan.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Ranjaka Pitta - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"","guidance":"Ranjaka is the second sub-type of Pitta. It is seated in the stomach. Its name comes from colouring, because its job is to colour and convert the useful product of digestion into blood. In the language of the tissues, it transforms rasa (plasma) into rakta (blood). It is therefore central to the formation and quality of the blood. Shared for understanding only, not as medical advice.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Sadhaka Pitta - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"","guidance":"Sadhaka is the third sub-type of Pitta. It is seated in the heart. Unlike the digestive Pittas, its work is mental: it supports knowledge, intelligence and self-awareness, and in doing so helps a person accomplish the aims and purposes of life. It is, in a sense, the Pitta behind clear thinking and achievement. This is traditional teaching offered for learning, not a prescription.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Alochaka Pitta - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"","guidance":"Alochaka is the fourth sub-type of Pitta. It is seated in the eyes, and its single clear function is to enable vision, the taking in and processing of what is seen. Of the five Pittas it is the one dedicated to sight. Educational information from an ancient source, not a cure.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Bhrajaka Pitta - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"","guidance":"Bhrajaka is the fifth sub-type of Pitta. It resides in the skin, and its job is to bring out colour and complexion, giving the skin its glow and appearance. It is the Pitta that governs how the skin looks and how it displays colour. Shared for learning, not as treatment.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Avalambaka Kapha - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"","guidance":"Avalambaka is the first of the five sub-types of Kapha. It is seated in the chest and at the trika, the meeting place of the shoulders, neck and back. By its own innate strength and by the power drawn from the essence of food, it carries out the watery functions of lubrication and nourishment and provides support. It also influences and supports the other four Kaphas, which is why it is considered the foremost. This is educational background from a classical text, not a treatment plan.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Kledaka Kapha - seat and role","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"","guidance":"Kledaka is the second sub-type of Kapha. It is seated in the stomach. Its job is to moisten and soften the hard mass of food so that digestion can proceed smoothly. It is the Kapha that keeps the digestive contents suitably moist. Shared for understanding only, not as medical advice.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Bodhaka Kapha - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"","guidance":"Bodhaka is the third sub-type of Kapha. It is seated in the tongue, and its function is to enable the perception of taste. Its name comes from this awakening of taste awareness, so it is the Kapha that lets us register flavour. This is traditional teaching offered for learning, not a prescription.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Tarpaka Kapha - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"","guidance":"Tarpaka is the fourth sub-type of Kapha. It is seated in the head, and its job is to nourish and satisfy the sense organs, keeping them well supported. It is the Kapha that sustains the senses from its seat in the head. Educational information from an ancient source, not a cure.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Shleshaka Kapha - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"","guidance":"Shleshaka is the fifth sub-type of Kapha. It is seated in the bone joints. Its function is to lubricate and strengthen the joints, keeping them smooth and stable. Its name comes from binding, as it holds the joints together. Shared for learning, not as treatment.","chapter":12},{"problem":"How doshas move and the stages of imbalance (Chaya, Kopa, Shama) across the seasons","category":"Prevention","dosha":"combination","signs":"At the build-up stage the body develops a dislike for things that would raise the dosha further; at the overflow stage premonitory symptoms appear; at the settling stage the dosha returns to normal.","guidance":"A dosha does not go from balanced to diseased in one step; it passes through three stages. Chaya is a mild build-up in the dosha's own home, during which the body protects itself by creating a dislike for whatever would raise that dosha more. Kopa or prakopa is the overflow, when the raised dosha spills from its seat into other channels, early warning symptoms appear and disease can start. Shama is the settling back to normal, the return of health. The qualities that drive each dosha differ: Vata builds with hot-dry, overflows with cold-dry and settles with warm-oily; Pitta builds with cold-sharp, overflows with hot-sharp and settles with cold-mild; Kapha builds with cold-oily, overflows with hot-oily and settles with hot-dry. The seasons follow the same pattern: Vata builds in summer, overflows in the rains, settles in autumn; Pitta builds in the rains, overflows in autumn, settles in early winter; Kapha builds in winter, overflows in spring, settles in summer. Like floodwater that rushes in fast but drains slowly, a raised dosha spreads quickly from foot to head, while restoring balance takes much longer, which is why prevention and early correction matter. This is background from a classical health text, shared for understanding and not as medical guidance.","chapter":12},{"problem":"How to balance aggravated Vata","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Vata is dry, cold, light and mobile, so aggravation tends to show as dryness, cold, stiffness, cracking joints, restlessness, irregular digestion and a scattered, anxious feeling.","guidance":"Treat raised Vata with warmth, oiliness, grounding and comfort. Use oleation (sneha): oil or ghee taken internally as advised, plus regular warm oil self-massage (abhyanga) and simple rubbing. Follow massage with sudation or gentle sweating (sveda), and warm herbal pouring (seka) or wrapping the affected part in cloth for warmth. Favour foods that are sweet, sour and salty in taste, served warm and moist, and warming medicated fats prepared with appetite-kindling, digestion-improving herbs. Oily, warming enemas (basti, including anuvasana oleation enema from fatty meat juice and oil) are a classic Vata measure. Keep life calm, warm and reassuring. If any purification is needed it must be mild and gentle, because strong cleansing itself aggravates Vata; strong emesis, purgation or enema therapy should only be done under a qualified Ayurvedic practitioner. This is educational traditional guidance, not a cure.","chapter":13},{"problem":"How to calm excess Pitta","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"Pitta is hot, sharp, sour and intense, so aggravation tends to show as burning, heat, acidity, inflammation, irritability and anger.","guidance":"Cool, soothe and gently clear Pitta. Favour foods and herbs that are sweet, bitter and astringent, and cooling in nature; milk and ghee are specifically useful. Ghee may be taken (plain or medicated) on a physician's advice. Anoint the body often with cooling pastes of sandalwood, camphor and vetiver, and inhale cool, pleasant herbal fragrances. Build a calm, cooling lifestyle: moonlit evenings, soft breeze, gentle music, time near clean water, gardens and fountains, and the company of easy, agreeable people, keeping the mind peaceful. When clearing is needed, purgation (Virechana) using sweet-tasting, cooling herbs is the preferred route for Pitta, but purgation is a clinical procedure and should be guided by a qualified practitioner. This is educational traditional guidance, not a promise of cure.","chapter":13},{"problem":"How to reduce excess Kapha","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"Kapha is heavy, cold, oily, sweet and stable, so aggravation tends to show as heaviness, congestion, mucus, sluggishness, weight gain, excess sleep and dullness.","guidance":"Lighten, dry, warm and stimulate to bring Kapha down. Eat dry, light foods in small amounts and favour tastes that are pungent, bitter and astringent, along with sharp and warming qualities; grain soups and honey are helpful, and fat-reducing herbs and therapies are indicated. Lifestyle should be active and even a little demanding: varied exercise, dry massage (udvartana), staying awake rather than oversleeping, and accepting some effort and hardship. Inhaling medicinal smoke, gargling and periods of fasting all reduce Kapha. Strong, properly conducted emesis (Vamana) and purgation are the classic deep-clearing therapies for Kapha but, being strong clinical measures, must be done under a qualified Ayurvedic practitioner. This is educational traditional guidance, not a cure.","chapter":13},{"problem":"How to treat two or three doshas aggravated together","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"combination","signs":"Mixed or shifting symptoms when more than one dosha is disturbed at the same time, or all three flaring together.","guidance":"When doshas combine, blend their individual remedies sensibly rather than treating only one. A traditional seasonal guide: manage Vata-with-Pitta as in the summer regimen, Kapha-with-Vata as in the spring regimen (because Vata is a carrier that amplifies whichever dosha it joins), and Kapha-with-Pitta as in the autumn regimen. Act early, while a dosha is only mildly accumulating, if you can. Once a dosha is truly aggravated, reduce it with measures that do not stir up the others. If all three flare at once, control the strongest or most aggravated one first, still choosing measures that do not worsen the rest. A sound treatment relieves the problem without creating a new one; care that fixes one complaint but triggers another is not considered good treatment. Because balancing several doshas at once is complex, work with a qualified practitioner. Educational guidance only.","chapter":13},{"problem":"When doshas are mixed with Ama (Sama) and how to clear them safely","category":"Detox","dosha":"combination","signs":"Blocked channels, heaviness, loss of strength, sluggishness and laziness, weak digestion, frequent spitting, constipation or scanty urine, loss of appetite and exhaustion point to doshas mixed with Ama (undigested residue). Clear, light, opposite signs mean the doshas are free of Ama (Nirama).","guidance":"Ama forms when weak digestion leaves nutrient fluid (Rasa) raw and spoiled in the gut; doshas tainted by it become sticky and spread into the tissues. Do not force these out with strong cleansing, which is compared to squeezing an unripe fruit and only damages the tissues. Follow the order: first use digestive and appetite-kindling herbs to 'ripen' the Ama; then oleation and sudation (oiling and sweating); and only when ready, clear the doshas by purification at the right season and to the person's strength. Medicine by mouth clears the stomach and small intestine, by nose the head and neck region, by rectum the large intestine. If the body is already expelling greatly increased doshas on its own through vomiting or loose motions, do not block it with medicine; in the early stage allow it and take light food, then later either digest the residue with carminative herbs or clear it by Panchakarma. Because the clearing (Shodhana) stages are clinical, do them under a qualified Ayurvedic practitioner. Educational guidance, not a cure.","chapter":13},{"problem":"The right season and time of day to clear doshas and take medicine","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","signs":"Applies when planning seasonal cleansing or deciding when during the day to take a remedy for best effect.","guidance":"Doshas that quietly build up in one season are best cleared in the following mild transition: Vata (built up in summer) by enema around Shravana (about August), Pitta (built up in the rains) by purgation in Kartika, and Kapha (built up in winter) by emesis around Chaitra (about April). First protect the body from the harsh heat, rain or cold of each season, then give the therapy, and do not let the stages of a developing illness advance unchecked. For daily timing, match the medicine to the condition: empty stomach for strong Kapha problems and strong people; just before food for lower-Vata (Apana) issues; mid-meal for Samana Vata; end of meal for Vyana or Udana Vata; between morsels for Prana Vata; with or mixed into tasty food for poor appetite; repeatedly through the day for poisoning, vomiting, hiccup, thirst, breathlessness or cough; before and after food for tremors, convulsions and hiccup; and at night for head and neck disorders. Seasonal cleansing therapies are clinical and should be practitioner-guided. Educational guidance only.","chapter":13},{"problem":"Reducing therapy (Langhana) — when and how","category":"Detox","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"Indicated for heaviness and excess: obesity, diabetes and urinary disorders, Ama conditions, over-oiling from too much oleation, fever, stiff thighs, skin diseases, herpes, abscess, spleen disorders, and head, throat and eye disorders. Also suits most people in winter.","guidance":"Langhana (also called Apatarpana) lightens, dries and clears the body, and is the right direction when there is excess, heaviness or blockage. It has two broad forms. Shamana (palliative) brings an abnormal dosha back to normal without expelling it, through seven measures: digestive/carminative herbs (Pachana), appetite-kindling measures (Deepana), bearing hunger or light fasting (Kshut), reducing fluids and bearing thirth (Trut), exercise (Vyayama), sun exposure (Aatapa) and exposure to breeze (Maruta). Shodhana (purification) forcibly expels aggravated doshas through five clinical procedures: emesis, purgation, decoction enema, nasal cleansing and bloodletting. Match the strength of the method to the person: the very obese and strong need purification; the moderately strong get digestives and appetite-kindlers first and purification later; the medium and weaker do well with simple palliative measures like controlled hunger and thirst, breeze, sun and exercise. Someone who needs reducing should not be given nourishing. Signs it is working well: light body, clear senses, returning hunger and thirst, clean belching, clear chest and more energy. Because the purification procedures are strong and clinical, carry them out under a qualified Ayurvedic practitioner. Educational guidance, not a cure.","chapter":14},{"problem":"Nourishing therapy (Brimhana) — when and how","category":"Energy","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Indicated for depletion and weakness: emaciation from illness, harsh medicines, alcohol, over-exertion, excess sex or grief; chest injury or wounds; excessive dryness; general debility; Vata constitution; pregnant and newly delivered women; children and the elderly. Also suits most people in summer.","guidance":"Brimhana (also called Santarpana) nourishes, enriches and rebuilds the body, and is the right direction when there is depletion, dryness or weakness. It works mainly through the heavy, moist earth and water qualities, and also calms Vata and Vata-Pitta, so it is itself a gentle balancing (Shamana) therapy. The classic measures are rich and grounding: meat, milk, sugar, ghee and healthy fats; nourishing enemas made with sweet substances and oils; good sleep and a comfortable bed; oil massage and bathing; and above all rest, comfort and a calm, happy mind, free of worry. For a lean person, light but nourishing foods such as wheat suit better than heavy, non-nourishing ones. Someone who needs nourishing can be given only mild reducing alongside, or sometimes both together, depending on their constitution, the season and their strength. Done rightly, nourishing restores strength and good body nourishment. Keep it measured, because overdoing it brings its own problems; where strong meats, medicated enemas or formulations are involved, use them under a qualified practitioner. Educational guidance, not a cure.","chapter":14},{"problem":"Signs you have been over-treated or under-treated","category":"General","dosha":"combination","signs":"After nourishing or reducing therapy, watch for the body swinging too far one way: either new heaviness and bulk, or new weakness and dryness.","guidance":"Both therapies harm when pushed past what is needed, so dose and measure are the key. Over-nourishing (Ati-Brimhana) shows as profound obesity and may bring scrofula, diabetes and urinary disease, fever, abdominal swelling, fistula, cough, loss of consciousness, painful urination, Ama disorders and serious skin disease; it is corrected by fat-, Vata- and Kapha-reducing measures such as horse gram, barley, green gram, millets and honey-water, more activity, less sleep, purification, and daily licks of Triphala, Guduchi, chebulic myrobalan or nutgrass with honey, along with classical fat-reducing herbal formulations. Over-reducing (Ati-Langhana) shows as profound emaciation with dizziness, cough, severe thirst, poor appetite, and loss of moisture, digestion, sleep, vision, hearing, semen, vital essence (Ojas) and voice, plus body pains, fever, delirium, vomiting, joint pain and blocked elimination; it is corrected by nourishing: sweet, unctuous, fatty foods, more sleep, comfortable living and a worry-free, happy mind, which build a lean body back steadily. Correct treatment instead leaves the body light and clear (after reducing) or strong and well-nourished (after nourishing) without these extremes. Deep obesity in particular is stubborn and hard to reverse, so correcting an overshoot in either direction is best managed with a qualified Ayurvedic practitioner, especially where strong herbal formulations are used. Educational guidance, not a cure.","chapter":14},{"problem":"Groups of cleansing herbs (Shodhanadi Gana) — what they are","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","signs":"Used by a physician when selecting ingredients for purification therapies or dosha-specific formulas","guidance":"This is a classical catalogue that sorts dozens of plants and minerals into named families, each sharing one action, so a physician can reach for a whole group and substitute within it. It underpins how Ayurvedic medicines are formulated, but it is reference material for a trained practitioner who dispenses medicine, not a home remedy. Many listed plants are potent or toxic and must be correctly identified and processed. Selecting, combining and dosing them is specialist work done only by a qualified Ayurvedic physician after assessing the individual. Never gather, mix or take these substances at home, and never let them replace prescribed medical care.","chapter":15},{"problem":"Emetic group (Chardana Gana) — herbs for therapeutic vomiting","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"Used to prepare medicines for supervised therapeutic emesis in Kapha-dominant conditions","guidance":"This family, led by Madana fruit along with liquorice, neem and Kutaja, provides the drugs classically used to bring on controlled therapeutic vomiting as part of cleansing therapy. Choosing and preparing an emetic drug, judging its strength and matching it to the patient are entirely the work of a trained Ayurvedic physician in a clinical setting. Therapeutic emesis is a medical procedure with real risks and is never something to attempt at home or improvise from these plants. It must never replace treatment your doctor has prescribed.","chapter":15},{"problem":"Purgative group (Virechana Gana) — herbs for downward cleansing","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"Used to prepare medicines for supervised therapeutic purgation, mainly in Pitta-dominant conditions","guidance":"This group gathers the downward-acting cleansing agents such as Trivrit, Triphala and Snuhi latex that classical texts use for therapeutic purgation. The strength of these drugs varies enormously and some are harsh, so the selection, processing and dosing belong strictly to a qualified Ayurvedic physician who has assessed the patient. Purgation therapy is a clinical procedure, not a home detox, and these herbs should never be gathered or taken on one's own. It is not a substitute for prescribed medical care.","chapter":15},{"problem":"Dosha-balancing groups (Vataghna, Pittaghna, Kaphaghna Gana)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata, Pitta or Kapha depending on the group","signs":"Used by a physician to match a formula to a patient's specific dosha imbalance","guidance":"These three families each settle one dosha: one calms Vata, one cools Pitta, and one reduces Kapha. A physician draws from the right family when building a formula for a particular imbalance. Deciding which dosha is actually disturbed, and in what proportion, requires formal clinical assessment, so matching herbs to a person is the physician's task, not a self-diagnosis exercise. These groups are formulation references for trained practitioners and are not meant for home preparation or self-treatment, and they do not replace prescribed medicine.","chapter":15},{"problem":"Restorative and nourishing groups (Jeevaniya, Vidaryadi, Padmakadi Gana)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata and Pitta (nourishing)","signs":"Used in formulas to rebuild strength, tissue and vitality, often after cleansing or in wasting states","guidance":"Alongside the cleansing families, the chapter lists nourishing groups meant to rebuild strength, tissue and vitality, support the heart, and in some cases promote breast milk. These are often used after cleansing therapy or in states of depletion. Even restorative herbs must be chosen and prepared correctly for the individual, which is a physician's judgement. They are formulation references for a trained practitioner, not self-prescribed tonics, and they should not be taken at home in place of proper medical advice or prescribed treatment.","chapter":15},{"problem":"Symptom-targeted and substitution groups (Sarivadi, Patoladi, Varunadi and others)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","signs":"Organised around specific complaints such as fever, skin disease, bleeding, thirst, urinary trouble and wounds","guidance":"Most remaining families are organised around a symptom picture, covering complaints like fever, skin disease, bleeding tendencies, thirst, urinary problems, abdominal masses and chronic wounds. The chapter also allows a physician to substitute an unavailable herb with one of the same properties and to work these drugs into prepared forms such as pastes, decoctions, medicated fats and confections. All of this is formulation knowledge for a trained Ayurvedic physician who evaluates the patient first. It is not a guide to self-treatment, these potent plants should never be gathered or combined at home, and nothing here replaces prescribed medical care.","chapter":15},{"problem":"Oleation therapy (Snehana) — what it is","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata (and prepares the body for cleansing)","signs":"Those about to undergo cleansing or sweating therapy, and people who are dry, depleted, elderly, or have Vata disorders","guidance":"Oleation means giving carefully measured medicinal fats so the body becomes lubricated inside and out, which softens and loosens stuck waste before deeper cleansing is done, and can also calm an aggravated dosha on its own. This is a supervised medical procedure, not the ordinary use of ghee in cooking: the dose is fixed to the person's digestion, often with a small test dose first, and the person is watched daily. Choosing the fat, setting the dose and running the course are all tasks for a qualified Ayurvedic physician after proper assessment. It must never be attempted at home and never replaces prescribed medical care.","chapter":16},{"problem":"Choosing and matching the fats (ghee, oil, muscle fat, marrow)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","signs":"The fat is matched to the condition: ghee for Pitta and for memory, oil for Kapha and fat disorders, marrow and muscle fat for depletion and certain pains","guidance":"Classical texts use four fats, ghee, oil, muscle fat and marrow, and the physician picks and sometimes combines them to fit the patient, since each behaves differently and digests at a different rate. Ghee is generally favoured for calming Pitta and supporting the mind, oil for Kapha and fat disorders, and the heavier fats for depleted tissue and specific pains. Judging which fat suits a given person is a clinical decision requiring assessment and training. This is physician's knowledge for preparing therapy, not a basis for self-dosing fats at home, and it does not replace prescribed treatment.","chapter":16},{"problem":"Who should and should not receive oleation","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","signs":"Suitable for the dry, depleted and those preparing for cleansing; withheld in weak or very strong digestion, obesity, diarrhoea, indigestion, after miscarriage and more","guidance":"The texts carefully separate those who benefit from oleation from those who would be harmed by it, and the list of people to avoid is long, including those with very weak or very strong digestion, the obese, diarrhoea, indigestion, throat disease, chronic alcoholism and women after miscarriage. Working out whether a specific person is a safe candidate requires a physician's examination, because getting it wrong can cause real harm. This screening is a clinical judgement, never a self-assessment, and the therapy itself must not be tried at home or used in place of care your doctor has prescribed.","chapter":16},{"problem":"Reading the signs and managing complications of oleation","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","signs":"Proper oleation shows soft stools, keen digestion and aversion to fat; excess shows pallor and oily secretions; wrong use causes swelling, nausea, fever and more","guidance":"A physician judges the result daily by clear signs: proper oleation brings keen digestion, soft stools, downward movement of Vata and a natural aversion to fat, while pallor and oily secretions mean it has been overdone and other signs mean the body is still too dry. Wrong dose, fat, timing or diet can bring complications such as swelling, piles, stupor, itching, fever, nausea and bloating, which the physician treats with fasting, lighter drying foods and specific remedies. Interpreting these signs and handling problems needs training and supervision. None of this is safe to manage at home, and it is not a replacement for prescribed medical care.","chapter":16},{"problem":"Sweating therapy (Swedana) — what it is","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Kapha and Vata","signs":"Follows oleation; used for stiffness, cold, heaviness and pain, and to mobilise loosened waste before cleansing","guidance":"Sweating therapy uses controlled heat to make the body sweat on purpose, normally after oleation, so that softened waste is melted and moved toward the digestive tract for removal, while also easing cold, stiffness and pain. The heat, method, duration and intensity are all set by a qualified Ayurvedic physician for the individual, and the procedure is done in a prepared, supervised setting. This is a clinical treatment, not a home steam or sauna session, and it must never be attempted alone or used in place of prescribed medical care.","chapter":17},{"problem":"The four methods of sweating (dry heat, poultice, steam, warm liquid)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Kapha and Vata","signs":"The method is chosen to fit the disease, the body part and the dosha involved","guidance":"Classical texts describe four approaches, direct dry heat, warm medicated poultices, directed steam, and pouring warm medicated liquid, and the physician chooses among them to suit the disease and the part of the body being treated, often adjusting whether fats are included based on the dosha. Each method involves real heat and medicated materials that must be prepared and applied correctly. Selecting and carrying out these methods is specialist clinical work. They are not home procedures, and using them should never replace the care your doctor has prescribed.","chapter":17},{"problem":"Who should not be sweated, and protecting delicate areas","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","signs":"Avoided in the very weak, dehydrated, bleeding, pregnant or recently delivered, Pitta disorders and many other states; little or no heat to eyes, heart and groin","guidance":"The texts carefully list the many people who must not be sweated, or only very mildly, including the very weak, dehydrated, bleeding, those with indigestion or Pitta disorders, and women who are pregnant, menstruating or recently delivered, and they insist that delicate areas like the eyes, heart region and groin receive very little or no heat. Judging whether a person is a safe candidate, and protecting vulnerable organs, requires a physician's examination and skill. This screening cannot be done at home, the therapy is not for self-use, and it does not replace prescribed medical care.","chapter":17},{"problem":"Reading proper sweating and managing excess","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","signs":"Proper sweating eases cold, pain and stiffness; excess causes thirst, fainting, dizziness, fever and skin discoloration","guidance":"A physician judges success by the easing of cold, pain and stiffness and the softening of the part, after which gentle massage and a warm bath follow. Over-sweating is harmful and can cause thirst, fainting, weakness, dizziness, joint pain, fever and skin discoloration, which the physician then checks with cooling, holding measures that must themselves be balanced carefully. Recognising when sweating is enough, and treating excess safely, needs training and continuous supervision. None of this is safe to manage at home, and it is not a substitute for prescribed medical treatment.","chapter":17},{"problem":"Therapeutic emesis (Vamana) — what it is","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"Classically indicated in Kapha-dominant conditions and some skin, respiratory and upper-body disorders; withheld in pregnancy, weakness, heart disease and many others","guidance":"Vamana is controlled therapeutic vomiting used to expel excess Kapha after the body has been prepared with oleation and sweating. In classical practice the physician prepares the patient over days, gives a measured emetic medicine, supervises the bouts, and judges by their number and nature whether the cleansing was too little, correct, or excessive, the last of which is dangerous. This is a high-risk clinical procedure with a long list of people it must not be given to, and over-treatment can even be fatal. It is carried out only by a qualified Ayurvedic physician in a supervised setting, must never be attempted at home or self-dosed, and must not replace prescribed medical care.","chapter":18},{"problem":"Therapeutic purgation (Virechana) — what it is","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Pitta","signs":"Classically indicated in Pitta-dominant conditions, abdominal masses, piles, jaundice, chronic fever and more; withheld in recent fever, lower-body bleeding, diarrhoea and others","guidance":"Virechana is controlled therapeutic purgation used mainly to expel excess Pitta, again after the body is prepared with oleation and sweating. The physician matches the drug and dose to the person's bowel nature and dosha, gives it at the proper time, and reads the result by the bouts and material passed, watching closely for over-purgation. The texts warn that a large dose given once can risk death and that weak or unprepared patients need mild, small, repeated doses or other precautions. This is a serious clinical procedure done only by a trained Ayurvedic physician with the patient under supervision. It must never be attempted at home, never self-dosed, and never used in place of prescribed medical care.","chapter":18},{"problem":"Screening and preparation before emesis or purgation","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","signs":"Each procedure has detailed lists of who is suitable and who must be excluded, and requires prior oleation and sweating","guidance":"Before either procedure, the physician decides whether the person is a safe candidate using detailed classical criteria, and prepares the body with oleation and sweating so the waste is loosened and can be expelled without harm. The texts warn that purifying a body that has not first been properly prepared can injure the person like bending a dry stick. This screening and preparation is careful clinical judgement based on examining the individual. It cannot be done at home or self-assessed, and it is never a substitute for the care your doctor has prescribed.","chapter":18},{"problem":"Reading the outcome and the recovery diet (Samsarjana Karma)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","signs":"Outcome judged by number and nature of bouts; digestion is left weak and must be rebuilt through a graded diet","guidance":"The physician judges whether cleansing was inadequate, proper or excessive by the number and nature of the bouts and the material passed, and treats over-treatment promptly, since it can cause severe weakness, dehydration and other dangers. Because both procedures leave the digestive fire weak, recovery runs through a careful graded diet, from thin gruel up to normal food over a set number of meals, which is itself part of the medical plan. Interpreting the outcome, handling complications and guiding recovery all require a trained Ayurvedic physician's supervision. This is not something to manage at home, and it does not replace prescribed medical care.","chapter":18},{"problem":"Decoction enema (Niruha / Asthapana Basti)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Classically indicated in severe Vata disorders, abdominal tumours and bloating, gout, spleen enlargement, simple diarrhoea, abdominal pain, chronic fever, blocked stool, flatus and urine, urinary stones and absent menstruation","guidance":"This is a cleansing enema in which a warm herbal decoction, blended with salt, honey, fat and a little herbal paste, is introduced through the rectum to draw out accumulated wastes and pacify disordered Vata. It is given on an empty stomach, after prior oleation and sweating, with the quantity matched to the person's age and strength. It is classically avoided in the very weak, in chest injury, recent or toxin-type diarrhoea, vomiting, breathlessness, piles, ascites, diabetes and in pregnancy. Because the fluid must return within a short, defined window and the dosing and timing demand close judgement, this is a formal clinical procedure carried out only by or under a qualified Ayurvedic physician, never self-administered, and never a substitute for medical care. No step-by-step home method is given here.","chapter":19},{"problem":"Oil enema (Anuvasana / Sneha Basti)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Classically for Vata disorders in those fit for enema, especially people with strong digestion and a dry constitution; the aged, children and the depleted may receive the low-dose form","guidance":"This is a nourishing, lubricating enema using medicated oil or ghee introduced through the rectum to soften and calm Vata and ease the channels. It is classically given soon after a light meal, in a smaller quantity than the decoction enema, and is usually alternated with decoction enema so the body is both lubricated and cleansed rather than one alone. The very low-dose version, Matra Basti, is described as gentle and well tolerated for children, the elderly, the overworked and the weak. Suitability, dose, timing and aftercare are decided case by case, so this is performed only by or under a trained Ayurvedic physician. It is not to be self-administered and does not replace prescribed medical treatment.","chapter":19},{"problem":"Urethral, vaginal and uterine enema (Uttarabasti)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","signs":"Classically for disorders of the urinary bladder and, in women, the vagina and uterus, such as prolapse, pain, and heavy menstrual bleeding; given only after the body has first been cleared by decoction enema","guidance":"This is a specialised enema delivered through the urethra, or in women through the vaginal and uterine route, using a fine smooth nozzle and a very small, mild quantity of medicated fat. In women it is classically timed to the menstrual period when the passage is open, and given in short courses. Because it involves delicate internal passages and tiny precise doses, it carries real risk if done wrongly and is among the most technical procedures in the text. It is performed strictly by a trained Ayurvedic physician in a clinical setting, is never to be attempted at home, and is not a replacement for medical evaluation and care.","chapter":19},{"problem":"Cleansing nasal therapy (Virechana / Shodhana Nasya)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Kapha","signs":"Classically for disorders above the shoulders with stuck Doshas, such as headache, heaviness and stiffness of the head, some eye and throat diseases, swellings and gland enlargement, growths, skin disease, seizure disorders and chronic nasal catarrh","guidance":"This is a purifying nasal therapy in which medicated fats, pastes, decoctions or strong juices are instilled or blown into the nostrils to draw stuck Doshas out of the head and neck. The classical method involves prior oil massage and mild sweating of the head, lying with the face up, warming the medicine, instilling it into each nostril in turn, then spitting out what drains. It is strongly dose- and timing-dependent and is classically withheld from the recently fed, the recently bathed, after alcohol or blood-letting, in acute rhinitis, cough or breathlessness, in new mothers and outside set age limits. Because the purgative and powder forms are powerful, this is done only by or under a trained Ayurvedic physician, is not for self-use, and does not replace medical care.","chapter":20},{"problem":"Nourishing nasal therapy (Brimhana Nasya)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Classically for Vata-origin headache, a headache that worsens through the day, loss of voice, dryness of the nose and mouth, difficulty speaking, difficulty opening the eyes and stiffness of the shoulder or arm","guidance":"This is a strengthening nasal therapy using rich, nourishing materials such as meat juice or blood of suitable animals combined with resins and plant extracts, instilled into the nostrils to relieve dryness and Vata disorders of the head. It follows the same careful preparation as other nasal therapy, with head massage and mild fomentation, correct posture, warming of the medicine and drop-counted dosing. Suitability, material and dose are judged by the physician against the person and the season. For these reasons it is carried out only by or under a qualified Ayurvedic physician, is not something to self-administer, and is not a substitute for medical treatment.","chapter":20},{"problem":"Low-dose daily nasal oil (Pratimarsha Nasya)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata","signs":"A gentle one or two drop oil instillation described as safe across ages for general upkeep of the head and senses; classically avoided in infective rhinitis, after alcohol, in worm-infested head and when Doshas are strongly disturbed","guidance":"This is the very low-dose form of oily nasal therapy, just a drop or two of medicated oil, which the classical text presents as mild enough for regular use from childhood to old age and at many everyday moments, such as after a head massage, gargle, tooth cleaning or bowel movement. It is said to cleanse and clear the nasal channels, ease fatigue, help eyesight, firm the teeth and settle Vata, and to need far fewer restrictions than the stronger dose. Even so, the choice of oil, such as the classical Anu Taila, and whether it suits a given person are decided by a qualified Ayurvedic physician, and it is best begun under that guidance rather than as unsupervised self-treatment, and never in place of medical care.","chapter":20},{"problem":"Medicated smoke inhalation (Dhumapana)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Kapha and Vata","signs":"Classically for Kapha and Vata disorders above the shoulders, such as cough, breathlessness, chronic rhinitis, voice disorders, bad odour of the nose and mouth, hair trouble and weakness of the ears, mouth and eyes","guidance":"This therapy draws the smoke of specific medicinal herbs through a purpose-made multi-chambered pipe to act on the head, nose, throat and chest, mainly against Kapha and Vata. The smoke comes in graded strengths, soft for Vata, medium for mixed Vata and Kapha, and strong for Kapha, and the classical rules fix exactly when each may be used and how many measured breaths are taken, with the smoke always let out through the mouth and never the nose. The text warns at length against it in bleeding disorders, after purgation or enema, in certain eye disease, head injury, anaemia and many other states, and says misuse or wrong timing can cause bleeding, loss of sight or hearing, fainting and confusion. For these reasons it is a controlled procedure performed only by or under a qualified Ayurvedic physician, is not a recreational or self-administered practice, and is not a substitute for medical care.","chapter":21},{"problem":"Daily oil pulling and gentle mouth rinse (simple Gandusha / Kavala)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","signs":"Everyday mouth freshness and comfort; the text notes sesame oil or meat soup for daily use, warm water to lighten and cleanse the mouth, sesame-paste water for tingling or shaky teeth, honey for sliminess and mild mouth ulcers, and fermented grain wash for bad taste and odour","guidance":"In its gentlest everyday form this means holding or lightly swishing a mild liquid in the mouth. The classical text treats plain comfortably warm water, daily sesame oil holding, and simple rinses as ordinary daily care that cleanses the mouth, freshens the breath, and comforts the teeth and gums. Used this way, gentle warm-water or mild oil-pulling practice can reasonably be a soft self-care habit for most healthy adults. It should stay gentle and non-medicated: anything using strong, hot, pungent or purifying herbal preparations, or done to treat an actual mouth or throat disease, moves out of self-care and should be set up and supervised by a qualified Ayurvedic physician. It is not a treatment for dental or medical conditions and does not replace seeing a dentist or doctor.","chapter":22},{"problem":"Medicated mouth hold and gargle (Gandusha / Kavala, strong types)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata, Pitta or Kapha by type","signs":"Classically for disorders of the neck, head, ears, mouth and eyes, excess salivation, throat disease, dryness of the mouth, nausea, stupor, anorexia and rhinitis","guidance":"In its therapeutic form, medicine is held full in the mouth and kept still, or taken in smaller amount and gargled, using liquids matched to the disorder: oils for Vata, bitter and astringent and sweet herbs for Pitta, hot pungent and salty herbs for Kapha, and astringent bitter herbs to heal. The mouth is prepared with shoulder and neck massage and fomentation, the liquid held until the mouth fills or the eyes and nose run, and the practice is classically avoided in indigestion, locked jaw, loss of sleep and certain other states. Because the choice of strong or medicated liquid and the judgement of timing matter, these medicated forms are set up and supervised by a qualified Ayurvedic physician rather than self-prescribed, and they do not replace medical or dental care.","chapter":22},{"problem":"Oil therapy of the head (Murdha Taila: Shirodhara, Shiro Basti and related)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata","signs":"Classically for scalp dryness, itching, hair fall and cracking, scalp ulceration, headache and burning, and in the stronger forms for facial palsy, loss of scalp sensation, sleeplessness, dryness of nose and mouth and grave head disease","guidance":"This is a group of head-oil therapies of rising strength: oiling with gentle massage, pouring warm medicated oil in a steady stream over the head (Shirodhara), keeping an oil-soaked cloth on the scalp, and holding a standing pool of oil on the head inside a fitted band (Shiro Basti). The classical method involves prior purification, careful fitting of the leather band and paste seal, a specific warm oil, and set holding times judged by the condition, with massage and oil removal afterward and a limited course length. These are clinical procedures needing proper set-up and judgement, so they are performed only by or under a trained Ayurvedic physician, are not to be self-administered, and are not a substitute for medical care. Filling the ears with medicated oil (Karna Purana) for ear pain or disease is handled the same careful way.","chapter":22},{"problem":"Eye drops and collyrium (Anjana)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Depends on the condition (Vata, Pitta, Kapha or vitiated blood)","signs":"Classically considered for a range of eye complaints with symptoms such as pain, itching, watering, burning and redness, and for collyrium specifically when the disorder was judged localized to the eye and in a ripe state after body preparation.","guidance":"This describes two external eye treatments from classical Ayurveda: medicated drops (Aschotana) and a medicated lid paste or powder (Anjana or collyrium). The texts stress careful choice of strength, temperature, dose and timing, and list many situations where the treatment should not be used at all. These are clinical eye-care procedures meant to be carried out only by a trained physician. They must never be tried at home, and they are not a substitute for seeing a qualified eye doctor. Nothing here promises a cure, and no one should stop any prescribed medicine or delay proper medical care for an eye problem.","chapter":23},{"problem":"Eye nourishing (Tarpana)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Mainly Vata and Pitta; type chosen to suit the disorder","signs":"Classically considered for tired, dry, stiff or rough eyes, weak or clouded vision, and in the recovery phase after an eye disorder's acute redness, pain, watering and swelling had eased.","guidance":"Tarpana bathes the closed eye in warm medicated ghee or fat held within a paste wall around the eye socket, and Putapaka instills a concentrated roasted meat-and-herb juice in a similar way to restore eye strength. Both are specialist eye-nourishing procedures of classical Ayurveda that require precise preparation, timing and after-care and were carried out only by a trained physician. They must never be attempted at home and are not a substitute for modern eye care. Nothing here promises a cure, and no one should stop prescribed medicine or delay seeing a qualified eye doctor.","chapter":24},{"problem":"Blunt instruments","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","signs":"Classically used when a foreign body had to be grasped or extracted, when a body cavity or channel needed inspection, drainage or suction, or when healthy tissue had to be held or protected during a surgical procedure.","guidance":"This chapter catalogues the blunt surgical instruments of classical Ayurveda, tools that grasp, hold, probe, lift, extract, suck and support rather than cut. They belong to a trained surgeon working in a proper clinical setting. Using any such instrument is a surgical act and must never be attempted at home. This information is educational only and is not a substitute for modern medical or surgical care. It makes no promise of a cure, and no one should delay proper medical treatment on the basis of it.","chapter":25},{"problem":"Sharp instruments","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","signs":"Classically used when tissue had to be cut, incised, punctured, scraped, drained or sutured, when a vein had to be opened, or when blood was to be drawn by leech, incision or suction.","guidance":"This chapter describes the sharp surgical instruments of classical Ayurveda and their uses, along with leeches and other accessory tools for drawing blood. All of it is surgery and bloodletting carried out by a trained surgeon in a proper clinical setting. None of it should ever be attempted at home. This is educational information only and is not a substitute for modern surgical or medical care. It promises no cure, and no one should delay seeing a qualified doctor or stop prescribed treatment because of it.","chapter":26},{"problem":"Bloodletting (Siravyadha)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Mainly vitiated blood with Pitta and Kapha involvement","signs":"Classically considered when curable disorders attributed to vitiated blood failed to settle with other treatment; strictly avoided in many people, including the very young and elderly, pregnant and recently delivered women, and those who were weak, feverish, anaemic or otherwise unfit.","guidance":"Siravyadha is the controlled opening of a vein to draw off blood thought to be vitiated. It is a clinical surgical procedure of classical Ayurveda that required careful patient selection, correct technique, strict limits on how much blood was drawn, and skilled after-care, all in the hands of a trained physician. It must never be attempted at home and is not a substitute for modern medical care. Nothing here promises a cure, and no one should delay seeing a qualified doctor or stop prescribed medicine because of it.","chapter":27},{"problem":"Removing foreign bodies","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","signs":"Classically indicated when a foreign body was lodged in the body, recognized by features such as bluish discolouration, swelling, pain, repeated bleeding and non-healing soft tissue, with the exact tissue judged from the pattern of symptoms and discharge.","guidance":"This chapter describes how classical Ayurvedic surgeons located and removed foreign bodies such as arrowheads and thorns, including which should and should not be removed and the many extraction techniques and instruments used. All of this is surgery carried out by a trained surgeon in a proper setting. It must never be attempted at home and is not a substitute for modern emergency or surgical care. Nothing here promises a cure; anyone with an embedded foreign body or injury should seek qualified medical help without delay.","chapter":28},{"problem":"Surgical operations","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Varies with the swelling (Vata, Pitta, Kapha or vitiated blood)","signs":"Classically indicated for a ripe (pus-filled) abscess or swelling judged ready to be opened and drained; opening an unripe swelling or neglecting a ripe one was considered harmful.","guidance":"Shastrakarma is the surgical incising and draining of an abscess or swelling, with the careful pre-operative, operative and post-operative care, suturing and bandaging that classical Ayurveda describes. All of it is surgery carried out by a trained surgeon in a proper clinical setting, with correct timing and skilled wound care. It must never be attempted at home and is not a substitute for modern surgical or medical care. Nothing here promises a cure, and no one should delay seeing a qualified doctor or stop prescribed treatment because of it.","chapter":29},{"problem":"Cautery (Agnikarma) and alkali (Kshara)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Alkali avoided in aggravated Pitta and blood; strength chosen by dosha and severity","signs":"Classically used for conditions such as warts, moles, external piles, fistula, foul sinus ulcers, growths and bleeding points, as strong measures and often when gentler treatment had failed; strictly avoided in many people and at delicate body sites.","guidance":"Kshara (caustic alkali) and Agnikarma (thermal cautery or branding) deliberately destroy or seal tissue and are among the most powerful and hazardous procedures in classical Ayurveda. Both demand careful patient selection, precise application, correct strength and skilled after-care, and were used only by a trained physician or surgeon. They can cause serious harm and must never be attempted at home. This is educational information only and is not a substitute for modern medical or surgical care. Nothing here promises a cure, and no one should delay seeing a qualified doctor or stop prescribed medicine because of it.","chapter":30}]}